        <p>At a meeting iTIO—Fee-rhe-ry 4tli, The Finance Com- mittee considered the Clerk's estimate of expenditure for the half )4st, ending 30th September next. The Clerk reported that although he lied included in his estimate • 111161 of £llOO which would be required in payment for the iron ataircases and alte:aui.ea required to be carried not at the Workhouse, the amount required from the overseer. was £6lOl lean than the amount levied in the correaponding half of last year. Further the estimate for ust-relief es hosed on the assumption that the tinardiane woul4he called upon to pray the Treasury a new squat to the amount paid by them to persona who are now in receipt of old age pensions, and who were, prior to January Ist, lent, chargeable to the poor rate, nod therefore the estimate could, with safsty, be reduced a further .CGOO. It erns recommended that the estimate of expenditure he approved and adopted and that calls be made Upn the overseers of the several towushit s within the Union for the awn of 1:13,000. . . .</p>
        <p>It awns decided dint the charge sisters lathe employ of the Board at the prenenf time he remunerated as follows i—E:3O for the first 3ear'ir approved norvice, £32 10s for thesecond yeer..o2 Los. for the third year, rising afterwards to a maxie,un. of EPA per annum. With reference to Mtn... appointments of charge cistern the eatery was fixed on follown i—EllO per •nouto, rising El annually to a maximum of EiEi. Pt,. JULLYA, in moving the adoption of the minutes, said the arrangement with :mined to the salaries of the charge risters would commend itself to the majority of members as an equitable and renaonable one. lie was sure that while there might he some little difiereraxe of opinion respecting that, there would be Illone with regard to the reduction • f the call on the overseers of the trivenbips. That, he iningincd, would boa welcome opring gift. The call wen .E 1,200 less then the corree- ponding call last year. The probable effect of procpec- tier expenditure under cue or two heads chich were not yet definitely nettled had been carefully considered by the committee, and they felt justified iu recommend. lug the reduced call. Mr. Fr lIN., seconded. .</p>
        <p>The Rex. A. M. M trill ELL said that so far an he could gather from the Clerk's summary of the eatimateedhere w,n no decrement in the normed expendi- ture but rather no increase. They were going to expend more on in-maintenance of omensi or the Workhouse end Schools and. no doubt in view of the advances to the Clerk of .C5O and to the blaster and Matron of .ClO each, there was an incrense of £45 in officers' gaieties for the half year. Office.' ration. were also to cost more. He hoped that in drawing up the estimates the Finance Committee tel considered the 411.11tiOn which he brought tip some time ago—the abnormal amount spent on wines and spirits in connection with the hospital. He would like to know, before the estimates were rowed...here the espenditore on wines anti Spirits came to. Wen it included in " in-mniutenance in the Work- flow* "or ttnder the heathen of" paupers inpaospital "? Lie strongly objected to the use of the wired" peeper" in dancing op the lista. He wished the clerk wonld kindly see that the word inmate or patient was sub- stituted when referring to those in the hospital He ' gretabled with all his might againnt thence of the word I P7r.'i„-5„,.r.„ told the iitiestiors of the experidi- tore on wine, sad spirits was entirely la the bands of the doctor.</p>
