      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>SUPERINTENDENT TURNER.</head>
        <p>SUPERINTENDENT TURNER.</p>
        <p>CONGRATULATIONS ON THE HONOUR CONFERRED BY THE KING.</p>
        <p>The honour conferred upon Mr. Frank Turner, the veteran superintendent of the Warrington Fire Brigade. by his Majesty the King, has given great satisfaction to the townspeople who greatly appreciate tne faithful services rendered to the borough by thin. old arid valued i.ervant of the Corporation. On Satur- day evening Mr. Turner returned to Warrington wear-1 ing the Edward medal which was presented to him by the King at St. JIIMP4.B Palace the previous Thursday</p>
        <p>" for :iii vears' service dislioginished La success in th.) organization of lire brigades in the town and by repeated acts of gallantry in saving life front lire." • Be arrived at Bank ,Auly State! about nine tied ck not bad a cordial . reception from friends who bad assembled to meet . him. Accompanied by Mr. Harold Smith, M.P , Aldermen Burton (chairman of the Fire Engine Committee, Smethurst 1.n.1 Fotahaw, tie superin- tendent rode in a brougham along Parker-street and Sankey-street to the Conservative Club. On entering the fir at-chars billiard room the superintendent (who is an old mends,' of the club) was cheered to the echo. Several congratulatory t.peeches were nest delivered.</p>
        <p>Aldermen BotcroN Field he felt aura he wee enly voicing the foelines of everyhndy in Werrinston when he said loos henrtily they welcomed their old veteran of the liras brigade hack to Warrington after receiving the King's Medal. If env nom had ever earned</p>
        <p>such an honour end distinctien' it was Superintendent Turner. (Applause.; Ile had been an excellent eervant to the people of Warrington. herhey knew, he had been cc/monied with the Brigade for more than 511 yoara,and tel discharged the duties which lied devolved upon him es superintendent for word than thirty tears</p>
        <p>in a manner which had reflected the greatest credit upon himself, and which wo I pleasing to ever, citizen. (Applause.) lie had not only berm Is servant to the borough, but oier s') out Iring dibtricts had been served by the Fire Brigade of lVarringtou. The Fire Engine Committee laid received many complisnenurry letters with regard to the Superintendent's conduct when he bad been attending tires. (/!pplattle) '</p>
        <p>Alderman Su firii CHST, • former chairman of the Fire Engine Committee, said Air. Turner was a Rood old veteran and he felt sorry that a grander reception had not been got up in his honour, especially no he had placed his life in jeopardy scores of times, rind had caved the lives of many et his fellow townspeople. (liner, hear, and applause. There wan no doubt that in his ability and sagacity in superintending the Opera- tions of the Fire Brigade, he was second to no-one. As a member of Os Corporation for about 20 yeere, and es a former chairman of the Fire Engine Committee, he (Alderman Sicethutht) could soy that although they bad hed many lug firea—and this wee a matter in bad referred to in commitiee —he hod never known an out. break that bad not been confined to one building. (Applause.) That could not be said of Mee which hed taken place in Manchester and I.iverpoul. Ile had also received letters of corigratulation to the Seperintendent upon the capable manner in which he bad saved property. He eiss glad to see Alderman Burton in that room. Tilsit was not a political meetmg, and he was glad to find that the Alderman wim not one of those ainpid people who would not enter a Cr...restive Clan. (Hear, hear, and applause). Mr. HAROLD Sol tort, M.P. said be agreed with what Alderman Smeteuret hod mud snout Ali:lemma Burton's presenoe, and he offered hint on behalf uf the club a wenn welcome. (Appian.). They also con- gratulated him t n the feet than he had no nroad a mind that he could, when occasion offered, en he hoped they all could, go far away above politics and join uncoil. gratultsting e fellow.townemnn upon an hotiour which ioul he.e conferred upon him. (Applause). Mr. • I Turner was a fo,au of deed% and nut ot Words. lie hod never heard Lim n.•ke a linemen, and he • !chi not suppose bet was io the habit of making then), but Ito bad done his work end he bad done ' it in a menaer which wise creditable to the town. (Ilear, hear.) Ile had lived a life of devotion to duty, and a the of courage cad modesty, which had °Amami him to every 0110 of his fellow citizens, and he mu mire he was voicing the sentiments of the whole of tbe people of Warrington when he expressed the hope that they would be able tr, meet hint for ninny years in the streets end et their social functions mad point the Iluger scrum the street and say '• 'there guts one of ' ' '</p>
        <p>„.. Warrington's heroes.' iCheers.) 'There wan no one in that room, or in %Verrington, who was prouder than Superintendent Turner himself at the honour he renewed. He bed let him (Mr. Smith) into a secret. When their gracious Sovereign heard the Superinten- dent's wonderful record read, he, in a manner which bad already endeared him to the people of England,dis- carding all formality, said two words, and two words only Good nian.” (Cheers.) He (the tnembsr) could thing of no greater praine out of the lips of Sovereign spoken to the subject. These words the whole of Wiarrington would reecho, and alien they thought of Superintendent Tuner they would say of him," Good man, good man." (Loud cheers•. Superintendent I Ult:0:11, who was grew It touched by the warmth of his receptioo,said :Our Member eau spirt that I ant not in the habit of making speeches end the remark is quite correct. My motto is " Business," and all I can say at present is that I thank you lot the trouble you have taken in meeting me at the star ion and giving roe such a hearty welcome borne. (Cheers.) Aldermen ['wolf ow pointed out that an honour had been conferred not anti upon Superintendent Dirtier hut upon Warrington. (Cheers.) there was no doubt that superintendent Turner had dune his duty and had dune it well and bad richly earned the medal.</p>
