        <head>ST. HELENS TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>THE SANATORIUM OPERATION CON TROVE.IIST.</p>
        <p>61.</p>
        <p>Alderman Beecham (Mayor) presided at the monthly meeting of the St. Helens Town Counci I, on Wednesday. Alderman Bates, in reply to a rotation by Dr. Jackson, gave particulars of the operationa performed at the tionatoritior during the past four yearn, and said that under the new arrangement mode by the Health Committee, which came into force iu 1910, a consult- ing SU rgeon res. called in by flue medical atiepdeat and the medical officer of health on te occosimis. In only ono of the cases did the con- s ting surgeon deem it adviarible to operate, All the Mal men who ores in consultation were of opiniou at the cona wee I. very obscure one, but the only .ossible hope war an immediate operation as the man ens dying. It proved to be a CASO of peritonitis and obstruction which arose from an Inoperable cancer, which, of court*, could not be removed, the case, there- fore, proving fatal. The minutes were passel. Mr. Tulsa complained to the Fligewlsy Committee about the state of Itorry's•lane, and the daugeruus brook that ran alongside the road. Mr. Wareing alleged that the chairmen promised two yeere ago to attend to It.—Alderman Bishop said he did net remember having done so. It was a queation of</p>
        <p>ical t at tiv ossible</p>
        <p>trying to arrange with the landowner, and they could not do as they liked.— I he minutes were approved. Aldermen Bishop moved the minutes of the Perlis- , mentary Committee, and drew attention to the pro- posals fore diversion of the routes by which the trims ran in sod ont if the town. . . .</p>
        <p>M. Fos naked if it eras proposed in order that the l'rescot and Rainhill cars should run or Cotbaru-street, to Inks down part of the Sefton Aril. Hotel. Alelemma liiohn p *aid be believed they could manage without. . .</p>
        <p>....... . Mr, Fox said it was n dangerous place at pro-lent, %IA the only war to nuke it mule would be to take down part of the hwel. Mr. Grace fully agreed with the proposale nf the oommittee. At the premot time the crania brought people practically to tor hack door of the town, whereat, they ought to be brought along the main Street. It would also lie a great conmoience to the people from , the Rainbill and Preacot sections to he taken to the railway station. , Mr. W. Bates mid the proposals did nut fall in with the idesa of the Trade .d Commerce Committee at ail.</p>
