      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>POULTRY FARMING.</head>
        <p>POULTRY FARMING.</p>
        <p>(By Redcap.)</p>
        <p>_AIII IF If 111. INt I'iIATION,</p>
        <p>Ignorance and prejudice are tire two chief enemies of al:Oficial incubating methods. Thome, however, are gradually dying out. The increasing demsnd hir early hatched chickens is forcing the need for practical experiments. The poultry farmer who j waits until hens are broody,mont certainly loses linsi- ness. Surely the result if 'rainy Apaher4 experience should remove any element of di add oh rut the soccess of. good incubator. If you are stunning a prejudiced position, then try one. As a result (providing the machine is properly worked) all doubts will be removed. If it were riot that thousands of chickens are nnnually hatched by artificial methods, our national hill would be nearer fl million pounds than eight millions. And it is only by a further extension rh this inethol that we can hope to redisce es-en tide alrently enormous, stun. I wrile in these termn because there fires lot of people who still untrofitably adhere to old-fashioned methods. It is all very well to to so if poultry farming to such people is piirelv a bobby. von to those Who only raise a few birds for ext;i. lion purposes. Theo the broody hen will fulfil all their reqiiirements. Bat there are down who Say that the method is wrong bectuso it is artificial. Providing you never attempt t, supersede nature— NS simply copy her—it i not monk Suture in herself Is ideal, then what wrong c.. 0 these ho in copying an ideal. Let es humbly copy Nature; Stunt lice gJfts so msnifold She Ilan given to every cieature, A chance; to imitate the mould. “I:SEIt U. 0 rmT N tits ins gNI. . .</p>
        <p>Ido not utend logo into the detailed rnanagemer.t offbe incribat a-, because nearly every different make has different methods of working. One cannot lay down bard and fast rule, to work by. 'those purchas- ing machines will at the name time receive a copy of instructions from the makers. Neverthless, let me here give you a few general nianagement hint First of all, place the machine in a room with pleat: of top ventilation, but free from draughts The tl ar of the incubator room inust also be free from any possibility of sudden vibration.. Vlr'hen you go into the roost to pay attention to its working, never slam the door after you. If a hot water awchine tee that it is fixed perfectly level.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>111,1,1 N., rllr E.:S.</head>
        <p>111,1,1 N., rllr E.:S.</p>
        <p>Itoessential that all the eggs should he marked sod [laud before being placed in the dottier, as it is necessary to turn theta every twenty-four bouts. The [narks will show the uprermoot side, while the date when the incubalion starts should also be known.</p>
        <p>c ")I INC; THE</p>
        <p>In this prceess you must be guided largely by weather conditions, and the heat of the room. In cold weather remove the egg drawer for about ten minutes, and from fifteen to tsenty minutes in warm weather. When the rugs commence chipping dis- continue cooling, perhaps about the ISM day. If it happens that in egg be chipped ott the lower side it slsoild hr turned on tnp at 011CP. I need make no mention of testing the eggs seeing that I have already dealt with thie in a previous article.</p>
        <p>II NII 4 OF Vt 44T1..N.</p>
        <p>It ,nay in,ere+t-'in); re—ade;... if I hereby com- pile a hoc going the period of incubation of duraeiti• cute.' and other !owls. Dare Days Days Ostrich 42 (hose 2.4 Duck 2$ Emu 42 Turkey IS Pli.stunt 21 Swan 42 Pea Fowl 2, Plim kip 24 Guinea Fowls 28 Bantams 21 Hen 21</p>
        <p>For acme of the above particulars I am indebted ti Mr. Chitties E. Ifearson's most irreienting and instructive little book " The problem solved " of which I shall lane more to say later on. In ronclimion let me dispel the idea that an incubator is a difficult !machine to work It is not, providing that 3on purchase a good machine from a reliable and .xperi- enced maker. One cannot write On the subject of artificial inculmtion and say ' Amen' at the end oft chapter on the incubator. So our next must be on the management of a</p>
        <p>10,1 -,1,11E1:. Pay at once, that it is unnecessary to sive &amp;I the technical points of a Later mother, because ample instructions. hr., are always sent rut with machines from reliable firms. One if the greatest mist ikes often made in that of blaring the machine indoors. Of course when it is unavoidable, then whatever place in used nl,ou,id hsve an earth door. I pointed this out when speaking of cramp in chickens last week. Always place a foster mother our of doors when posaili.e. Select the most southern and sheltered spot you can find. See also that the ' ground is fairly level and somealtat raised. .o that rhea the rain comes you avoid a wet run. Whatever you do keep the run as dry so possible. If chickens are allowed to nun shout in a puddle of water disaster soon foliows. With these few hints following silkily printed instructinne, you should have no difficulty in !waling successfully a foster-mother.</p>
        <p>NIFAI S 1131</p>
        <p>How ninny timer should f .wls be fed per day? 1 helve often been asked this question. great deal depends upon what accommodation you nave for the stock. Cod, natural circutnstanres a fowl would only get ooe meal per day ; hut of course that w •old he all day. Stock kept on a free range ate very much like what they would he under natural connitions,and eonnequently they would is mire less feeding,for they, having opportunity, would forage for most of their own food. by no mesas then should they he fed more Mau twice a day.</p>
        <p>Now under these conditions feeding ii the all- important item. It is not so much a que-tion of what numbs •of meals per day. as onantily Riven in each meal. You should calculate the amount given to correspond as nearly as may be with getting their food under natural conditions.Al ways leave theta at the end of each meal no that they will straightway lo k and work for more. This nil, should he rigidly carried out e,pecially with second year lien. Pullets seem naturally inclined to forage (to a certain extent) however fed. It is not so with hens. I have just finished a motith'e experitnet.t with a pen ol Leghome. five pullets and one two year old hen. I gave them two full ;coals and ems scanty midday meal per day, and oven then the pullets worked for more, but the Id hen simply huddled herself contentedly in a corner and</p>
        <p>lee rivend.., noon and evening ainiete, aoid</p>
        <p>AGENTS:</p>
        <p>CARTER &amp; SON, Bridge St. Warrington.</p>
        <p>waited for the next meal. She didn't even trouble to lay an egg. Whether in confined runs or on • free range fowls should be induced to be active, so that you see how essential it is not to overfeed at any meal time. The egg organs of the fowl can not be kept in good condition by encouraging idleness. A little at a time, and a bit oftener, with • sub- stantial meal at Light, works well. I have, experimented with three genii meals per day but not with the hest iesnlis. I have hail better returns by taking the same titiantity of food, and giving it at four times instead of three. Some people ray that the light breeds ,such as tlie Leghoins are small eaters. So they are ; but they will almost accept a Lind every tone you happen to go into the yard. I find from experience !hot f ital. are very much like human lieings. They generally like all they can get without working for it. But it is also a fairly true maxim, and a new one I think. The more they get without work, the fatter aud more useless they become.</p>
        <p>THE CRY SUCCESSFUL WAY. SPRATT'S CHICKEN MEAL must altm, form the first 1'1N7..*- tnomtng rnnaL atm be omen WWII, SP L ..cSP ITT'S 0 n '-n. es thn after- • n nods RAI - .</p>
