      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>•LVIM. COMlfllTllfl-</head>
        <p>•LVIM. COMlfllTllfl-</p>
        <p>A great deal of progress in now heing made in the poultry industry, and not • little of the credst should lie given to the various laying tee we have in the shape of laying comi.etitione. Toe following article from the " Daily Diapatch " will I.elp to prove my etatement :</p>
        <p>POFI..TRY. THAT 'nv.</p>
        <p>A laying corer etition ha. Jim been brought to. close seer Burnley, in which the Northern Poultry Soc,ety bee demonstrated very forcible that, in spite of silvers., conditions, egg. ow he produced in I great quantities by a system of feeding aid good ...gement. The scene of the competition is •11 typical et bit of litinceshi re as can he found. Tall mill chimneys predomitotte, vegetetion is very scarce, and the plot is perched at a height, from which one cell look down over the spires and chimney. of Burnley, while at the back there is open country receiving the full force or the "rind as it ClAlle, over Peodle Hill. All sorts of bad weal her have been experienced daring the competition: rain beg fallen &amp;riot:, more then half the deys of it, and attendance upon the birds has bores trying matter for the manager. To ehow the impartiality which prevailed in the com- petition, It may be noted that the ownership of none of the birds was known Si, the manager, ishile one ovrner who went daily to the scene of the con.petition was unable to ideplify his own birds.</p>
        <p>Ninety-stx bird.. were entered in the cornpetitien, the vairiety being white Wymtdotte, then huff Orpington, white ['Pallornn and white Orpingtona. The first flee prize winners were wilite Wyandotte., the sixth position felling to buff Orpinat on, Many farmers roiled Lancashire here not had en egg front their birds all winter. yet these birds hone been persuaded to lay 4,232 eggs in niete n wee.. The birds laying Ivrge egge have more credit than those laying small 4.013. nod certain points are allowed for the !greatest weights, so that it is net always the greatest 1 number of eggs which fixes the birds' position, unless the weights correspond. . . .</p>
        <p>1 If theaverage acorn of the first six pens. added op , and the eggs valued nt the lowtat winter prier of I 'it. leech it amounts to Co 95. I Id., showing that 21 birJa hare produced over 10s. worth of open per week. The fond airon to the ljr,l. hen been of a plain nutritious character. In all 3,1110,. of fowl has boon consumed and 130114 of o..nt grit, while 77111,. of wan meat have been given. , The birds were fed twins a day—a warm soft food mash in the morning containing tlie ...would a food it nolid groin at night. That thry hays not been ptimperyd in any WAYS shown by the fact that the nest •anixer are , outride, although protected front the weather, es that the birds hod to go oot to lay.</p>
