        <p>A vote of thank. was passed to the auditor (Mr. E Bradshaw) for hie service..</p>
        <p>The Cll'A I KMAN, in .117n;ing a ainillar compliment to the hon. mecretary and hon. treasurer, referred to the great work which Mr. Jenkins bad done. Ile was the father of the camp soil had been its most active supporter during the whole of its existence. War ring- ton owed deep debt of gratitude to him for his work in connection with that movement. Mr. Ransoms took over the accounts when they were not in the most flourishing condition end had worked very hard indeed. Mr. FOll,ll W seconded and the note wns cordially approved. Councillor INS in returning thanks, said he sincerely hoped that Miring the coming newton renewed efforts would be pot forth for the success of the camp, and especially in the direction indicated by Mr. Murray. The camp offered every comfort for weakly or crippled children, and all that was needed am more funds to keep the place in constant and efficient work. Mr. RANsIIME said the camp had endless possibilities for giving crippled children or adults healthy accom- modation and a good holiday, but they must have the money to do it. His work atirin the past sermon had been mock easier owing to the committee's fortunate selection of a caretaker (Mr. Quinn). Votes of thenks were alter peamad to Mr. C. White, for medically examining the children before they were sent to the camp, to the voluntary helpers, to Mr. Craddock (who was described as Councillor Jenkins " right hand mats" in the work of Om camp committee) nod to the Choir sine</p>
