      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>rite,, in a remarkably good programme at the Palace thin week, and there are several items of ont- o: nding merit. Heins and Co, as acrobsta and dancers in a sketch entitled " Hunasti Itamsti," are really gumming, and there., is no doubt that their comedy in of a high-class order. Similar fare is pro- vided by Ferguson and Mack, termed " eccentric ceinedians." They are certainly eccentric, and it is this very eccentricity which make. tbeir " turn " a ode-splitting one. They open their performance with a smart etre.; of repartee, and before it becomes weary they switch on to a laughable &amp;bawdily," in which they imitate a couple of ballet girls go'mg through a graceful donee. The imitation may not be entirely true to life, but it gives Ferg seen and Mack an °ppm- t unity of going through a few antics which greatly awn., the audience. As a ventriloquiet, Norman Osborne has struck out an original line for himself. He nianipulaten his doll in a manner which almost at timen maken its imagine it is: alive arid speaking for itself. Nosa-days the appearance of • ventrilemist and his inevitable doll is sufficient to make the •ndiento lean in boredom back in their seats, bet Norman Osborne's contrite:mat poweraand:the origin- ality e ith which he expnottees them, come* as • welcome Normane to his audiences. Mies Helen Martin has a sweet and cultured veer and she sings several old favinirite song.; such on " Annie 'aerie in a tasteful manner l'he remainder of the pm. gratorne in TIM, good. Sheila McNeil, a Scotch coinmerlienne, sings neveral Scotch songs in an •ttrartive manner, whilst Stanley and Vandyke participate in an amusing skeich. Other terns are Sam Redfern, "The black beim of the benighted Bohemians," a comedian, and the Walwoll iurroutio.. " Perfection " pictures are shown, and Mr. Widdep directs some high Claus manic ins capable manner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>OVERRUN BY COCKROACHES.</head>
        <p>OVERRUN BY COCKROACHES.</p>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>ELECTIVE AUDITORS t 'l'l BONO? It can hardly be pretended that the diction of Messrs. T. Pemberton and B. Si!cock to this ellice greatly exited the ratepayers at large. Such an event generally attracts little public attention anywhere, and even when a content occur., comparatively few consider it of any importance. 11 0110 who happened to lie fairly well-inforined were milted the reason of this cornier,o indifference, his retort would be probably something like thin The accuracy of the Corporation , counts is certified by profensienal accountants. What need ix there for smateur per Inner.? On the present occasion a Warringtemisn wonld not i.e quite justified in assuming that precise altitude, but it into he borne in mind that I now argue die point on broader linen. I have a friend who has occupied a bimilar office for several yearn and I consider his response to nip appeal for further light upon the sub- ject sufficientlyoapt and timely and convincing to warrant the publication at this „juncture. Here is the gist of his communication It is the enthority which the holder posFeasee as a (hetet representative of the people which giver the office its value. Ile gees to the discharge of its duties as an equal and , independent authority. lie haft abeolutely no control ' over the Council, nor hostile Council any control over him. He iv empowered by the ratepayers and by the law to make any investigation or inquiiies he may deem to be necessary. It in quite poe4ble for money to I. legally spent and accurate acciont to be kept When this is done, the profeseional auditor certifies to that effect, nail his duty ends there. An elective aeditor's dutv may begin there. Ile tinny think that, even if legally spent, it has net been economically spent, sad may have ocoasion to say re. A critical faculty and courage to tole it are needed. The acecitnts reveal very much of the buitinese of idly concern, but t everything A knowledge of the business in quite aa necessary in order to understand the accounts An auditor needs to posted up in all the trannactions of the business. An investigator is not always wolcome when he goes to niche inquiries. He in apt to Inc regarded a. a " pry." Even when no objection is raised and all inquiriee duly answered, it reqnires some courage at first to make them. Ile can exercise no check on expendi- ture. The only thing he can do in to report the result of kin inquiries to his constituent.. He can watch the expereliture in the interests of the ratepayers and by the information he is able to obtain and to communi- cate he can put them into a position to form a judg- ment of their own. They can then exercise a cheek themselven if need be. When the proper time comes round they are better qualified to roper. approval or otherwise of the conduct of their repreFentatkes. Posting up the ratepayers in much noire useful and important work than the adding up of figures miff the licking of totals, and requires men of another quality. Hitherto the are and possibilities of the position do not appear to have been appreciated either in War rington or elsewhere. '' SEAM/ AT TIIE POLICE COURT."</p>
        <p>Let me add to the note appearing in last week's " Examiner " under this heading an equally amusing experience of toy own. A Vot knhire solicitor, named Leming, now dead, wan before the magistrates examining the complainant, his client, in support of a en:unions fir defamation, and this pruned : " Well, and what more did the defenlant say to you ? " Alter that, he told me to go the devil." "After that what did you do ?" " I came to you, sir, at once !" I am aware that something very like this has pre- viously appeared in print, and that in my reason for giving the name of the lawyer ; of course, there may have been similar cases, but to this particular instance I was actually present and can vouch for the Let. lIIS 1101.711 lIAD COME.</p>
        <p>Alternatively. it is conceivable that some enterpris- ing Press' hoer" has continued to urnst more or leas honest penny at my earense. I have hail such experiences A cousin of mine named Emmott, now living in retirement in the neighbourhood of Chicago, formerly travelled for a New York house, and on one occasion found himself stranded at a small place in Virginia. As only two trains sopped there in the course of the flay, he said to the Irid darkey who acted an 1/00t8 at the tumble-down woodvn " hotel " at which lie had pei force to put tip for die night, now kook here. uncle, I moat catch that 7.30 in the • morning, sad as Flu a strand sleeper, you must knack</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>" The Arcadians "</p>
        <p>'Hie return visit of "The Attailia. to Warring• ton has proved a very popular engagement. Among modern musical comedies it in deservedly one of the most popular and entertaining, for side splitting humour, tuneful mode, and pretty scenes are welled together into a homogeneous whole. Simplicita. and Peter Doody are unquestionably two of the moot c-mical parte, seen in musical comedy of any period, and the widely diversified character of their (un- making prevents the appetite being in the leant cloyed. It was a pleasure to nee, once more. Mr. i'harles Shepliennon an the hil dim., mountebank who ham penetrated Arcadia and come back to London to tell the tale. Ilia athletic orations in the twenty c.tanne of Arcady, and his persistent panning and jocularity, are almost incredible. One would panne for breath if Simplicitax would provide the oppir- tunity, but his fond of humour Inns inexhaudible as his agility. The famous jockey is still played by Mr. Alfred Berra, who may have imitators lint 'annut r tone a superior as the droll Peter Doody. That air of profound melancholy, combined with the ecream- ingly funny song " Always merry and bright," is i reeistibly comic. There has been nothing smarts, seen or heard for a long time than Doody'a dialogues with Eileen and Maria. They sparkle with genus of drollery. Mrs Smith (Maria) is another, vinitor we ' are glad to see again. Miss i ertruile Aylward's study of the part is no natural that a goodly share of oldie appreciation fall, fairly to her lot. The parts id Sombrit and Chiron (Arcadians) have changed hands since the last visit. but the standard of artistic excellence has }peen well maintained. Mine Vivien Carter is admirable an Sombrs, and her singing of '' The pipe, of Pan and other number. which belong t ; the tile in. delight to the ear. Mies Tina Linroln is another new corner as Ede*. Cavanagh, and whether in song or dance she in equally charming. Ifer Irish naivete, coupled with the tuneful rendering of " Stop your philandering,- and " l'ni no tired of violet. " is altogether delightful. Mr Austin Castle makes a capital rover of Eileen in the part of Jack Meadows, and the irrepreesible Bobby in again *meetly played by Mr. Cyril Lane. We need hardly all that hart and NI iretou's prockaelln of the comedy in on an elaborate and tasteful twale. The orchestral work under the direction of Mr. Wells Knight, is thoroughly efficient. and down fall justice to the piety aim with which I hie score abtamds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>CHILDREN S SUMMER CAMP</head>
