        <p>Hundred. of poor ehildrt n in %Torrington bare (WIMP to bless the Cluildrede Summer Camp with gives them their only o bent e of a healthful holiday ot the /reside. Every iun mer large (alt hen of boot mid gßltt ere token to the pr to ily oitueted romp st Hreetatyn end after a winker, puy tent of tnumbing line, ann snd vigorous recrestionso hey return home the pictures or health and heppinme and with anew outlook on life. It in solely by voluntory contributioos thnt the camp ill maintained but unfortunately the amount forth- coming doting the pent 3euer hes rot icon ourlicient to make ende meet. It Is hoped that in ownmentonitlon of the rt ronetion (fort will be made ibis year to relieve the moron ent of tho o mut/lb/an. of debt. The annual meeting of robscribers mod Mean of the camp wee held at the Old Ace/lolly on Monday evening, Mn. J. Longlend prmiding over a mist tocottraging ettendame. Amongst others present Wire Mr. J. Nfoore Murray, Mr. J. J. Firth, Mr. J. A. li•osonte (hon. treasurer', Councillor W. PP. J. Jen% it,. hon. secretary), sad tomer°. lad en. Mr. F. W. Richmond u-rote tend/ ring hie renignation from Ihr committee, en he would shortly be lensing Warrington, end encluoiug donation to Ow funds. THE YEAR'S WORE.</p>
        <p>Councillor .lesgts, presented the ennunl report which stated that during the past ...lon it had Anal. been ;muftis to inc.nne the no ober of children sent to comp. There were 1.1 I girls end 213 I oys, or A total of 467, no against 42'7 t. ken last (ear. The total nun her enjoying tiny fecilitien afforded by the camp woo in the aggregate 3,190. With the extended sleeping accommo- dation that somber might have her n still further nuguyented had the committee felt that their funds warranted it. In consequence of the heavy calls merle on the public during the not season, there hid henna Flight falling off in the regular contributions which the committee hoped might be adjusted dor ing the coming season. The nom' en of "Atilt visitors to the camp (at Prestatyn) wes somewhat reduced, hut that was largely accounted for by the bad weather. It no. hoped • more extended .e of the camp might he merle by the people of Warrington Av A health and holiday resort during the time before and after the children were there. Th, Accommodation had</p>
