        <head>Dr. Williams' – Pink Pills</head>
        <p>Dr. Williams' - Pink Pills</p>
        <p>CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB</p>
        <p>THE SIZE OF THE SEA.</p>
        <p>An officer of a steamer once made the remark to me that most people are as ignorant about the size of the sea as they are of the distances between the stars and the planets. The trouble in both cases is that the figures are too great to comprehend. Sometimes astronomers help us to get an idea of the distance of the sun and planets by telling us how long it would take a railway train to travel there ; and perhaps I can in a similar way give just a little help to those who would like to understand the vastness of the oceans. Here arc a few facts: The Pacific covers 68,000,000 miles, the Atlantic 30,000,000 and the Indian Ocean, Arctic and Antarctic 42,000,000, To stow away the contents of the Pacific it would be necessary to fill a tank one mile long, one mile wide and one mile deep every day for 440 years. Put in figures the • Pacific holds in weight 948,000,000,000,000,000,000 tons. The Atlantic averages a depth of not quite three miles. Its waters weigh 325,000,000,900,000,000 tons, and a tank to contain it would have each of Its sides 430 miles long. We could make similar startling calculations about the other oceans, the Arctic, the Antarctic and the Indian Ocean, but I think these figures are quite enough for us for the present. They are so great as to be almost incompre- hensible even to the cleverest mind. But just one other fact. It would take all the sea water in the world two million years to flow over Niagara. ABOUT PANCAKE TUESDAY. _</p>
        <p>Now-a-days Shrove Tuesday means nothing hut pancakes to most of us, but in time past it meant much more. As everybody knows, Shrove -Tuesday is the day before Lent, and used to be observed as a day of special preparation therefor. It was a day of confession of sin and the people used to flock to church to obtain absolution. This is the meaning of Shrove Tuesday; it was the day when they were "shriven or cleansed from their sins. It was made also a day of feasting and holi- day. In villages they had feasts, and there was a general fasting before Lent brought fasting and quietness. In some villages the old customs are still kept up. Boys go round to the houses begging for money or cakes and singing the old song A-shrovin', a-ahrovin', I be rornel-sbrouin' ; A piece orbreod, a piece of cheese, A bit of yont fat bacon, (Jr a dish of dough nut; All of your own ! In Nuneaton and Chester-le-Street they allow the boys to play football in the public streets, and in Manchester up to quite recently all the apprentice boys hada holiday. Another Shrove Tuesday custom is the "ringing of the pancake bell." Formerly a bell used to be rung a little before twelve o'clock to warn the women that it was time to begin to fry the cakes. This old usage is still continued at Audlern in Cheshire, and one other place that we know of is Buckingham.</p>
        <p>THIS WEEK'S STORY. ANOTHER FOR PAT.</p>
        <p>One jay, a; at, the water--carter, under the influence of liquor, belted at the top of a liver bank, a iron f trawls for his inquisitive mind stopped and</p>
        <p>"!low long have you hi uled water for the village, my good man ?" 'Tin year, Bor.'. " Ah ! how many hada ho ycu take in a cloy ?" "From tin to.fifteen,..or."</p>
        <p>•h, yes. Now, I have a problem for you How much water at this rate hare you hauled in all? The driver of the watering cart jerked his thumb backward towards the river, mud replied ; All the water ye don't see there sow, ear.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>L.I. IIIiZTS,I</head>
        <p>L.I. IIIiZTS,I</p>
        <p>"HON ORIA."</p>
        <p>BY LILA FIELD Author of "One of • Legion," "Reaping the Harvest." "Countess Veria Flight," kc.</p>
        <p>" I don't work on a day like this!" cried Ralph Gunning, berating into the large. corn. fortable, untidy studio, where two young own of about hie own age stood together, leaning against a table and contemplsting a good msed canvas on an easel before them. " The weather has been precisely the same for the pant six weeks," said Chris Stenton, in a manner that bespoke a strong faculty for arguing. " That may be, but the glories of June have gripped me, and rendered me absolutely unfit for work. I rimed candor idly under the tree., down the lanes—and, finally, batik by the babbling brook budding • Castles ir Spain'!" Gunning laughed merrily at the anxious faces his friends had turned on him as he proceeded to unfold hie programme for the day. "Sunstroke!" ejaculated Guy lielekin, Ask. in him head sadly. 'Well, the eooner you take yourself off It the brook and your 'Castles in 'Spain,' the brder for us—we mean work. 'The better the day ' you know the rest my boy." Menton said, wcverely. But his wise words; were wasted, a. Ralph Gunning, with cheerful whistle, left the room with as much speed as he had entered it; banging the door behind him.</p>
        <p>"I know what shape hi, 'Castles in Spain' will take. The roseate vision will he his can- vas as the picture of the year, and himself being lionised by the great of the land!" guy Melekin laughed and strolled across the room to a well-cushioned window-neat, and, kneeling on it, leant out of the open casement. For • moment lw, too, drank in the splen- dour of this the first day of June. The scented breezes fanned his face, and the shafts of golden sunlight streaming through the trees foil on his hair, turning it to bur nished gold. • " ' "</p>
        <p>These three young men had been fellow students from the beginning of their careers, and during the summer months shared a cottage in (rile of the loveliest parts of Devon shire, transmitting their glorioue surround- ings to canvases, which they hoped would one day grave the walls of Burlington House. "It is a magnificent morning!" said Mete- kin, turning almo7t reluctantly from the open window, and joining his companion once more before the canyon.</p>
        <p>"Look here, Melekin, are yon contemplat- ing following Gunning's example? You are chewing strong symptoms of hie ailment," Menton remarked, with some irritation. "Don't worry—l'll he good," said Mele- kin, giving the other a friendly slap on the back. "Well, what do you think of it?" ho added, nodding towards the picture on the easel, then sitting up with a jerk on the table. "Quite the beat thing you've done so far— o( course, it isn't finished."</p>
        <p>"Almmit—very little logo on." "Yes; and just that little will make •Il the difference—it will give the necetwary depth—supply what I feel in wanting, and yet can't express definitely or point out se- eurately," Stenton lapsed into silence, but continued to gaze with the keen eye of the artist at every point of the really lovely pic- ture, which represented a girl in the full beauty of early womanhood. The perfect face illumined by a smile, which one felt instinc- tively must be horn of pure happiness. Iter white, gauzy dress looked ethereal against the wilderness of trees, creepers, and shrub- bery amongst which she stood. "Splendid piec, of work!" said Stenton. warmly..</p>
        <p>" thanks, old fellow; I've lived in the pic- ture. I shall be eorry when it is finished." Helekin sighed, then broke into a eong. He moved shout, collecting his palette, paints, and brushes.</p>
        <p>"What time i• ifonori• doe?" asked Chris Stenton, glancing up from .01110 subtle colours he was blending. "Ten, I think; she is never pisrictital— bless her dear, happy-p-lucky nature." Mile- kin smiled fondly at his picture. " What a splendid model the girl is— quite the prettiest and sweetest one I've ever</p>
        <p>"Don't call Ilonnria a model." broke in Guy Melekin—a frown of displeasure mar- ring for the moment his sunny good looks. Well, my dear fellow, what in she, then? I'll tell you a secret you are hugging to your- self, Melekin—you are in love with Honoria," Stenton laughed teasingly. "Bash! Anti if I ant!" cried Melekin, a note of challenge in his voice. He looked at his friend questioningly. "Yost don't mean anything serious, Guy —you can't. Think of your people—reflect on their disgust at being asked to welcome a model as the future Mrs. Guy Melekin—ite ludicrous! The first of June in working havoc with more than one brain, it seems," said Stenton, a trifle sarcastically. "Oh! that eternal caste! I euppose my handiwork is all that I am over likely to pox- revs of llonoria!" Guy idelekin muttered bitterly, and his eyes Seabed angrily at. Sits- ton, who was working steadily, all uncon• scions of the raging storm beside him. "What a glorious day indeed!" sighed Chris Stenton, with a mocking laugh. "I think that subject has been exploited long ago—equally, you are catching the tection."</p>
        <p>" I was joking—your face has utter despair writ upon its comely surface. My dear boy, • midsummer dream is a mad, merry fan- tasy • somewhat resembling the trannforma. lion scene of a pantomime. Both linger plea. mntly in the memory for many dayo—then they fade to give place to other dreams; no sane man ev,.- clings to an impossible—in- tangible—" "Honori• is neither a dream nor is she Intangible. She will never fade from ray memory," Melekin peened his hand across his brow. He looked out to the rose hedges and to the garden-gate, for he knew that Honoria always came that way. "So the pretty little markt is your mid- summer dream—it is a very mad and merry one!" said Stenton, laughing. Hifi whole attitude was bard and imam. pathetic; he had no patience with the emo. time; that carried a man out of bin depth, blind to danger and In consequences, " You discovered my secret. Menton, and shattered my beautiful itlyll—l had not seen my love MI you (hewed it to me. You are right, no doubt, and I was selfish—l am not the only man who ban loved the wrong woman." "My dear fellow, you stand in excellent company, also on • crowded platform," /Men- ton said, adding a few touche, to his canvas, then standing back to see the effect. "Oh! I can't bear any more of your cod- and-dried speeches! Life in • cold, straight. laced existence a. viewed through your eyes —l'm going out. When Honori• comes, tell her I shall not work to-day--t couldn't nee her just now." Guy Melekin turned away from his picture, and threw his palette and brushes into a tin box, then, pulling him panama well over his eyes, plumed out to the Bower-laden garden. Chris Stenton continued bin painting unconcernedly; he even gave vent to a of relief as his companion disappeared. "Two lunatice! What • pity! One ing his superabundanee of joy to the Wing brook—the other mingling unavl tears with the laughing waters," he inured, shrogging his shoulders. "Am I latet—so awfully norry. Teti one of his paws, and I've been doctori said a girl'. sweet voice; then the scrambled oneeremoniously throughi the open easement. It was Honoria, the lovely houri who had captured Guy Melekin's heart and _amen wonder! Even the cold Stenton heldr his breath, for her beauty Mruek him with pr. greater force on this particular morning.! She stood before him in her cool white dre•s, dower-trimmed Leghorn dangling by a rit,. bon from her hand, her red-gold hair all ripples and waves, and the nearkling deep blue eyes set in a perfectly bewitching face: "We are used to the erratic clock lc beats time for you." mid Stenton, quiet o When+ is Our?" Honoria swiftly round the studio, then went e- . .</p>
        <p>[tom</p>
        <p>qt!itti</p>
        <p>, :sour. - -.4-hort doctori it," PNaker h</p>
        <p>her portrait, and examined it fondly. " must Out csU L. iteiekia</p>
        <p>Christian name, tionoria—it is not polite." said Stenton, in his coldest voice. "Way? Ile does not mind!" the girl cried In surprise. "Mr. Melekin dose not like to hurt your fezlings. But it is not usual for a model to he familiar with those who engage her rer. vices—perhaps you did not know that; of mune, we have been at fault, no doubt, in Wing alroomt friendly with you." Menton panned, hut did not look away from his work. Honoria was silent for a moment, a deep flush dyed her cheeks, and her eyes Bashed haughtily. "1 do not know why you are speaking to Ise like this, Mr. Stenton; you are hiding something from me," she cried, in an alarmed voice. "Where arc Mr. Gunning and Mr. Melekinl" . .</p>
        <p>"They are both out for the day. Mr. Mele- kin asked me to tell you that he would not require you again he will probably write you." Stenton painted for dear life as he finished this brutal speech. "I did not think • man could be sorb • coward," said Honoria, scarcely above a whisper l "he will never see me again! , " Don't be hard on him. lionoris—he nas been foolish; however, he has seen his folly in time."</p>
        <p>"Thin us your doing, Mr. Staitnn—oh! know, don't explain further. It is a pity, though, that Mr. Mekkin has all,ved him- self to be putty in your bands." The girl Id- geted nervously with the ribbons of her hat —two bright tears trembled on her eyelanhes. Without another word clue panned out with heavy heart, on had Guy Melekin, to the glories, of the garden. The man who had lied to save hie friend, an he imaginal, from a terrible blunder, worked on ninny through that June day. When the nun had net, and the fragrant dusk wan creeping over the country. Gunning and Melekin returned to the cottage. Chris Stenton was smoking and enjoying a well-earned rent on the verandah.</p>
        <p>Did Honoria come to-day?" asked Guy Mackin, in a atrained 01e. P.</p>
        <p>"Yee—she paid a lightning vinit to the studio, and intimated she wan leasing this part of the country almost immediately," (Atria Stenton• voice in an irritating drawl, puffing at hie pipe now and again', and glanc- ing up through half-closed eyes at his two companions. "Minoru% Wi • beautiful butterfly—well. I ant morry she hoe betaken hermit to fresh fieldu. A good thing your picture it nearly finished, Melekin you don't really need llonoria again," mid Gunning, turning to his friend.</p>
        <p>I can never finish my picture without llonoria, no I am afraid it will have to re• main as it ie," Melekin laughed mirthlesely, "I am returning to London to morrow, early," he added, shortly. Before the others could offer any opinion on this extraordinary announcement, he went into the !mune.</p>
        <p>"You have dreamt your dreams. Gunning —mod, merry dreams, my dear boy! The awakening, I am sure, is a.nnoying—Melekin has been indulging in just the same pastime to-day unfortunately. Change of scene is the only cure for him. Hard work in all you need to effsee the disagreeable effeetn," Chris Stenton nmiled at Ralph Gunning's puzzled ,face, then, cloning his eyes, continued to smoke.</p>
        <p>Three yearn had sped by with their kaleiv &amp;weenie changes. Guy Melekin had not returned to Devon- shire since the slay in June when he derided to put beautiful 'lmmix oat of his life. Had he auceeeded in ii g this? No, hie thonglita were ever running on her, and he hod found it impnavilde to take up his painting again. The picture of the girl he loved }minted him; he had left it behind in the cottage, and, with it, all love for him career.</p>
        <p>klelekin had wearied himself travelling from place to plate in quest of the forgetful. fleas that would bring peace in its train. In the end he chose Paris as the meat likely place to make a slay before returning home. He had been in the gay capital but a short week when he ran up against Chris Menton —Hnnoria anti the days in Devonshire rose up in full force before itfelekin once more. . . . . .</p>
        <p>r" Still dreaming?" salted Menton, laugh- ing. and with his usual rough taellessuces bumped straight against the other's warmly healed wound.</p>
        <p>"You remember?" said Ifeiekin, shortly, "I wish I had never left the eottage so sinl. denly. Did you or Gunning ever see llonoria again?" No. I daresay she hue been flitting about forming a brilliant study in colours for other artiste—my dear chap, it's time you woke up, had a good stretch, end went back to the cot. tine and finished that very excellent canvas you were foolish enough to desert!" [Renton nodded his head vigorously. Guy Melekin said nothing for a moment; he glanced away. "Are you over here on business?" he said, striving to divert Chris Stenten's attention from himself. "Yes, I've a fair-sized canvas in the Salon —very decently hung, too. You know, you've been warding some of the best years of your life tramping foreign lands—the result is, you have not forgotten! Because in your idle existence you've done nothing but remember. I'm going eleadily up the ladder—you ought to be nearly at the top with your talent—</p>
        <p>Renton nitwit his shoulders, his eyebrows continuing the movement upward. The ges- ture Was borrowed, and did notsrome natu- rally, hut still went well with his French sur- roundings. "I've lost interest in the whole thing, !Renton, and if I had stayed my work would have born poor stuff —.no one would have looked at it.</p>
        <p>"Are you doing anything to-night?" said /Benton, fumbling in one of his pockets. Ile produced a leather case bulging grotesquely with letters and odd papers. "No; I thought —" "Don't think! You'll wear out your brain. Relieve me of this invitation and enjoy your- self. I can't manage it myself—bueinces!" Btenton thrust a card into Guy Melekin's hand. "I'm staying at the Bedford, drop in and see me to-morrow." Hailing a "there." Christ Menton jumped in and drove away, leaving his friend handling the mid invita- tion with great disfavour. lie glanced at it, and discovered it was for • reception at the house of one of the leading ladies of l'arisian society. "I'm not going to turn out to-night for this," he muttered, and in this frame of mind strolled down the boulevard to his hotel. After dinner Melekin found himself specu- lating curiously about the reception, and at last decided to satisfy this sudden curiosity and look in for an hour at the great mansion. fie was not sorry either, for the magnifi- cent house, full of art treasures, repaid him amply for the trouble. The crowds of brilliant men and women also proved a source of in- terest. Metekin moved from one room to another, and realised that he was enjoying himself. A email white and gold octagonal room, leading out of the grand salon, drew his attention. He made his way to it by the palm huittervatory. "AM this is delightful!" murmured Metes kin/sinking into a deep chair under a spread- ing palm. He closed his eyes and listened to the hum voiced and music, blending not intim- inonionsly. Melekin glanced now and again at the ani- mated picture which the roomn before him preeented, and the artist in him gloried at the, wonderful combination of colour.</p>
        <p>..... . "You have found yourself a ,quiet corner— just what I am looking for," said • tall, slight young man who had entered Guy Mete• kin's retreat, and stood looking down on his recumbent form.</p>
        <p>"Thin ie the way I enjoy a reception—be- sides, I don't know anyone here," Melekin smiled up at the intrnder. 4'l know everybody—you see, 1 beton to the Englieh Embassy. Mow did you get Eere to-night?" "A friend of mine passed me on hie in. vitation; I am glad I came," eairl Melekin. "Would you like me to introduce you to mom people, then we could all go cad have supper together." "That's very kind of you." "Come, then; but first shall we exchange the tieceessry confidences—my name in lemr• ence Wentworth„" he said, with a bright smile that WI. verr attractive. "Mine in Guy Melekin." The two men moved toward,, the door, but before they reached it an ezquiiitely beauti- -1 Sul girl lair* tivir yrsz, -</p>
        <p>Goy Melehin stepped Wick invottintanly, an exclamation of surprise nearly bursting from him. Ws heart WM crying over and aver,"lionoria! Horioria!" but him lipn re• maimed closed. The strong emotion, which swept over him at night of the girl he still loved no dearly rendered him speechless. Honoria looked Melekin steadily in the /yen, a hatighty contempt in her own; then, with a amile and bow to Laurence Went. worth, passed on her way and disappeared. " Who is that lady?" asked Idelekin, in a low voice. . . .</p>
        <p>"The Lady Honoria Riven; she in the centre of attraction wherever she goes—and so delightfully clever and charming—no side, quite the contrary, in fact. Lady flonoria is always first and foremost where there in fun on hand," young Wentworth laughed. "She looked rather angry just now. I wonder who has been upsetting. her—one of the numerous suitors, I suppose. ' Guy Molekin listened to his companion's gay chatter an if in a dream. Hia meeting with Honoria wan 60 extraordinary, the pant so inexplicable, that he wee utterly confounded. "</p>
        <p>The rent of the evening dragged, and he left et the lint opportunity, only too glad to weleeme the solitude of his room. _</p>
        <p>. . .. " I deserve her hatred—what • blind, weak fool I VIIIII—Ond now it is too late! What non- elem.—too late ! Why, even if we hey' met in the ordinary way, the Lady Honoree Riven would n.,ver have looked at me. She ex- pressed that pretty eloquently to-night. Caste ! Ah ! the irony of Fate. 'The first where there is fun on hand!' " Melekin re- peated the words Laurence Wentworth had uttered to express Honoree's high spirits-- they threw light on the incidents of that Hum- mer three years ago, when he lest his heart to the lovely model, and with it his peace of mind. Cloy Melekin sank dejectedly on to • roach drawee clone to the open window, and dreamt and dreams of regret. "1 moat go back to my work I have awakened from my mad, merry dream, an Renton cells it. Nothing now remain. of the pant but my picture no one ran take that Honoree from me," he murmured.</p>
        <p>With set detei:minntion in his fare, Mete- kin closed the window, shut the gay world from sight, and alerted hie preparations for leaving Paris early the next day. Chen Stenton decided to May in the hotel until he beard from Melekin. Towards noon a letter clime from his friend telling of his hasty departure. Menton shrugged hie mhoulder. "The erratic moods and movements of a feverish dreamer," he ejaculated to Mimed with • marcasite laugh; "it took me three minutes to do Melekin the good turn of send- ing Honor's to the right about—he has epent three years knocking round trying to forget her—and be is not the least bit cured of hie foolish fancy yet incredible!" Chris Sten- ton glanced with genuine pity at the letter, then tore it up, and threw the pieces into the waste-paper basket. The fleet of June and lovely Devon once more! Guy Melekin stood on the verandah of the little cottage, and looked out on the glorious, sion•batbed world. Something rain to peace and happiness bed come to him since he bad arrived from Paris. He was sole occupant of the studio now, and wovkcil the day long. llonoria's picture stood on an el in the centre of the 1,0111 - Melekin, palette and brushes in hand, gazed on it with the love• light burning steadily in his eyes. The por- trait was a relic of the happiest days of his life. He recalled with vivid dintinctness the girl's dainty gestures and smiles—that faith- ful memory which so exasperated Stenton. " lionorie -my love!" Guy Melekin whis- pered, passionately. He approached the pic- ture, and, bringing it down to his level, premed his lips to the painted onem, which seemed to be smiling end glowing with life. "What a fool I am, with my hopeless love!" he muttered, sinking wearily into a chair be- fore the easel.</p>
        <p>He I,ant on the table beside bim, and buried his face in him bands.</p>
        <p>The impudent ditty of one of God's own songster* broke shrilly on the ear. The fluffy bird perched on the window-sill, twisting his tiny head from nide to side, as if asking for • little attention and crumbe from the lonely inmate of the studio. After vain efforts, he fluttered his wings angrily, and made off. skimming the velvety lawn, and joining his companions in the cool wood beyond. "Am I late? So very sorry!" mad • sweet voice, breaking like the chime of silver bells across Cloy Melekin's and day dreams. He started op, thinking that his Fu.now, played him false; • cry of joy dew from his heart to his lips .:. . . .</p>
        <p>Honoria bto:id before him, in her while dress and flower-trimmed hat. "I am not a dreamland apirit—only Honoria," she said, holding out ..slim white hand to Melekin. . . .</p>
        <p>" tanoria!" he murmured, taking her hand and touching it with his lips. 'For- give me." " You were not to blame. T know the troth—Guy. Oh, I forgot! May I call you by your Christian name?" Two dark grey eyes and two. violet.blue looked deep into reel, other's dei.tahs, seeking for secrets which they found. Few were th 3 whi3pered words needed to explain away all doubts.</p>
        <p>A golden sunbeam shot into the stodio and lighted mischievously on two beads quite close together. Surely Cupid. too, must have made a triumphant entry on that golden shaft of light!</p>
        <p>The opening day of the Academy, one year later, end great flutter of excitement within the stately walls of Burlington House. "So Guy Melekin has arrived!" said Ralph Gunning to Chris Stenton, nodding significantly towerils the splendid portrait of the Lady Honoria. "My 'dear boy, it is pronounced by general acclamation to be the picture of the yeer. I knew he had it in him—you are, I was right," Stenton glanced round the crowded rooms. And the little model turned out to be • princess in disguise—she made the loveliest bride I've ever seen. Sort of woman • knight of old would have risked his head to win. Guy is justly proud of his sife. What do you think of the denouement, Menton?" Once a time Guy Melnkin revelled in ▪ mad, merry midsummer dream. The fairies were kind to him, Gunning, they let him dream on in the fantastic palm* of delight; • nightmare broke the happy spell for • while—hut the fairies conquered in the end. They gave him back the threads of Wm dream Just where he had dropped them."</p>
