      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>WHY BRITAIN IS SAFE FROM INVASION.</head>
        <p>WHY BRITAIN IS SAFE FROM INVASION.</p>
        <p>An Article that may help you to Sleep o' Nights.</p>
        <p>II Tommy Atkins</p>
        <p>An erre sllo hen had unurTus—l opportunities of com- paring the relative efficiency or otherwise of the Br dish and foreign armies. lv th in men and maler;rl, the writer of this article feels confident that his remarks will amint, in grime email manner, in allay- ing that panicky feeling which eceme an prevalent. Ass matter of sober fact, there is no real cause for alarm. Our coast defer ern were never in a higher slate of efficiency than they are et present ; one guns are of the most modern type; our •rtilleoprien are intelligent, seasoned, and quite as tench mechanics arr soldier.; and the watchful, never-elosing eyes of the coastguard ard Navy acrutinim every vessel which approaches or leaves these thorn. if our men have not the Ravine recklessness sad hooligan ferocity that characterieril the Tommy of the Waterloo p rim!, .they at least have, to more than balance it, trn firms more intelhgenn, and, when an is said and dove, not one whit lens the courage. , Firstly, as to the policing of our roasts. Every I mile of our cant coast-line where a possible landing cothl be made in daily patrolled by • c mpetent icily of keen eyed, intelligt nt anilore. men with years of experience, and who can tell the character of • vessel at a glance as easily as a gardener con die tinguish a particular flower. EVERY VESSEL CAREFULLY WATCHED. Every vessel of any considerable tonnage, and certs Only every war vessel, which comes within the ken of these tone is reported at once by leleglaph to the Admiralty in Inedon, no that at any hour of the day or night the " heads" (es the Service man 'Ara,n it) at Whitehall know to • ship not only the number of vends —whether merchantmen, pleaeure craft, or men, f-war - but the exact locality of each rune!, whether entering or leaving port, its speed, displicement, ete., wel the only exceptum to this tale is perhaps made in the cane of purely local !Insure or ferry steamer., fishing smacks and lighters, and other obvioenly inconsiderable etaft. On an inarneene nip of the Watson around the Urinal Isles them vese•li are represented in their rtepective positions by VIII ioonly coloured pins,: denoting tonnage, nationality and if ; and, in the case of warahipe, the type of fighting strength. MANtEUVRINO THE FLEETS FROM LONDON. Not forone solitary hoer in the Admiralty net of direct touch with the Atlantic, Channel, and Home Fleets ; in-feed, niece the istroduction of wireless telegraphy, the fleet nu almost be manometert from the meeting-room of the Lords of the Admiralty. Ann bon, fortifications arid coastgli fence guns, the Gib wart of all roadsteads and harbourn are &amp;eh- rrately strewn with nolimarine and gloating inters, the latter (with booms) being placed only when actual hostilities have broken oat, or are expected to break ,nt.</p>
        <p>The types of snlinutrile mines are no multifitriona that it is imposnible to describe •ny of them in tlet tit here, bat.. they fall naturally into a few recognisable groups flair principle can at leant be indicated. Fir..., there are mines fired "by ohsemation." These con•ist of large charges of guncotton (about :M(JM. on the average), plae ii inside watertight iron casing, which casing% are moored to the bottom of the harbour or eduary at any mitivm,ient distance from ilie surface of the water, and are tired electrically from the land hy means either of • small magneto machine or • primary flattery. Distance over water is extrenv ly deceptino, and ; the observation devices (which ore many and highly ingenious) call for very fine atljtmtment. One device ins camera obacura arrangement, In rerern . being marked off into a number of wpiares, rind each each entrees is numbered Or named. Es cry tonere correeponds with a croup of electric pings, and the . plugs are connected by cablea to their reapective IrOMI Of mitte:ntit Lice out at sea.</p>
        <p>To make thin clear euppo—nn a hostile wari.hip to he peening over a certain pelt of the outer harbour of any anvil pout mined in the manner deneribed ; say, that part of the sea which is repreeettled by square No. l on the *mean. The piitnre of thin reseal in seen in mimeo No. I, corresponding with its actual position out at sea. The page connecting up the cables to the mines in No. I mot are pudied iota their respective Intel on a holed similar to We- plmne exchange switchhiaril, the circuit is com- pleted, the electric spark ignitee die de inching coin- 1100Dli in the iron casing of the mine, the gnn cotton charge explodes, and, if the ship in not more than a few cable lengths away, • rent is torn in her hull, and ehe promptly founz.'ers.</p>
