        <head>TRAPS FOR TRESPASSING MIPS.</head>
        <p>TRAPS FOR TRESPASSING MIPS.</p>
        <p>In addition to being mined, t 1; —a.pp—roric—. hes to harbours are defended in war time by a tieries of " bonne "---1. e. huge arrangement. of logs or iron pules chained together by .tonit chain-cable., intended to obsinict the fairway or channel. These laa-ima may stretch entirely across a roadetead, or, as i. more neual, be laid only acmm the fairway prorer, the shallow marts of the harbour being a protection in themselves, for a vessel of any considerable draught would only be grounded in attempting to navi.ate such shallow water.</p>
        <p>Th." at the best, in but • device fer delay- ing a hostile Tenet within the zone of effective shell- fire, or fir tearing eruch liAhtly.lmilt craft as torpedo- b sts. In practice, it can often be forced and broken by remmtning —an recurred in lie...port not i.ing ago in ma...erre experiments. Oar main reliance in dm cafe r.f attack by foreign warshipn ia in the gully which bristle from our fortifications. As to the fortifications, no alarm nee.l be felt on this wore. Our cliffs are elaborately burrowed out into long gallerien, communicating nub each other. From every eraeny, upon declaration nf war, would portrude the grim amid nonc of a 6,9, or 12 inch breech-loading gun. Dover and Shakespearen Cliffs !are little thhraliarn thongh the guns cannot be !teen from the decks of vessels pa.ieg iii the Channel. It must be reniembered, too, that the advantages of a fixed land battery meting 'against an oncoming enemy are 34 five to one; and all of our most important for- it ficatinea me quite impregnable. WARRANTED TO KILL AT 11 WILES. , The dander,' coast-defence gun is • rifled, breech- loading piece, throning a projectile some -1901 b. in weight, and it is sighted up to 20,0n0 panic—i.e., • little over eleven milee, though the maximum I.ffeelive range for armour-piercing would be about half that distance.</p>
        <p>These mighty weapons are monnte I in fotindationa of solid concrete, and the actual foondai ion pedestal and base machinery are under corer. Beneath the gun. •Iso, are the magazines for abells, cartridge, the. Throughout the Service the parts of every min, no mailer of what calibre, are strictly iaterchangeablewo that if damage is done to any part of a gun, the corresponding part of another gun of the name calibre can be need</p>
        <p>The gone are fired electrirnlly by the mere press- ing of • trigger, and, in tho care of • breakdown of the electric current, arrangran.rnts are provided for firing mechanically by percussion.</p>
