        <p>what cerrerpooda to the " barrel" in a rill.) has only a certain " life" -that is, the ritlieg bccomen worn out after in me seven or eight hundred ronnds have been fired from the " piece." A flaw" piece " has then to lie fixed into the tux! " cradle," if the gun in to maintain its stlinlard of range and effretvettesti. The yearly outlay in this respect alone in enormons. S perfect are our arrangemet ts at the pre.ent tone, that within fifteen minutes of an alarm being given of a hostile fleet apprueching (and anything Iran than a few howls' notice is unthinkable), the men would be aseembled on the gitupladormn, the sight. fixed, ammuni tin ready fox of and disti Owned round the gun, and the piece itself trained on ths enemy. Each gon,it must he retnemberedis of Vie heavy quick firing type rti inch and 9 :!,for out ince),and can get off at least three rounds in live minute., any one of which—if the hits are in the right place--ie sufficient to disable a vessel, if not scivally to .ink her ; no the nervouely-inclined in our midst need not take the recurring invasion scaren too much to heart.</p>
