      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>READY FOR _WAR IN 30 !</head>
        <p>READY FOR _WAR IN 30 !</p>
        <p>As an instance of the conimen-dahle celerity which the British gunner can display, on • sadden alarm being given, it wilt COMO as a eurprisci to mans to learn that at the time of Admiral Hojdeetvenaiy's outrage upon the Noith elea fishing fleet, trotla:n !holy ntinriten of the alarm being given to " Get ready " at Giloaltar, the whole of that gigantic fortreas wan upon • war (doting ; every gun was manned in reach- new, every "number ' was at hi. appropriate place on the gun-platforin, the alin!li were in 1,0411.1013 round the guns, sod the gunners were (mite ready with eel fuses in thrir hands.</p>
        <p>If there had been ten hostile navies within range at the moment —attempting to furce the Straits—not MO of them but would hive limn sent to the bottom of the ilea within twenty minutes Apart boa th,s, it i 1 interesting to know that Gibraltar is piovi-ioned against a i.iege of mven years',leration. The fortress snow, SS it Si s'ayS int. been eine&amp; first eec Wore] it from the Sp tniarda, ab•ohdely ;ftpretinable. To som the matter up, we are at the present moment quite prepared for any attempted invasion which our enemies may be plotting, even &amp;seeming (a rather far-fet,liel assumptton, hy the by) that our Navy breaks down. Our ships and guns are the heat that money can buy, and our roc" are the beet in the world, being of the sante her dr stuff that wrought a Waterloo aid a Trafalgar.—Frorn the " Penny Pictorial."</p>
