      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>TriLtTICSr '</head>
        <p>TriLtTICSr '</p>
        <p>Notes by ' Ovalist."</p>
        <p>To-day will nettle the question whether Warrington ore to sunken bold show in the N.U. Cup Competition for this season. The draw for the second round has involved them in severe trial at Wigan, which hatt usually been the butial ground of Wartington's hopes in League matches. But cup-tie football has to be weighed up fecin a different standpoint. Warrington have a superior tecord an cup.fighters to Wigan, and were the choice of ground on Warrington's side they would be favourites for the match. It i• the ground advantage which mates the essential and only differ- ence. Can Warrington use superior to sitcom- sconces nod either beat the Wiganers nr make a draw which sill ensure a replay at Wildetspord ? There is room and ?elision for hope. Widnes beat the Wigan let. in a Laneashirn Cup-tie at Wigan thin season, and lest year Win rinpton only lost in the last live minutes lathe tame competition on the same j ground. They will need to glee of their very best undoulttedly.but it join cup-ties that one has learnt to I rely on the Wirepullers. however erratic they tnav he in 'League football. In nty view they have a better .cbance of surviving the count than many reople think.</p>
        <p>Mitch, of course, depends on the conetitution of the team, and writing before the final selection last night. I express the lwpe that Brooks will he played at half- back, and not at three-quarter. Lloyd ani Creevey have done fairly well together, but they hare one weakness—defence. Neither is good It tackling and it is abeolutely ,sential that Warrington shall haven half-hack capable of checking. Johnny Thomas. Brooks has shown lvmself to be the only man curable of the job in the Warrington ranks, and with anyone else as serum-worker Warrirgton will lose. This creates difTiculties on the left wing, with Fish out of favour, hot the half-back ptwiti,n is the crucial one and either rnsworth or Mclntyre can fill the vacancy nfl the wing with a fair amount of succeis,</p>
        <p>Very much depends on the o- rk of fhr Warrington forwards. They have shown dready this season that they can master the Wigan six when honest work is put forth—and there must he no shirking to-div. Nothing but the domination of the scrums I.y the Warritigti omen will lead to the team'. success. I believe the forwards realise this, and if so we may look to them regaining a reputation which haa been somewhat tarnished of late.</p>
        <p>Wigan are fortunate in having their best team available for the encounter and the following side has been selected : Sharrock ; Leytham, Jenkins, Todd, Miller ; Gleave. Thomas ; flernadale, Whittake., Shellcrese, Francis, heeling, William..</p>
        <p>Given a fine day and there glould lie a crowd equalled iilv by the one at Watersheddings. Win or lose Wallington should share a sum of something like 1400.</p>
        <p>The Cop-tie matches to-day are :-- igan a. Wairington : Referee, W. McCutcheon. Oldtiarn v. Wakefield Trinity. Barrow v. Salford. St. Helena v. Leeds. Halifax v. Bradford. Rochdale Hornets v. Widnes. Bra.alahton ft. v Densbury. Runcorn •. Batley.</p>
        <p>It inn fortunate thing that Warrington's parlor- tnances in the League may he largely disregarded in reckoning up cup-tie presibilities, for they have been a disspprintirg aide this season. When well in the running for the first four they succumbed at home to St. Helena! On Saiurday they got in tllt raining again by virtue of A reeliteliolle win at Swinton. Here ell the learn planed well under very had conditions and were decidedly superior to the Lione, though the score was only a try to nil. The forwards were vigorous. the halves clever and the threeptarters and full back reliable if nor speedy. Warrington shaped like a clip team on Saturday.</p>
        <p>Then came Tuesday, and the visit to Halifax. A win there would have made the struggle for the fourth place exhi1.11,11314 and everyone watt hopeful. 'Result : HALIFAX 7 GOALS V/ THIES, If POINTS; WARRINGTON, nil. Bravo, Wirer niters ! They can create a sensation when they try —and when they don't ! But, speaking serionaly, the match on tihrove Tuesday was n disgrace to the Warrington Club and one does art find it easy to forgive the men who let Wartingtrn's reputation down so badly. They never tried a bit after Halifax had got the lead in the first few minutest, and time only prevented the scorn reaching three figures. If the men were unwillirg to exert themselves they should have paid so instead of enter- ing upon the contest under false pretences All they seem to care about is the money—of which they get far too much far too racily. Such a thing as loyalty to the Club and it, colours is, I ain afraid, unknown among the men who at present wear the primrose and blue jersey. They ewe only rehabilitate themselves in one way, and that is by winning to-day.</p>
        <p>Halifax's score on Tueeday wan absolutely the record to the hiatory of the Wallington Club. !sfever before have the Wirepullers had their line crossed ten times in a match. Shame on them for such an exhibition !They did just the mine thing once before when the Cup-ties were on, and in the corresponding fixture too. Halifax put ups record on that occasion, March 11th. 1907, when they scored 7 goals 7 tries, points— the highest eco, against the Warrington club up to this week. It sicken me that Warrington had better drop the Shrove Tuesday fixture at Hanson-lane.</p>
        <p>There is one curious coincidence shout these two reel:mils, and pei baps it is a happy augury. In 1907 when Halifax made such a show of the Wirerullers, the latter went right through the Cup competition and annexed the trophy. Perhaps they will do the same , again. Perhaps ?</p>
        <p>Tuesday's match C,tt was played on the Cricket Ground at Halifax, the football pitch being in such a shocking state. That may be one reason why there mass cricket reore. Anyhow, Halifax played brilliant football against the sorry opposition, as well they might. Some of Wartington's tackling would not hare scared is kitten.</p>
        <p>The League table up to dale is as follows : For. Agit. Per. P. W. L. D. G. T. P. O. T. P. ease. Oldham . 22 3 1...59 84 370_34 36 176... 56.53 Wake6eldr2o 20 4 1...50 68 364.22 30 154.- 02.00 Wigan 26 21 5 0-66 115 477...22 35 151... 80.76 Houslet. 27 17 10 0...54 71 321...44 70 296... 6216 Widnes "5 11 8 3.31 56 =l_lB 29 123... 6200. 11011 10.18 22 13 9 3...81 67 423...42 59 203,.. 56.00 Halifax 27 15 11 1...54 66 292.33 41 194... 57.22 23 13 10 2...10 64 292...34 51 221... 511.00 Dewaburs.2s 14 11 0-29 61 241...34 44 200... 56.00 Warringen2l7 12 9 2..38 49 223...35 60 250... 5540 'Batley 2O 13 11 1...34 61 251...29 30 166_ 54 00 Hull 27 13 11 3.30 67 271...35 54 244... 53.70 Keighley _24 12 11 1.-33 46 204...53 77 337_ 2000. IoochdaleH26 13 12 1...43 57 257...29 51 220... 51.92 St.llelena 26 13 12 1...48 77 323...39 68 242.- 51192 Hudderao424 18 14 0 .116 100 432...37 59 251... 501/0 Swinton . '25 12 13 0...30 44 194-25 37 161... 41000 Salford -.25 II 13 1.. 35 77 271.. 41 112 260... 4000 Leigh '4 11 IS 0...31 36 178-44 55 353... 45 63 Beeton 1825 14 14 1...29 36 168_41 56 230... 41.00 PradfordN2B 9 /4 1...26 34 154...51 65 297... 26.03 Ituncorn . 2.0 7 15 3.-22 44 170-43 70 298... 34.00 Ebsso Val. 25 8 17 0...17 42 160.-35 61 253.- 33.00 &amp;UMW.- 25 7 17 1.25 51 203-48 66 354... 30.00 Mer‘hyrT.l4 5 13 0...14 IS 90...47 81337... 27.77 York . 23 617 0...32 37 175...49 CR 202... 26118 Coventry -23 517 1...3' 44' 206 ..56 91 31)5 2391 Braruley 25 2 39 1_25 26 128...57 92 344... 22 00</p>
        <p>Warringt-n come down to tenth p'sce, with a intim* of points on the wrong side. I think it will in found, when the Pelson c•oses, that Warrington's points record for and against is the worst kr a good many years.</p>
        <p>I leatn that Fred Paley, the tralner of the Club, has resigned sod that Johnston, the Colonial forward, has been app doted to tne •acancy temporarily. Johnston bad a pLysical culture achool ;n Wigan and knows enough ti keep the men in condition if he can per.nade them to train. On Thursday the team went to Northwich to indulge in brine baths.</p>
