      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>CHARLES DICKENS.</head>
        <p>CHARLES DICKENS.</p>
        <p>HIS SON LECTURES ON THE NOVELIST'S LIFE AND WORKS.</p>
        <p>I nteresting Reminiscences.</p>
        <p>My father's life and works" was the snhiect of a lecture delivered in the Parr Hall on Tuesday evening by Mr. Alfred Tennyson Dickens, eldest surviving mono( Charles Dickens. The visit bad been arranged by the Warrington Pbiloinathic Society, who bare than once more placed the teopleof Warrington under a debt of gratitude by giving them the omswinnity of learning more about the master novelist, his home life, and his svr,rks than is ti lie gleaned from , biographies. Mr Dickens trent IT the beaten track: ,1 and entertained his hearers with anecootes—smnsing' 1 and pathetic—which 'creed to allow the novelist's kindness of heart and keen Renee of tumour. Ile also gave a racy account of life at Tavistoca House and (lads Hill end the lecture was rendered addi- tionally interesting by recitals from Charing Name' works.</p>
        <p>The Rev. CANON STEVEN,ON, who presided over large attendance, said they were very much indebted to that young and extremely energetic society in having arranged the lecture. This was the centenary year of the late Charles Dickens, end therefore they felt that no fitter permoo could stand upon the platf,rou that night than hie eon. (Applause.) About 111 years ago the late Charles Dickeae was due rugby a reading in Warrington. But while at Errs on ha was stricken down with that illness which was to be the forerunner of his death. and to the dieappointatent of many in Warrington that engagement was never fufillled. The Canon next reminded the audience of what Lord Rosehery called attention to in hie charming epeech the other thy, when he said that Charles Dicke. was , the first person to teach the English people to laugh. He lived at a time which was very sombre ; when literature was very sombre, and when there was much 1 distress and suffering and touch to make life grey and !dark in our country. With that marvellous gift of humour in which he had never been excelled ; in that : wonderful character-drawing which wan our wonder and marvel as we read his books at the present day, he taught the people to laugh and brought bum. into English homes. We in the present day were beginning to learn what a good thing optimism was ; how much better it was to look on the bright site of things, and how much healthier and happier we were when we lived our lives with the intention of making them as bright for our- eel,e and other people as we could. Applattee). Charles Dickens also did much go it by his vend satire of abuses. He took the Fleet Priem', he caricatnred it, and Ord prism with all its horrors panned out of existence. Ile dice the sketch of Mr. Bumble, and Mr. Bumble was no long, in oar midst. Ile gave on the portrait of Sarah (lamp and she had been succeeded by those mat and charming nurses who were the ministering angels of our tick beds, What he did in those two or three instances lie did in , many more, and ae we enjoyed hit ruial satire we began to see how many armors and blots upon our English life owed their abolition to the way in which Chadea Dickens satirited them, kindly but firmly, in ! his pages. Mr. Dt, KENS, who hail a very hearty reception, said !he regarded their •pplause ass tribute of respect to the memory of the man whose name in time to route would in his (the lecturer's) opinion stand second only , to Shakespeare, and whose works, like Shakespeare's. : would be read as long as the English language was tpoken throughout the civilized world. (Applause.) Other lecturers.apart from the readings and recitations which they selected from his works, &amp;silt with Dickens' private life almost entirely from the stand- point of Fortter'e Life of Dickens " and touched rather too much upon those unhappy days of his boy- hood when he untie, went an many vicissitudes and trotibles. "I want you to come with me" said the lecturer " aml to see him from a different standpoint altogether. I want you to see him in the very zenith of his prosperity, when with ate oI friends eround him, unspoiled hr adulation, untouched by flattery. he ktoo(i the sane bright, genial, sympathetic, large-hearted matt, as he was when as s'ming man he took his seat as a shorthand reporter in the House of Commons, confident in him- self that he was destined to make a name in the w rid." (Applanee,. Mr. Dickens then rreallel an early reminiscence, illustrsting Dickens' kindly nature. In ISil the novelist purchased Taviet ck House, in 'lavistork Square, London. That house, with two others, one occupied by Frank Stone, R.A., aryl the other by a London merchaot, stood within iron gates of its own. In front there was a plot of land, eith large carriage chive, aunts flower bed in the centre, shortly after be took up his residenee, there appeared on the scene</p>
        <p>cItoSSIN,I an emaciated little boy ,about fourteen years of nee, who was to all intents and purposes the origin of " I' or Joe" in " Bleak House," which was vrrittea iu 185:!. This boy made the bonuses his headqu(trters, ! keeping the pavements end drives scrupulousty clean. During the winter monthe, when snow was on the ground, he managed to collect little pieces of holly and mistletoe with which he decorated the beds. After • time an intimacy sprang up between the novelist and the friendless, neglected boy and, finding hint to be honest, industrious and inielligent. Diekens said that he could take his meals ii. the kitchen of Tavintock House and sent him to school at night. The boy got Orl very well with his education and when he reached the age of sixteen, Dickens procured for him a very substantial ontlit and sent hint to the colony of New South Wales. The boy prospered and after he had been there two or three years he wrote to hie benefactor, thanking him for all his I,ntiness and telling him he hoped in tie course eta few years to be able to takes top back to the old country and to thank him personally. That was only one of many hundreds of similar actions which this warm-heaited man performed during his life-time and of which the world knewnothing.lt slight ! ti thy be said of lam that he never let his right hand know what his left head was doing. (Applause). As • rung man Dickens used to work strenuously night and day. On one occasion he wan not only writing two novels simultaneously but also editing the " Daily News " which commenced under his auspices, and attending to hir voluminous perttonal correspon- dence. From his youth up to tne time of his death Dlckens almost invariably answered every letter he received in his own hand-writing. As a matter of course, such strenuous work when young told upon boo as lie got Inure Matllre in age, and sometimes after a very hard morning's work he would for a Into tnoments be</p>
        <p>“I,ENT-MINDr.D, not knowing anyone about him, but he very soon recovered himself. His eldest daughter nimie, in a charming book which she wrote about her father for children, gave a very accurate discription of this. She had as a young girl been recovering front a severe and long illness and at her father'. request she was taken to lie down on the sofa in his study while he was at work. For a long time nothing was heard in the room but the rapid moving of his pen along the paper ; when suddenly he sprang up, rushed over to the looking-glass, looked at himself in it, then paced up and down the room muttering to himself all the thne, then over to the sofa where she was lying, looking at her but evidently not knowing her, then back to his teak, where he kept on bosily writing until lunch time. It was a unique expel inure and served to show how he threw himself coaplete'y into the creations of his own genius. w 1, myself, recollect as well as if it wore only yesterday "continued the lecturer ” when I and my brothers Frank, Harty, Sydney and Edward, who might be termed the younger generation a the family, were home for our holidays at Gads Hill, from Boulogne in France. We were in the habit of having nor dinner in the middle of the day, while the grown-up people an visitors to the house would he having their Intich.n round about the bide board. I hese very often seen him I come in, in the middle of our meakuite absentominded I and not knowing anyone stall in the room. Theo 00e of I my sisters or one of the lady visitors would get up and I mba him a glass of port wine with egg, or something ! light of that description—for he was naturally one of the most atistensibus menace, born—and alters few momenta he would be quite himself again, arranging who was going to play croquet, who isas goine tf. drive into Rochester or walk through the Earl of Darnley'. park. On ace occasion Frank, Sydney end myself were walking with him between liochaster Castle and toe Cathedra