        <p>reouth, which wax hi• nett ive town. At that time my ,brother Sydney wee. midshipman on bard the ' Britannia," of which Mr. Harris on. thy captein. Dlcken• can anxious to ehow the ceptaii, .nrt,e little attention, so he wrote Raking lahn and his wife to gown and hear hini rend it the theatre, and ill so suggested that they should call in hit retiiing room on their why in snit hey. a few words Of conversation with hint. My father was very often in the habit of changing hi.. everyday clothes in hit I retiring room and potting on his dress suit, wind] he did Ile this occasii.. Although he had faced no many audiences throughout the United Kingdom and the United States. be was no highly strung and of</p>
        <p>t,i'efl • Na that he was alwo)• in a terrible tutu, of agitation before he went no to the platform. In conselpience he was in the habit of throwing his every-day clothes 'Captain and Mrs. I birth. came in tognther and had ayes, or'uTienworhyatlifee'rehle""talY about 'r aa t.l)eperks'onifl7on. althoughtionofnet in worda of c ...rotation with hint. As tary were going into the they re,sl ra. [lards left her opera cloak Cation? be choirs. Vhen the reading wee over they cattle hack into the roorn,accompthied bin number of Dickens' friends, to partake of thaw I ight.refrethment. When the explain end lila wife gut up to leave, Dicke-no., being naturally anal.a to show the poi utnevs of a Wea1....t00 l*"' went over ton chair and picked up, as he thotight, Mrs. Itarries opera cloak which he put very carefullt: round her shoulders. Hut a roar of freighter showed bum that instead of putting the opera cloak there, lie had put air own pnir of ineopreetthleo." r Loud laughter/ In lhlj Illckette favourite sinter Fanny dad. She had been hie c instant companion from early childhood arid was herself poiteothed of exceptional ability. In addition to lating a gold niedritiot of the I,"Y"I Academy of Mustc, the could paint end sing tqovly well. There was every protheot of hot corning very prominently before the British public, whet, lbu fell disease consumption laid its hand upon her and she died while • cthiparetively young woman. It ems a very great grief to Dickens an two or three year. after her deaih, while thinking of n paper to write for "Household Words "ha rententhered how, an thy and girl, they were in the habit of wandering olinut together hand in lumni, wondering shout a great tinny things but moot of all about the MOM. root thve torn the ilea of writing" .5 child's dreatu of a CAI" (which the lecturer recited). Dickeria had a very great horror of going to funerals and would never go to One if he could poptittly help IL In that respect he prectioed exthtly what he