        <p>i'ERSCNAL IWTRRVI,V W ITII qUEIII V icrom at Buckingham ll,duce.There wave long end interesting conversation lietween them ateott different places Dickens had visited lathe United States, ae well ea eh.: hie own books and current topics, and at the close bar Majesty askei him to .nrl her a full set of his books, autographed in each volume. At the same time she presented hint with a copy of her .ork on the high• lauds, with the autograph inscription " From one of , the very humblest to one of the very emit." of , writers.° (Applause.) This brvik wan .w in the possession of the only other surviving son of the novelist. Mr. Dickens, The lecturer gave an amusing description of e "glow," hunt at Gads If ill end of an adventore with some tramps which bete/ Merton Stone, ICS. Continuiiig hr said they would probably all agree with him, en most readers kind en tic. of the novels did, that about he</p>
        <p>VFM, FINE, OF DICKi,SS. WORKS, from a literary and artistic point or view, was " A tale of twit " which was founded on Thomas Carly Le's marvellotts " History of the Fret,.', revolu- tion.' the hero being the ne'erol i•well, niseoltate, disaipeted barrister, bat still nobleaminded and noble- hearted Sydney Carton. Mr. Dickens then outlined the plot or the novel and concluded the float part of his lecture with a graphic recital of the lam chapter, which describes the •xec•tion of Sydney Coruna. . Resumine his lecture, Mr. Dickens said the fleet hook ' Marl. Dicke. wrote " Sketch., by lien." , In " David Copperfield,' which Irnii in a nrent mmoture . his a dohlography, Dicken toll how he wrote the eketebee during his spare monients when • reporter in ' the House of Commons. Willa fear and trembling, he dropped them Into the publisher's box, mid, to hie intense .tordithrnent, • week or so afterwards the whole of the mty of London w-,e ringing with the question Who the Dickens is bloc ?" In a very soon ape. of time he found himself on the top brooch of the literary tree. The sketches were mostly descriptive of scan. of Load. life, interspersed with short humor°. stories. The lectorer then greatly entertained the audience with no account of " The Bloomsbury Christening." !Staving related the circumetthcas antler which Dickens purchased 6d'. Hall, where he spent the happiest years of his life, the lecturer told a mory of the kindly thouchtfultiess with whieh the novelist looked after the mother awl daughter of sn old school fellow who had to go abroad on military service. A fern months before his death, Dickens received a litter from • Liverpool merchant who said he attri- holed /lynch of his success in life to the teaching in the novelist'a writings. Ile therefore hoped he could be pardoned for sending him</p>
        <p>A A 1.1. A cheque for E 7.00 ARS env-toted. Dickens wee much touched and in returning the cheque, he maid the, although, not a wealthy men, he was fairly prosperous, but the tone and eentinient of the letter had no touched him Bost he would gladly accept some peall pre.nt if the writer thought it to send it in another form. The present soon came—a rich silver filigree hnsket for flowers, eccompanied by a massive centre piece, the design for which represented the newton, with the exception of winter which was not depicted becalms the kindly donor said 't he could not send winter to one with. whom be associated 01 that WAR lightest and brightest in his life." One or two critics who could not possibly kayo known anything about Dickens' privato character—hie charity, generoeity, thoughtfulnets for other, and self- abeegatii;n—hail ventured to assert that he wee an irreligious man. "lit show the injostice of Bus asperti to the lecturer iinoted It letter which Dickens nddre sed to his son Edward when he went out to Australis ix " Never take a meen since ego of anyone in any transact ion, "it ran, " and fleece ho hard upon people who are in your power. Try to do to others RS you would have them do to you, and do not he discouraged if they fail some times. It is much better for you thst they should fail in obeying the greatest role laid down by oar Sisvloar than teat poi should. I put A Nor, Tett/meet among yoor biotite for the very sante reasone and with the very same hoptts that made me write teensy accouat of it for you when you were A little child, becluse it is the beet Book that ever was °raver will be known lathe world and leenause it teacher, you the best lessons by which any humnti troetu re who tries to be faithful to duty can be guided. . . , I now most solemnly impress upon too thig troth and besuty of the Christian religion as it cline—, tot Christ Himself and the impossibility of your goie&amp; Inc wrong if you humbly hot heartily accept it." 00 Jun