        <p>r down hie cheek, end he teas gone from them. As they r locked upon his calm end beentifol fees there was not one amongst them, even if they 60.1 had ouch power i given to them, who would have called his sweet spirit hack to earth. Thus plumed calmly and peacefully . away one of the greatest writers of any age who, an he hated hcpoeriny, malice, deceit and uneharitablenene. denotinced thorn •ices with • ruthiens hand end with a scathing pen, and who as he loved mercy, truth. charity, pence. and goodwill, dwelt upon those ounlitieri lovingly rind kiedly. Thouris Carlyle, writing alter Dickenni death, said "My float acquaintance with him commenced offer thirty years ago when he wan a very young man, end every new meeting shows I me some new trait in ton churecter, until at lent I had ruche recognition of him, as I have rarely had for env other man of my time —the cote], lovable, seer Amiable Dickone. every inch of trim no benefit man.' Dean Stanley, at the end of the beeutiful funerel sermon which he prenched at Wentminster Ahhey, Ti, another great humorist who line buried in thin church have been sacrihod the swords ' Ilia death eclipsed the enlace of notion,' but of him whine lose we now deplore, would venture to any in humbler langusge ' No ewe wren more mourned : no one .al more beloved.'" With whet words could he better conclude thnt credo end imperfect lecture than eith those which the master hi...lroned at the end or that chepuer where inn Kr 4eserlhed the death of little Paul Dombey. " l'he out, old fathion ! The fashion that cairn in with our firer. ',ciente and will last tincnanged nail our men ham run tut course, fled the wide firmament in rolled up like • scroll. The old, old faxhion—Denth ! But thank UM, all who SO9 it., for thnt oldert ftshion yet, of Immortelity. And look upon in, angels of young children, with regeriln not unit • inatrioterni, when tire nwilt riser bears its to the oCenn !" (Appinane). rne Chairmen win thnnked for preeiding,