        <p>Tnci,Miaif..Ahingdon thought it wag. great pity that they hail no details of the Tory Party's pro. portal, Sloe. Mr. Joseph Chamberlain introduced his proposals nine years ago th•re boil icon ninny mutila- tions and aniumainationn of Tariff" (Inform "an various objections were advanced 'against it. Even now there war nothing hotn vague,indettnite and nebuloun scheme bolore the country. r Anoint..., I.ast week the Prime Minim., (Mr. Stannfleld) told theta that England woo the richent country in the world in industrial produe• Iron, and then evidently foarfill lent he had made an awkward adrularion added that that wnS 00 account of the wealth atoreil up 7,0 years ago. flow indontrini prodoction could be stored up be failed to ondermand,for wealth stored up for 50 aratv could not be very valuable to-day esoept in the ca. of (nod and property Mr. WOUIVRTON (C., N. Bristol) thought it was a di•gra. to England that it was proisible for Um Cnited Staten to maim preferential nrr interments with , Canada, as the States would bit ahle to push their own articles iii referents to Englilh for they would be numb cheaper than we could send thero. Ile contended that unemployment and the need for the Poor Law ens mitered for Iry our Baca nentent. To give a prefer.ce to the Colonies woold only mean a aninll tan, and that would consolidate the Empire. The Radicals acknow- ledged that all was not well, bt.t had not brought in one single echeme to sore thennelven.("A voice: flow shout , the Budget ?") By vary komll tag on manufactured goods the home market would lie improved COnsidP, ably end wonld find Incrermed employment a, the dumper was &amp;implored. (Applatt.). Mr. G. CIIA II firhiLA ri L. Gorton), naid thot no evidence had been brought forward to prove that our fiscal nyatem we, not adapted to our commercial requirements and male.s noton evidence wan proltmed to that effect they had no jitattilcatioir in revolutionis- ing the nacal fitment of this country. (Opponition