        <p>Mr. Pow,: \Vicki side 'S., Anglereal in denting with the contention of the Clovernmen; that tariffs wrield provide employment, mid that the aim of econorniets should he not to provide work, hut to increase prod,- tion.lt did not follow that wirework would mean more production, for wealth Wag not determined by the , amount of Lahour, lint by what labour prodoced. The 1 more Olt:lent labour wee, the greater vvive the wealth ' produced and as the denten.] for employment came from . the net national wealth. the greater the wealth pro- domed the greater would be the demand fir the agent. of produci ion. As Englishmen were so lacking in imaginstion is to require a thing in the concrete, he would try to make his point clear by an illustretlon and for that purpose he would severe. the oath of two workers of raryirig efficiency. The euperior worker produced in eight hoots artist it took the other 10 hours to pr•cluce, arid from that they would era that the amount of Inhour did not determine the antorint of the product. Following that further they would lind thnt if both workmen worked 10 boort, each, the more efficient would boors greater amount of production end would, therefore, hove a greater power of demand for the labour of others—Ct., he would have a greater excbange value. The amount of wotk dui not decide the amount of production, sad if they lessened produce Con they lemened the demand for employment. So anything that interfered with the efficiency of labour would reduce production, and lessen the mill for levier. The great advent/me of the freedom of trade was that it determined whet a country wee hest adopted for, and the great drewbeck of tariff@ was that they prevented a country engsging in industries for which it was most suitable. If, there- fore, by protective duck., they crested industries for which a country rota not adopted they diverted it. enemies into lees productive chunnels mil interiered with the efficiency of labour. Tort would mean less 