        <p>The recent increase in the price of Anitralian wine, in thia country how caused some sorrier, to the uninitiated in new of the growing popularity of there wines among British consumers. inqoirie. made of the leading limn of Australian wine merchants elicit the information that this increasing demand ie one of the factore g morning the increased price, the other factor being the present inability of the Auatralisn firm to maintain evert their nortnal output That dread enemy a the wine grower, phylloxera, hart ravaged the vineyards of Victoria and New South Wales, and not until the niggly-planted phylloxera- resisting •ineacome into bearing can the demand of the British consumer be adequately met. In the meantime the British market hair en content itself with a supply still further diminished by the growing rtreference of the Aturtralian people for the prod.&amp; of their own vines, and the larger consumption of Australian brandies. The firm referred to above elate that they are determined under all circumstance• to maintain the high quality r,l their brands.</p>
