      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>National Union of Journalists.</head>
        <p>National Union of Journalists.</p>
        <p>The second annual dinner in connection with the , Warrington. Widnes and Runcorn branch of the National Union of Journalists was held at the Royal Oak hotel, Biidge-street,Warriugton,on Saturday even- ing, and proved a complete success. Over twenty members and friends assembled under the genial presidency of Mr. H. Corrin, of Runcorn (chairman of the branch), the chief guest being Mr. W. N.Watts, of Manchester (general secretary of the Union). Dinner was served in Mr. Sangster's best style, the catering giving the utmost satisfaction. The loyal toasts were proposed from the chair, and duly honoured. The toast of the evening, "The N.U.J.," was submitted in felicitous terms by Mr. W. Mason, of Warrington. Mr. Watts, who was sceorded musical honours, responded, and gave an interesting review of the work of the I Union, which now has 72 branches, with a member- ship of over 2,000. The Warrington branch, be said, was one of the best managed in the whole Union,l At this stage Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., and Mr.l Frank Turner, the veteran seperinteudent of! the Warrington Fire Brigade, who had just returned from Loodon, after receiving the Edward Medal at the hands of the King, joined the company. Their health was enthusiastically drunk, on the proposition of the Chairman. and Mr. Smith respoded in his happiest vein. He referred to the spirit of camaraderie which exists mongst journali.ts, and said Britain had cause to be proud of its Press, which was the cleanest in the world. Mr. Smith then delighted the assembly with a dramatic recitation, When I was twenty-one," which was so cleveriy given that an encore was accorded, and he responded with • vivid and amusing account of an old man's visit to the haunts of his younger days at Oxford. Mr. G. H. Warburton Steel proposed the toast," The host and hostess," and Councillor ti. Archer responded on behalf of Mr. and Mrs. Sangster. The health of the Chairman was cordially honoured, on the propo- ,' skim) of Mr' T. Weston. Daring the evening songs were given by Councillor Archer, Misers, B. Barer and H. Walker, and recitals by Mr. H. Corrin, Mr. R. Garner was an efficient accompanist. An artistic ..onvenir prognanme had been deigned by Mr. C. A. Pennington, of Widnes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SArCRDAY. MARCH 4. Mt</p>
        <p>There is, perhaps, too WHAT THE much disposition to DEBATE c•mplain of what are REVEALS, sometimes called the long-dra sn-out debates of Parliament. It does often seem, in fact, that business which might have been trans- acted in a few hours is stretched out over as many days. It was this which most astonished those representatives of Labour who first found themselves in the House of Commons in 1906. They had been accus- tomed to the rapid methods of the Trade Union Congress, and they did not enough attend to the distinction between a meeting of delegates and an assembly of representatives. It is the glory of the House of Commons that it is a deliber, tive Chamber. It is under no sort of obligation to hustle.' On the contrary, it is bound by custom and tradition and the public interest to take it. time. Measures passed ins hum, cannot, of course, be so well considered, though they might on occasion be quite as admirable as I those which have been so fully debated as to make it practically impossible that there can be any argument on either side which has not been placed before those with whom the decision rests.</p>
        <p>Four days have seemed an extravagant length of time under the circumstances to allot to a second reading debate on the Parliament Bill. But this generous provision for extensive speechmaking seems to us to have amply justified itself in more ways than one. Listening to many of the speeches must, we imagine, have been hard work for the strangers in the Gallery, who cannot, like members of the House, escape from a dull speaker whilst still retaining their seats. They must " thole it," as the stoical iScottish people say. The reward of sitting out the debate is that every now and then there is a speech which ought by no chance to be missed by those who are closely interested in the subject of the dis- cussion. Such a speech was that made by Mr. PHILIP SNOWDEN on Wednesday night. It stated with singular clearness and force the reasons why the Labour party objects to a Second Chamber, and why it should prefer a clearance to a reconstruction. But also it stated in a vivid and striking manner the objections which Labour entertains in respect of the recent action of the House of Lords. The Trades Disputes Act was passed,.. said Mr. SNOWDEN, because the Peers were afraid of the shadow of democracy that lay behind it. It is impossible to get a really representative House of Commons because the Peers threw out the Plural Voting i 'll. Mr. SNOWDEN has lived for some years in Ifondon, but he could not vote at the last General Election because the Lords rejected's measure which would have placed a London voter in the same position as a voter at Manchester or Liverpool, or at Plymouth or Norwich, or any other of the large centres of population. What really is that House of Lords which insists on the preservation of these indefensible anomalies? It is usually, said Mr. SNOWDEN, 60 Peers out of more than 600. There are instances in which one of these Peers has more legislative power than 30,000 voters.</p>
        <p>There is at least one other important respect in which the prolongation of the debate has mostly justified itself. It has ' revealed the shifting and confused and ' illogical position of the Tory party. "The Times," which may still be properly described as the leading organ of Conservatism, sug- gests the formation of a new Fourth party, which, it implies, could be constructed with less objection than in a former case, because the Conservative party in these days has no policy. It is a startlingly .candid admission, but it is not at all in advance of the facts. The truth has been painfully revealed during the debate which closed on Thursday night. It has been revealed not only to the Government side of the House, but to the members of the Opposi- tion themselves. They have found them- selves drifting helplessly and sometimes in opposite directions. When they are brought together to say the same thing it is only by the crack of the party whip. The confusion. springs out of the now universally recognised , fact that the Government has made out its 'case and must pass its Bill. Nothing remains now, in fact, but the formalities—which will doubtless be prolonged.</p>
        <p>The spirit which LETTING THE CAT animates the Opposi- OUT OF tion in their proposals THE BAG. for House of Lords Reform as an alterna- tive—not, as the Government demands. a I complement—to the Parliament Bill, was unconsciously illustrated in Monday's debate by a private Member, Mr. Tobin. He laid stress on the argument that, if the Parlia- ment Bill had been in the Statute Book in 1906, the Education Bill of that year might have become law against the wishes of the people. The Bill was passed by an enormous majority in a House of Commons just chosen at an election in which the Education ques- tion had been a notable factor. But Mr. Balfour's Education Act of 1902 was passed in glaring defiance or election pledges. yet the House of Lords afforded no check. Mr. Tobin has let the cat out of the bag with a vengeance!</p>
        <p>Mr. Ly ttleton, on Tues- TORIES AND OLD day, was as maladroit AGE PENSIONS. as Mr. Tobin. He declared that "no one could dispute that all the social reforms pro- posed by the Government had been accepted by the Unionist Party and had passed through the House of Lords, with improve- ments suggested by the majority of the Upper House." The Old Age Pensions Act would have been wrecked by the Lords' amendments if the Speaker of the House of Commons had not ruled them out as a breach of privilege. Do's the Unionist Party repudiate Old Age Pensions as a "social reform ?"</p>
        <p>The two champions of THE CHURCH AND the liquor interest to THE TRADE. whom we referred last week have both taken, up the cudgels this week,and with a tolerance , for which they hasten to commend us, and for which we feel distinctly virtuous,we print their letters in full. Mr. Carey pleads for the same treatment and consideration on behalf of the liquor trade as we would accord any other industry carried on with public sanction and legislative support. Our reply to that plea is that the trade has already received much more than its fair share of " consideration " from both the public and the State. Did not Mr. Balfour's Act establish a monopoly value in the licence which conferred millions upon the brewers at the expense of the community? No other trade stands in so favoured a position, and no other trade is is so nationally demoralising. Instead of being merely licensed, it is privi- leged and protected by partisan legislation. That it should seem, now, to be plundered through a turn in the political wheel only a natural consequence. "A Fortiori" seems to think that because the Trade bolsters up the Church it is entitled to absolution from all the sins laid at its door. His argument , suggests the same mental twist as that of the brigand who salved his conscience by paying for Masses for the souls of his victims.</p>
        <p>There is an unmistak - THE CHItDREN'S able reproach to War- SUMMER CAMP. rington in the fact that the subscriptions to the Poor Children's Summer Camp only total £133 tis. 6d. for the year just closed. But for donations, and the generous help of Lady Greenall, this grand institution would be 'starved into inanition. Warrington ought to contribute at least £5OO a year to the maintenance of the camp, and provide for double the number of poor children who . have so far, been treated to a health-giving annual holiday at Prestatyn. We cannot conceive of any charity which should make a stronger appeal to every individual who realises his share of responsibility to the community. The poor we have always with us, and that being so the least we can do is to brighten the lives and lessen the suffering of the innocent children who are born to poverty, and, in the main, condemned to bear its shackles through life. It has been suggested that the usefulness of the Camp should be extended by finding accommodation for the poor little cripples who do not come in for its benefits with the ordinary day-school children. " Ways and means" are again the stumbling block. Will not some of our well- to-do townspeople shoulder the burden?</p>
        <p>The Government has THE FLOWING had a remarkable" vote TIDE, of confidence "for its policy from the nation in recent by-elections. The Tory majority was brought down almost to vanishing point at Horncastle. In the Westbury Division of Wiltshire Mr. Geoffrey Howard, in spite of the cry of" a Wiltshire man for Wiltshire," has secured the seat by a substantial majority. And in the Forest of Dean the Liberal Candidate achieved the miraculous by winning by a larger majority than was given to the late Sir Charles Dilke.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS. 1....YN...‘,..NW \ ~.,s.,"•''</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS. 1....YN...‘,..NW \ ~.,s.,"•''</p>
        <p>So far as committee men are concerned, the ladies are the best workers" was the curiously worded testimony of a speaker at the annual meeting of a local philanthropic organization this week. • •J We wonder if it is another case of "too old at forty " 1 The Sanitary Works Com- mittee has been inspecting its stud and has decided to destroy two horses which were deemed to be aged and worn-out, and replace them by new ones. There is no old-age pension scheme in this department! • •</p>
        <p>More hope for the ladies—not votes this lime! The Baths Committee of the Corpor- ation has decided to apply for sanction to borrow £8,347 for the erection of new swimming baths. It may be presumed that some adequate accomodation for lady bathers will be included in the new scheme.</p>
        <p>The Burnley Electricity Committee have set Warrington a good example by deciding to hand over in relief of rates, £4,500 of their profits for the municipal year just closing, instead of the £4,000 estimated at the begin- ning of the year. The Committee has also decided to build another Tramway waiting room and shelter at a cost of £270.</p>
        <p>The Health Commit-tee is persevering with its policy of improving slum properties in Warrington, and a large number of houses have recently been reported upon by the Medical Officer, and inspected by the Com- mittee. In some cases a closing order was made, but in most the owners promised to carry out the requirements of the Committee and make the property sanitary and habitable. * 4 * . .</p>
        <p>Many collectors and others will have read with interest the remarkable case which Lord Chesterfield won in respect to one of the suits of armour disposed of along with the other art treasures formerly at Holme Lacy, his former seat near Hereford, which, it may be remembered, was sold two years ago. Holme Lacy is one of the loveliest places on the Wye, but it was costly to keep up, and the once great possessions of the Chesterfields were divorced from the title after the death of the seventh earl, when they went under the terms of his will to his only sister and her descendants. • _ •</p>
        <p>The Chesterfield peerage has, indeedj experienced many vicissitudes. The fourth earl, the famous writer of the " Letters' ("a wit among lords and a lord among.wits left noissue,and the fifth earl had to be sought, consequently, in a descendant of a younger son of the first earl, who died in 1656. Further, when the direct succession failed twice subsequently, it was a descendant of that same younger son who preserved the title. The present earl's father, who suc- ceeded his kinsman as ninth earl, was one of these lucky descendants.</p>
        <p>The second reading of the Parliament Bill was carried on Thursday night, after three divisions by a majority of 125, one more than on the first reading. Could anything testify more plainly the solidgeity of the people's party in the House of Commons.</p>
        <p>. . • . • Mr. and Mrs. A. H. Crosfield are at present holidaying at Cannes. We notice in the " World •' for this week that at the Golf Ball, Countess Zia Torby (elder daughter of the Grand Duke Michael) made her debut and led the cotillon in company with Mrs. A. H. Crosfield. • ~ •</p>
        <p>Mr. A. E. Pigott, secretary of the Man- chester Crematorium, in a lecture the other evening, stated that the burials in the two Corporation cemeteries exceeded 266,800. If placed end to end round the eityloundaries the coffins would encircle the city with a wall over 12 feet high. Laid out in a con- tinuous row an express train travelling at the rate of sixty miles an hour would take over five hours to pass them It does mot seem improbable that, as cemeteries become more and more congested, something like compulsory cremation of the dead will be in vogue before many decades elapse. • •</p>
        <p>Additional delivery stations in connection with the Borough Library are to be opened in Bewsey-road. Orford-lane and Latchford. We have heard it stated that one of the eoonomies of the near future was to be the closing of existing branch libraries. Evidently the Committee has other, and wiser views on the matter. It would be false economy and unenlightened policy to I restrict the opportunities of reading which are afforded to the townspeople of Warrington. . .</p>
        <p>The weekly revenue returns show that up to last Saturday the increase over last year is 584 millions. Of this the last four weeks have yielded 114 millions. The total estim- ated increase for the whole year is 68 millions There are still five weeks to come in the financial year, so that if the present rate of receipts is maintained there should be a sur- plus of several millions so far as revenue is concerned. But that is only part of the story, since it remains to be seen whether the expenditure has reached the estimated total or not. As a fact, the estimated expenditure of late has usually exceeded the actual expenditure.</p>
        <p>• • Among stories told in Mr. Ramsay Colles's _rish reminiscences, "In Castle and Court House," just published, is one about James Payn, the novelist. It appears that Payn received a letter from an unknown person praising his works. " I liked to hear my book. called 'works,' "said Payn," and II replied. He wrote me again, and I wrote in return a jocose letter. He replied in like terms. I again wrote telling him a funny story. He sent me a funny story. I capped it, and in a short time got a Roland for my Oliver. . . . Finally, I got an indignant letter, commencing, 'Sir, are you aware that I am a woman!' "I often," said Payn, "blush under the bedclothes when I think of the stories I told that woman!" • • •</p>
        <p>A conference representing a large number of local authorities was held in London, on Monday, to consider the demands which should be pressed upon the Post Office in connection with the taking over of the National Telephone Company's undertaking. A deputation was sent to the Postmaster- General, who promised that there should be an inquiry if the public were not satisfied with the reasonableness of the charges to be made when the Post Office announced them. He stated, however, that he only desired a moderate return upon the outlay. He dis- couraged the idea that municipalities should undertake services in their own localities. Warrington was represented on the deputa- tion by Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., and the deputy town clerk (Mr. A. T. Hallaway ), pressure of the work preventing the Mayor's attendance.</p>
        <p>We learn that there was nearly a tragedy associated with the departure for Canada of our esteemed townsman, Mr. E. Pickavance. A number of his friends gave Mr. Pickavance a hearty send-off on Saturday, and on their return from Liveroool an incident occurred which resulted in the party reaching Warrington with white faces and shaken nerves. One of the party was standing up in the railway carriage when the door suddenly flew open, and he I fell through the aperture with the train going at express speed. Luckily those near by managed to seize the unfortunate gentle- man by the coat and arms, and he was with great difficulty dragged back to safety. For a second or two his life hung in the balance, and it was by luck more than anything else that he was prevented from falling on the line. • * • _ .</p>
        <p>Apart from the personality of the lecturer, which was probably the chief attraction to the large audience that gathered to hear Mr. Alfred Tennyson Dickens on Tuesday even- ing, the lecture itself had a more than ordinary interest, for it did not deal with the life of the master novelist in the usual stereotyped way, but was full of reminiscences of which the public has hitherto heard little. Mr. Dickens' splendid fund of amusing anecdotes was a particularly welcome feature. One of the stories deserves mention here. A Rev. Mr. Clarke was giving recitals from Dickens in Australia and one evening his large and aristocratic audience happened to include a Mr. and Mrs. Harris—two of the best known inhabitants of the district. All went well until Mr. Clarke came to the little episode where Sarah Gamp and Betsy Prig take supper together. To quote Mr. Dickens: Everything goes on swimmingly so long as they confine themselves to the pickles, salmon and cucumber, but when Betsy Prig takes a little more than her share of the cordial known as gin, she becomes somewhat argumentative and quarrelsome, and winds up by telling Sarah Gamp that "she don't believe there aint any such person as Mrs. Harris." Mr. Clarke had got that far when, to his astonishment, up sprang Mr. Harris in the middle of the hall and exclaimed " This has gone quite far enough. I didn't bring my wife here to be grossly insulted." And a moment later he pranced out of the hall, leaving his poor wife in the midst of the giggling audience.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>CUP-TIE AT WIGAN.</head>
        <p>THE CUP-TIE AT WIGAN.</p>
        <p>Warrington s Team</p>
        <p>The Warrington tearn t, in.et Wigan at Wigan in the second round of the Cup-ties to-day was chosen last night as follows :—Tilley, full back ; rnsworth, Dickenson, Taylor and Waywell, three-quarters ; Creesey and Brooks, half backs; Thomas, Angara, Naylor, Morgan, Johnston and aelhorn, forwards. reserve, Poison.</p>
        <p>LINSEED COMPOUND' for Coughs, Cold&amp; and B-oucbirel trouble.. 91d., Is. lid. Of Cberuists.—Ad.</p>
        <p>LOCAL PARS. S</p>
        <p>Marriage of Mr. John Watson. The marriage was solemnized at Fairfield Pres-, byterian Church on Tuesday of Mr. John Watson, F.R.A.S., Wilson-Patten-strert, Warrington, and Min Mary Elizabeth Watson, of Blackburn. The Rev. J. P. ilogarth officiated. " Sexton Blake " at the Theatre. Next week the great drama, " Sett xi Blake," the detective," will be presented for the first time in Warrington. It is founded on the celebrated stories which have appeared in certain popular periodical., and illuarstes in thrilling style the different methods of criminal investigation, the introduction of the pedigree bloodhound" Pedro " being a novel feature. Mr. Murray Yorke will impersonate the intrepid and resourceful detective. Howley Cricket Club. The annual dance in connection trill, the above was held in the Co-operative Ball on Friday evening of last week, and proved • great succees During the evening Mr. Harold Smith, SIP., addressed borne cordial remarks to the members. Mr. G. Cnderwood's orchestra pl:yed excellently for the dancing, and the M.C.'s were Messrs. W. Warburton and F. Herman. The catering was in the hand. of Mr. F. Harding. ,Slegolar Warrington Accident.</p>
        <p>Arthur Goodier, a j-niner's improver, 19 years of age, living at 7,4, Scott-street, Warrington, was admitted to the local infirmary suffering from a lacerated wound on his left leg. It appears that he was pushing a handcart in Queen-street when a lorry caught one of the wheel and one of the broken shafts entered his leg, inflicting a nasty wound. lle was detained in the institution. Admiral Nelson Lodge (10 0.F.) M.U.</p>
        <p>At the above lodge on Monday' evening last an interesting presentation took place when P.G. James Bemis's was the recipient of a Past Grand medal for successfully going through the chairs of his lodge. P.P.O.M. Frank Webster in making the presentation, eulogised hie services and PG. Burrowe feelingly responded. Songs were rendered during the evening by PG. W. Webster, P.G. W. Hewlett, P.O. N. Hare, Bros. W. Dilworth sod T. Healing. P.G. W. Barnea accompanied.</p>
        <p>'St. Thomas' Teaparty. As usual the annual teaparty in connection with St. Thomas' Church, Stockton heath, passed off well. About 150 people sat down to an excellent tea on Tuesday evening, after which their number. were greatly augmented, to enjoy a concert arranged by Mrs. Arthur Willett. The Vicar (Rev. J. Collins) pre.,ided. The items received the appreciation due, the artistes being Misses A. Bridge and Dorothy IlalItnell; Messrs. W. W. Whittle, A. Lloyd, Master Harry Dodd, and the Beresford Comedy Sketch Company. Women's Circle.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday last a social in connection with the Warrington Wonten's Circle, was held in the Circle rooms, Bridge-etreet. After tea a miscellaneous pro- gramme was gone through, which included • piano- forte duet by Miss U Jones and Mies Armitage ; recitations by Miss Taylor, Miss Fleming and Miss Elliott, songs by Mies Hamer (all encored) ; tnueical monologue', Mr. Spiogelhalter (enrol-tech and piano and violin s dos by Miss Fleming, Miss V Jones and Mks Armitage, 31re Lightburs proving herself an effective accompanist to the songs and monologues. All the items were well received, and everyone spent an enjoyable evening.</p>
        <p>Warrington Photographic Society. The society was honoured nn Tuesday by the preaence as lecturer of Mr. T. Lee yms, His subject wee " Finishing the Exhibition Print," and Mr Syms, after many years both as exhibitor, winner and judge at almost every important exhibi- tion in Britain, has hardly a rival in Lancashire as a pictorial photographer, and no one certainly could 'Amite such a subject better. The lecture was simply the amplification t,f the word "Trifle, make perfection hut perfection is no trifle." And anyone who has seen Mr. Sym's work will ha inclined to repeat that saying. A capital audience accorded for once, at least, very sincerely, the usual vote of thanks. The secretary, Mr. Holloway, again urged the formation of a record section of the society for the preservation of perman- ent pictures of the many interesting relics of the old. town that are no rapidly disappearing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>Warrington's Opposition to St. Helens Bill.</head>
        <p>Warrington's Opposition to St. Helens Bill.</p>
        <p>A conference f local authorities effect€4l by the Bill which the St. Ilelens Corporation is promoting was held at Liverpool on Thursday. The Bill embodies powers to supply gas outside the borough of St. Helens, and as Buttonwood is included in the proposed area, the Warrington Corporation considers its rights would be seriously affected. At the confer. ence Warrington was represented by the Mayor (Councillor Dr. Joseph). Alderman Pierpoint and the Deputy Town Clerk (Mr. A. T. Hallastray). On the proposition of Alderman Pimp' int, seconded by the Mayor of Wigan. it mg decided to take concerted action against the Bill in the House of Commons with a view to the elimination of the objectionable causes. The Warrington Corporation ban already deposited a petition against the Bill, Ragged School Mission. ~• . . .</p>
        <p>.cti-appeal has just been issued to subscribers, for increased pecuniary support in view of the extension of their work in connection with the new Labour Home. The :I:lrd annual report states that the com- mittee feel that the satisfactory position shown is an evide ice of the publir appreciation of the valuable work accomplished. The premises acquired nearly three years ago as a Labour Home were opened in November last, and work commenced on a small scale, which the c .MMittle hoops wal develop as time goes on. The sum of oho tt £l.OO has been spent upon machisery, furnitsre and wages, which has been necsasary for the work contemplated, also another £.2.)) which tnakes 0,1) is all which instill required to free the Fln oe from debt. It is confidently hoped that this amoont may be speedily contributed to enable the Committee to proceed with this good work. The , ho As of the school, mission and Labour Home are opts for inspection by any contributor. No one is suirorisei to solicit subscriptions who does not hold c 41,min¢ book bearing the official stamp of the comminee.Toe accounts of the Mission are audited and certified by Messrs. Bennett and Benuett,Market Gate. VACUCM CLEANERS OR blre, Ss. per day ; MARNIA LADE CUTTERs, IS. per day.—J. S. GARLAND, 51.55 Bridge et.., Warrington.—Adel. Pretty Local Wedding. • • - -</p>
        <p>A pretty wedding wits solemnized at the Gospel' Hell, Forster-street, Wanington, on Thursday, the contraction parties being Mints Lily Green. eldes t daughter of Mr. James Green. 74, Marahhouee-lane , Warrington, end Mr. Thomas Hugo De Ville, only son of Mr. Thomas De Ville. of Uttoseter. Mr. Thomas Holt performed the marriage ceremony. and as the happy couple left the Hall. the organist, Mr. W. Street, played klendelssolon's Wedding March. The bride who was given away by her father, was attired in &amp;dress of ivory silk. She carried a bouquet of tulips, enrage lilies and smilax, with streamers of white silk, and wore a wreath of orange blossom with veil Tbe bridesmaids were Misses Maude Green and Bertha de Ville (sisters of the bride and bridegroom). Their dresses were of white Swiss embroidery, with old gold mashes. They wore black picture hats, and carried bouquets of daffodils, and their ornament. were pretty gold brooches with chains attached, *6 gifts of the bridegroom. The duties of best man were discharged by Mr. Selwyn Forster, of Stoke. The reception was held at the bride's home, after whirl the happy couple left for London. The bride's travelling dress consisted of • Donegal tweed costume, with black picture hat trimmed with French miss. The happy couple were recipients of handsome and numerous presents from friends and relatives, includ• presents from the Sunday school class, teachers and assmbly of the Gospel Hall, Warrington.</p>
        <p>T" 1</p>
        <p>Town (Mandl. The monthly meeting of the Town Countil sill be held on Tuesday evening oest. Freemasonry. The in.tallation of the three ptincipah of the Gilbert Greenell Chapter No. 1,250 took place on Tuesday at the lion Hotel, their mines being E. Comps. C. Leigh, ME.?,., J. Beswick, If., and J. Waldron, J. The r rimers were invested as follows —Comps. T. D. Ararat, P.Pr.G.O., Scribe E.; T. Healing, Scribe N.; C. G. McDowell, PS. ; W. M. Omen, let Assist. S. ; J. W. Bowers, 2nd Artist S.; tarkineon, organist; F. J. Dell, janitor ; and E. Comp C. flollsnd, P.Z., treasurer. Wanington Co-operative iSselety's Concerts. One of the I.eriee of concerts given during the Winter tone held in the Wesleyan Schoolroom, Latch- ford, on Tuesday evening Mr. B. Farrar (chairman of the Educational (ommittee) presided and spoke mon the work of thelsociety and of the C.W.S. The following urtMles were greatly enjoyed :—Miss Silcock, " A May Morning ' and " Good-bye, ; Madame Eckersley " Valley of the Sea" and '' Aug a. MtDonald "; Mr. Wilooth, Thora " and " I seek for thee in every:tiower " ' Mr. W Humphrey., " I fear no foe " and " The old soldier." Mr. Bennett greatly ansueed the audience with hi• humorous smogs and wee repeatrdly enrolled. Mr. T. Tanner ably acCompanied. Amy was a large and appreciative audience.</p>
        <p>Sir Gilbert Greenell Presented at Court. The King held the second Levee of the reason at St. James's Palace. on Tuesday. fins Majesty, who again wore navel uniform, drove dawn Irma Buckingham Palace. attended by the Master of the I Horse and other official., just before noon. An escort of the Household Cavalry accompanied the Royal carriages from Buckingham Palace to St. James'e. Included in the Royal circle were the Duke of Connaught, Admiral ince Louis of Betterffiarg, the Duke of Trek, Prince Alexander of Batteubarg, and the Prime Minister. Among those present, in addi- tion, were the Archbishop of York, the Lord Chan- cellor, the Duke of Norfolk, General Sir John French. Mr. ll'Kenna, Mr. Austen Chamberlain, Mr. Walter Long, Mr. George Wyndham, and a large general com- pany of naval and military officers, and representa- tives of the Church, of the law and politico. The Levee concluded just before one o'clock, and his Majesty drove back to Buckingham Palace under wort. Among the numerous presentations to His Majesty was that o Sir Gilbert Uremia, Bait ,by the Right Hon. Ailwyn Fellow.. Field Club. Mr. W. Webster presided at a meeting held in the , Museum Lecture Room when Mr. H. Womerrley read a paper on " Dipters." He began by indicating the , relationship of title order—of which the gnat and the house fly are types—to other groups of insects. Of the Diptero or two-winged flies mole than 3,0011 species are known. and as the smaller kinds are being more systimatically studied it seems likely that this number will be oubstantial/y increased. They range is size from a length of about three inches down to form so minute as scarcely to Its visible to the unaided e3e. The general anatomy was next described, , special features of the structure and of the life ' hirgories being dealt with as the various families and sub-families were passed in review. Many of the curiously interesting reproductive experiments within the. order were recounted in a clear and interesting style, with descriptions of the habit, of the larvic which are often parasitic. The frequent mimicry, I both in shape and colour, of other insects, especially Of been and waspe by the eyrphids or hover flies, wan reamed to. The leCthrer was heartily end deservedly congratulated during the discussion which followed. Peony Popular Concerts.</p>
        <p>. -.- At the concert in Bold-street School on Saturday evening the artistes were Miss May Isherwcorl and Melton.. J. Willett, George Gough, Davie., and David Sutton, and the Warrington Band of Hope prize choir. The programme was a most interesting and enjoyable one and was much apprecisted by a large audience. Messrs. Gough and Willett gave an effec- tive rendering nf "Flom gently Dens!' while the former was heard to distinct advantags, in the song " The old bargee." Mica May Isherwood was warmly applauded for an expressive interpretation of " Annie Laurie," and Mr. Davies gave a number of concertina solos in a very creditable manner. The choir's rendering of " The oars are splashing lightly" showed careful training. The humorous side of the programme sae in the capable hands of Mr. David 'Setts.° who caused great merriment with his Lanca- shire dialect selections. The Rev. W.Seed occupied the chair,and delivered a abort address during the interval. Alderman Bowen and Town Planning. . . .</p>
        <p>At the National Conference on Town Planning at Liverpool yesterday week, the morning sitting was devoted to the consideration of the extent to which a local authority should, under a town-planning scheme, relax or alter conditions relating to widths of roads and methods of road construction. Mr. Raymond FOWiLI suggested that if they reduced the number of houses to the acre they would approximate the treatment of roads in a garden city to the treatment found satisfactory in a country district. Heur, Lear.) In addition t having main arteria, secondary, and residential 10.018, they , should have small drives for giving communication to a limited number of houses. Alderman Bennett (Warrington), questioned whether the schemes men- tioned by Mr. L nwin were applicable to conditions in the industrial towns with which most ot them were associated, where a great number of the residents only received ns. or 24.., week wages? How could they provide these ideal conditions low these men at rests corresponding to their means? He had always ',roam up against the atone wall of that practical difficulty.</p>
        <p>Richmond Gas Stove and Meter Co., Ltd The report of the directors, covering the year 1910 to be prevented to the twentieth ordinary general meeting of the shareholders on Thuraday next, states that the balance standing to the credit of probt and 10n..i account (after deducting Debenture nterest), iccluding £3,0:10 brought forward, is £30,1159 134.1 which the directors ncommend be appropriated as follows :—Depreciation on buildings, patterns, plant, showrooms, fixtures, &amp;c., £5,434 In. hld.; dividend on Pleferred capital at the rate of 6 per cent, per annum (less income tax) for year ended December 31, 1910, /5,880 IGs. 2d. ; divi- dend (free of income tax) at the _rate of</p>
