        <p>work ii and branches have., as uslnd. been kept good condition and maintained out of revenue.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Society. At the annual meeting of this Society held at the Old Academy the following officers were elected : President, Mr. W. Long: hon. treasurer. Mr. John Fairhurst ; hoe, secretaries, Alderman Bennett and and Mr. J. Richardson ; auditor, Mr. Harry H. Evans. In bin annual teport Aldernian Bennett stated that an appropriate tablet bad been ordered from Messrs. .rab Wedgewood and Sone. Ltd., oi Etruria. to indicate the site of the Roman Station at Vcilderapool and to commemorate the fifteen hundredth sithisersary of the departure of the Romans from Britain. ~ A suit- able ceremony was contemplated, and the coalmine* trusted that the green enclosure which, thanks lo the r\ indefatigable labours of Mr. Thomas May, had proved a perfect treasure-trove of priceless ics, would henceforth be treated with the reverence it deserved and permanently set apart as sacred grou . Reviewing the chief events of the year, th committee noted with pleasure that a vigorou crusade had been inaugurated against the heavy infantile mortality in Warrington ; that our slums were slowly disappearing; and that preliminary steps were being taken to prepare plans I to regulate the growth of the new Warrington on I tines which should be adequate to its increasing needs sad worthy of its ancient fame and present reputa- tion. They trusted that in the preparation of the plans, due care would be taken to preserve, as far as possible, all picturesque and ancient buildings and all places of historical interest or natural beauty. In this connection they observed with special pleasure that the Council were considering the desirability of acquiring Orford Hall, end they trusted that when the time arrived to demolish the Old Fox, in Butter- market-street, it might be possible to re.erect that quaintest bit of Warrington now left to us in aims other suitable locality. The suggestion was also advanced that the signs of old inns which had to be demolished would ultimately had a place in dm' Museum, and • plea was put in for the revival of thee* signboards which added to the picturesqueness of the town.</p>
        <p>Local Law Sawn. Mr. Charles Henry Cee•rortft, jun., eon of Mr. C. H. Unsworth, solicitor, of this town, was amongst the successful candidate' at the recent Preliminary Law Examinations held at the beginning of this month. Young Liberals. Au interesting and instructive series of lectures in connection with the Warriagtoa Young Liberals was , begun lam evening in the Wycliffe Memoritl Hall, when Mr. F. Boater Alcock, M.A., of Bolton, lectured on " Colonial Preference." Councillor J. Hawthorn presided over a sety good attendance. Salvation Army. , .</p>
        <p>A inimical evening was held on Monday in connec- tion with art arranged eerie, of ppular service,. The following artiste* with others took patt :- Mr. And Mrs. Warburton, Mr. Clayton, Mi-e Vihitronre, Miss (otter presided at the organ. Adjutant John Colitis proposed a vote of thanks, and a very enjoyable pro- gramme was brought to a close.</p>
