      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>WALKING DAY.</head>
        <p>WALKING DAY.</p>
        <p>FRIENDLY SOCIETIES AND THE PROPOSED ALTERATION OF DATE.</p>
        <p>In reap-mse to an inviter) at of the Town Clerk a meeting of the Friendly Societies of Warrington was held in the Town Hall on Wednesday evening to con- eider the data it Walking Day. Mr. T. P. Bradshaw (1.0.0. Manchester Unity) presided. The Deputy Tome Clerk .11r. A. T. Hanewsy) explained that at a public meeting recently held it had been decided to hold Walking Day on the day fixed for the Coronation ; last that objeet ion had been taken thereto and that a public mewing w, old shortly be held to further comider the matter. After a dismission it wan resolved ,—" That thin meeting of Friendly Societies of Warrington are of opinion that )Valking Day should not be held upon the Coronation Day but up4a Friday June 2rd." The delegate. then discussed the general arrange- ments for the Coronation in so far an they affect Friendly Societies and the general exprension of opinion was that all societies would heartily co-operate in any arrangements for a public proceesion. The Mayor also invited the members of Frimelly Societies to consider a proposal which he made with regard to the raining of a permanent memorial to take the form of houses for the deserving poor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>CORONATION.</head>
        <p>THE CORONATION.</p>
        <p>PROPOSED £l,OOO SALARY TO THE MAYOR.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the special sub-committee of the Warrington Town Council appointed to make arrange- ments for the local Coronation festivities WBA held at the Town Hall on Wednesday sherd. on, the Mayor presiding. It was decided to recommend the Council to vote the Mayor • sum of .0.,(00 by way of ealary, to cover the expenses of the festivities. No definite arraxgements were arrived at. lota suggestion which found much favour was that each child should be pre tented with a permanent memento of the occasion. The Mayor was requested to call another meeting with a view to Walking Day being fixed for Friday, June 23rd, instead of on Coronation Day,as suggested by the meeting held last week. The meeting hat been fixed for Thursday, the 16th inst., at 8 p.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>EMPIRE SKATING RINK.</head>
        <p>EMPIRE SKATING RINK.</p>
        <p>There h again been an attractive programme at the Empile Skating Rink thin week. The hockey team had a stiff engagement on Wednesday against the crack Salford United team, and did well in finishing ,1111 game on even terms. The game was a close one throughout, at half-time the score being 1-1. During the second moiety the game was still of an even nature, and the game ended in a draw of 2-2. Wadaworth and H. Hemming were the scorers for the Empire. On Thursday evening several competitions wok place. A ladiesis two-step race aura, ted a good number of entries and after an amusing enciiiinter Mist. A. Parkinson was successful in obtaining first place. An egg and spoon race woe another humorous event. Mr. W. Whittaker winning after • very close com- petition.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>There in • full set of events for next week. On Werfneeday a league hockey match will be played between the Fggpire Rink and New Brighton. I here will be the tundil competitions cn Thnrsday evening. All excellent example of the sporting spirit of the hockey team is evidenced by • letter received from the President of the Liverpool and District Amateur Rink Hockey League.lt appears that Warrington were due to play on Wednesday, February 22nd, 1211. at . the Promenade Rink, New Brighton. They were. however, at the last minute left with only bur men instead of five, but nevertheless they decided to play the game. Although the New Brighton team are considered prospective hampions forth. Liverpool and District League the 'maim team gave them a better game than they had nticipated, only losing by four goals to three. The me team were quite p,eas...l with Warringt m's rformance as the following letter testifies : Ca.nadian_ Roller iikating lank, Promenade, New . . . . .....</p>
        <p>tr . . ighton, 27th .k, arch, 19117 To Mr. E. Baxter, Empire Skating Rink Hockey oh, Warrington. Irear ~ir,-1 have been netructed by my Committee to write thanking your leans for the sporting manner shown last Wednesday ' February 92nd. 1911, by playing a match with only Inn; mentor such a sport is rarely met:lhe match was much appreciated by the audience 34 a clean and fast game of hockey. Shall he pleased if you will convey the above to your Committee. Wishing you every ruccess.—Yours faithfully, iSigoeti) J. PooLE, president, Liverpool sad District Amateur Rink ockey League.</p>
        <p>GAMIC( ROLLILRY, special I ie.) at re. Cl. each.—J. S. I•IILAND, itoncuvoit r, Bridge et., Warrisgtoo.—Adet</p>
