      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>IRISH AFFAIRS. NOTES FOR IRISH READERS.</head>
        <p>IRISH AFFAIRS. NOTES FOR IRISH READERS.</p>
        <p>HOME RULE AND ROME RULE.</p>
        <p>We would like to direct the attention everyone interested in the Irish question. the recent statement of Mr. Redmond on the Unionist bogey of Home Rule meaning Rome Rule. It is characteristic of the Irish Leader's insight that he should have selected this point for a recent lengthy statement. Nearly every Irishman in Great Britain who discusses the Irish question with his friends knows how frequently the suggestion is made that theiProtestant minority in Ireland would be oppressed under a Dublin Parlia- ment. A few weeks ago in this column we submitted the free testimonies of representative Protestant ministers Iliving in Ireland which entirely refuted such a theory and now we have Mr. Redmond drawing on his own experience of troubled times in Ireland to show that the Catholic people of that country have ever resisted any idea of interference from Rome in matters of Irish politics. O'Connell's famous declaration that "the Irish would as soon take their polities from Constantinople as from Rome is vindicated in every chapter of modern Irish history, " Rut" say the opponents of Home Rule, "If Ireland got a Parliament of her own, she would at once proceed to alter the laws of the land so as to inflict oppression on the , Protestant Minority." To this, speaking for Nationalist and Catholic Ireland, Mr Redmond replies " I indignantly protest against such a prediction. It is a grievous insult to Ireland. Were I and my colleagues members of an Irish Parliament in which any such proposal to inflict oppression on Protestants were made, we would fight against it to the death." Mr. Redmond proceeds to say that the supremacy lof the Imperial Parliament, which every Nationalist accepts as a prime feature of Home Rule, would remain 'to prevent such an abuse of power. Any proposals which the wit of man can devise, providing they are consistent with ordinary democratic principles, the Irish I party eagerly welcomed, and although it is perhaps too much to hope that no more will be heard of Home Rule meaning Rome Rule we trust that the number of Englishmen who give ear to this absurd cry will grow fewer and fewer.</p>
        <p>TORIES ASKING FOR A FALL Not content with having brought about a signal Home Rule victory once already since Parliament met, the Ulster Unionists are already looking for another fall on the sub- ject One would have thought that the way in which Mr. F. E. Smith and other Opposi- tion orators have on several occasions made I Home Rule dominate the Veto Bill debate would have sufficed. In their political despair however, they seek yet another defeat. The redoubtable Captain Craig has given notice of his intention to move an amendment to the Second Reading of the Veto Bill, declin- ing "to proceed further with a measure which would enable the Government to grant Home Rule to Ireland, against the determined opposition of the whole loyalist population and without first submitting so Constitutional a change to Parliament or the electorate." The gallant captain has evi- dently overlooked Mr. Balfour's pre-election statement that a Liberal return to power meant Home Rule and Mr. Austen Chamber- lain's own grouping of the issues when he put Home Rule as the first item of Liberal policy. It is earnestly to be hoped that this proposed amendment may come on fora full discussion. No greater help to our cause can be imagined than a few more exhibitions of Unionist weakness and vaocillation,-as have been displayed in recent Parliamentary debates.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>NEW CAMPAIGN.</head>
        <p>A NEW CAMPAIGN.</p>
        <p>The announcement that Mr. John Red- mond, together with Messrs. John Dillon, T. P. O'Connor and Joseph Devlin and other loading Nationalists are placing themselves at the disposal of the Liberal organization shows clearly that events are moving quickly in the direction of a great Home Rule cam- paign. Mr. Stephen Gwynn is already engaged on the preliminary arrangementa for a series of mass meetings throughout the country. Literature in the shape of pamphlets, reprints of speeches and leaflets, is now ready for distribution, and the Liberal and Nationalist Committees should make early application'to Mr. J. W. Dulanty,North- enden, Cheshire, who will be ,glad to supply these or any data required for Home Rule propaganda.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
