      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>SUNDAY GAMBLING AT ST. HELENS. CONSTABLE'S EXCITING CHASF..</p>
        <p>At St. Helena on Monday, James Tick le, of Cow ley Hill lane, and Patrick APCormick, of Fenton•atreet, were charged with gambling on Sunday afternoon on land at the rear of Fonton-atteet.</p>
        <p>Constable Cook raid that he and another constable chased a gang of over twenty young rnen,whom they had been watching for some time. Bowdon went through a house and saw six or eeven of the youthe rush into the yard and bolt the door. They were very much sur- prised when they saw the constable was in the house, and scattered again. Tickle tried hard to get away. He lashed tbror gh the stater of the Windle Brook wnich runs through the district. The constable crowed and recrossed the brook eight times before he captured Tickle. Before they got the two men not of the riot they were eurrounded by • hostile crowd, who tried to get the prisoners to turn on the police. Cook amid that he and Tickle were likenlair of mudlarks when they bad finished their hunt up and done the brook. . . .</p>
        <p>—ihe'Cliief Constable said the conduct of these men constituted n noisence to the district. Defendants svm-e fined 101. and costs, or fourteen days.</p>
        <p>NATIONAL UNION OF LABOUR. SOCIAL FUNCTIoN AT ST. HELENS. . . .</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Glover, esLebour M.P., presided at the fifth antral whist drive and tall of the St. Helens branches of the National Amalgamated Union of Labour at the Town Hall on Setradey night. About 140 were present and met excellent arrangements had been made by the officials The St. Helens branch include n GOO slags vvorkers, and are affiliated a ith the local Trades Conoci I. Mr. lrlover, after congratulating the ollicials on the large gathering, said he was aorry to stand before them ri• the ex-Al P., when he ought to have been returned again Nat yearns their member, for there were many tnings coming before Parliament that he could ha•e given assistance in, especially questions affecting the: miners of the country. %Viten they remembered th• griat calamities that bail bvertelion the miners the need for more inspectors and the need for an amendment of the Alines Acts became apparent, and should prove to the working men that they ought to h•ve MOT(' represented Vea 10 the I louse of Commune who thoroughly underatood the requirements of the miners of the country. fie was glad to see Ow the Government was going forward resolutely with the Veto Bill, end he WAR proud to belong ton party which ass going to do all it possibly could to get the Veto Bill parsed, but their present member would do all he pomibly could to prevent it becoming lave. Mr Bell, general secretary of the N.A.U.L., in • brief address, said he hoped the words of encourage• meat and advice which had been given by the chair- man as to the need for organisatiun would be taken to beam, and that their organisation would go on rind pro•per even more than in the pest. This wrote time of very greet mie in the industrial world, and the worker. seemed to he waking up to the posaibilities of their position, and if they realised their prospects great I mprov.ment would be brought about in the condition 01 the workers. ...</p>
        <p>PERIL OF MORMONISM. ST. FIEI,ENS VICAR'S DENUNCIATION. _ . . .</p>
        <p>All Saints' Church, Shen-Id, was crowded on Sunday evening, when the Rev. A. Maine., a former curate of the parish, nnd now vicar of St. Helena, preached on the perils of Mormonism. Mr. Baths said that until recently Mormoniera could be treated with [unused contempt and incredulity,laot the impudence with which this amazing imposture wet seeking to foist itselt upon thin country, helped along with en astonishing amonnt of hluff, made it a matter of serious concern. After alluding to the havoc wrought in certain families that had been brought to his notice, and showing that the cult originated in the dreamy of an epileptic, the preacher remarked that while not prepared to any that the Mon von Church could not lire on without polygamy, he wan prepared, to any that polygsmy wen an integral pert of the Mormon tenets. That wAn elasolutely indisputable and soy 116.44/t101) to the contrary he would characterise as an utter untruth. One of their petri•rchs, Brighton Young, left neventeen •• widow," and lifty-sis children. The argu- ment that this practice wan paralleled by Old Testa- ment Inc denta was "a palls/tide lie." In any Ca., it scllll an abnordity to compare nineteenth century doings with ttone of four of fire thousand years ago. He challenged the contention that even in the Old Testament there could be found any sanction to poly- gamy. The ()Id Testament we. a faithful record of the vices as well an the virtuea of the men of it, time, and its record could not he construed into a commend or • sanction of those vices. At hottkan Mormonism won nothing more than a mere nnoney-making enterprise. Milliorin Honed into its coffers front its converts, nod : with its aid the political aims of the Murm IP, were nolvaneed. It was a lust for power and a lust for money. It was a cult that was inducing the wives and daughters of England, and the danger called for the most drastic measures of rep re . sion. No nation could allow Cu alien power to nap Its pLysical and moral life, and there should go forth an effective protest. no ,</p>
