        <p>HOW THEY SOLD A HORSE AND FLOAT. A rernerkable store was related to the Borough Bench on Tuesday (Councillor 11. Roberta presiding), when two young boys, Richard Fotheringhem, 114, Winwick-street, and Peter Collks, Union-street. were both charged with stealing s pony. trap and harries@ value Lb, the property of • farmer named Ralph Lawton, mending at Sandy.] roe, Orford. Mr. 'l'. S. Steel am-oilfired for Potherinehern. The Chief Constable stated that at about 12.30 on Wedeersday, February 22nd, Mr. Leston left hie horse nod outside a Mr. Barlow's chop in Winwick-etreet while he went into Bewsey-street. He left n hey in charge of the cart, end when he returned the her nod the cart were gone. lie reported the matter to the police and the next Mr. Lam.on heard of the horse aod cart wax that the two prisoners had taken them to IVidnee on the same day and sold them ton man named Merrill saying thee It was the property of a Mr. Mersey. Merrill asked how much they wanted for It and they said 15s. Thinking the circumstances were rather eumiciotie he told them to come next (lay, and handed thorn a shilling each to buy some food and to pay their fare hick to Werrinetnn. He then informed the Widnes police, who communi- cated with the Warrington force. An the Warrington police bed had the theft reported to them, they looked out for the boys in Warrington. Marnehile th• boys heel returned, end on the following Thursday Pother- ieghem went to a man mimed Locker, who was nrotrehly a little weak in the heed, and mid, "I beve a job for you.' Will you come to Widnes eith rne to take some money horn Mr. Massey fort pony he bee sold in Widnes ?" Locker went with Fotherinehato and said to Merrill, " You can give the nirmiey to Fotherinehani. It will he all right." Merrill, how- ever, was not satisfied, and communicated with the Police, and the boy was arrested and taken to %X./miring- ton by Detective Woolacutt. At the Wm rington police Station Fr therinehana was chimed, and in the mean- time Collins lied also been errested.—Fotherinpliarn In reply to the charge sail." We both neat to Whines and soil the pony and eart for 155." Collin. replied " We both went together." IWO Lawton. the owner, corrohoreted the Chief Constable's statement regarding the circumatences wider which the pony and cart were stolen. Edward Nlerril I, 7, Deninn-street. Widnes, the carter .1 to whom the boys sold the cart, rive evidence to the effct that he recollected the boys coming in him on the ' day in question between flee and six o'clock in thel evening. Potherinchare told him that they had been sent by Mr. Massey. They asked 151. for the cart and pony. Witness being a little suspicious aid not hiry it, and told them to call again, but the boys mid they were bard up, so he gave theme shilling eneh to buy rime food and its back to Werrington. Witnees novindietely reported the matter to the police, aud when Fotheriugenrn celled Remit) he was arrested. Collins, who pimpled not goilty, expinined to the Bench that be thought that there woold fre no berm in going fora ride, but Potheringham took him straight on to Widnes He told him Collins) that he had to sell the pony for Mamey. Mr. Steel, for Fotheringbeni, mired that the boy should he put under the supervision cf the Police Court Missionary instead of being sent roan Industrial School or a Reformatory. HerAlr. Steel) cm not very much impressed with them inntiturirms. It wee stetted by the Thief Constable that Fothering. hem had been before the Court before. on October 191.11 of IWO year, on a charge of larceny, when he was ordered to receive six strokes with the birch. "He is altogether incorrigible," Mr. Nicholls added, "he has many times been found sleeping out. Neither the parents or the Education Authnritiee have much control over him whatever. It seem. likely that this boy will become a nuisance to the police and the community ger-rerslly unless he is teken in band." . . . . s</p>
        <p>With regard to Coition, it erne stoned 'lnd he alvo had been before the Court on a chnree of larceny. " Hie bon.. stirroundi op," vtated the Chief C..nstanle,"tvere not (It for hint, and he bad evidently never had a chnnco in life."</p>
        <p>Both hoy 4 Isere reminded for • week for the purpose of eroding them to a reformatory school.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Borough Police Court</head>
        <p>Borough Police Court</p>
        <p>S-ATI itProrl.:Mr. J. W. Hayward (in the chain. Alderman Tionion. Councillor Locker, Messrs. I]. 'l'. kaitclough, A. 3..1011ey and T. Pemberton. A NIKAN TtieeY.—An elderly man na,t,ed Thomas Mather pleaded guilty to atealing £l, the money of James Houghton, Pl 3, Tbelwail-lane.—The Chief Constable awl that Mather ens the tombs of the prosecutor. (In the night of February 10th Mn. Houghton mule out an order for groceries and wrapped the Al in the paper. The following morning at 0:10 the sovereixo was miming, and the prisoner had din- appeared. Houghton had been giving shelter to the prisoner for several mouths, and he was allowed to sleep on the sofa. When arrested and taxed with the thett prisoner stoutly denied any knowledge of the I money. Ile was taken to the Police Station and subse- quently admitted that he bad taken the money. , Prisoner.—l do not know what caused me to take' t I throw myself on the mercy of the Court. Mather I it s sant to gaol for one month', hard labour. ' " . • . .</p>
        <p>Inc Lost-Pr:I.E.—A girl, Minnie Broughton, aged 14, see cborged with etealing s purse containing the property of Ellen Flenaghan, 53, Birch/ill-Jarmo , and her mother, Elizabeth Brpughton, of lb. St. Peter'. place, appeared in the duck' for having received the money, knowing it to have been stolen.—lt was stated by the Chief Constalile that at ten o'clock on ',Helot, Fobrriary 10th, Mrs. Flonaghen went to the Albron Hotel and there bed in her ptineseion a puree contrein. ing Vll and a shilling. She tendered a novereign rite bar end re 115.cei•ed . ltd. change. Wrien eh was leaving the hotel she felt the purse quite safe i pocket. She than scent too shop to make mingle chem., and the parse wee missing. She repot' matter to the police. Nothing wee beard of ' until a few da3 a ego,o hen Mrs. Flanaglien %omi/ that the girl Lad found the puree, which she borne. Airs. Honest.nn went to see 31re. oho atoutls denied that her doughier half purse. She tile° saw the girl, who made statement. Subsequently Mrs. Flanaghan the prisoner's •inter that there seas no don% that the girl had found the purse, and thal had been spent. She then wean to tetthe told her what she bad learned. —The girl fotind the iiiirke °welds the Albion Hotel. her foot on it until there wits nobody lookin, she picked up the purse, which she took 131 Clan against the girl was dismissed. and the t lad 40s. including cost., era month, lin the lilliame,</p>
