        <p>Ch</p>
        <p>Phao]</p>
        <p>VICAR OF ST. BARNABAS', WARRINGTON, St. Flarnahme Church afford. as striking example of mccembil work upon quiet, persevering and definite Church line.. The parish has a workingclam popula- tion of over 7,000 and the church— originally an off- shoot of the neighbouring church of St. Paul—has gathered to itself • steadily increasing and regular congregation, which appreciates its ministrations and supporta it loyally and well. During the eleven years that the Rev. J. H. G. Hullingworth has bad charge of the parish, gratifying progress ban been made in every direction and the outto ,k to-day its full of hope and encouragement. Mr. Hollingworth, who is the only son of the late Mv samuel Hr.llingsrrth, was born in Isoncoster in the year 1870. Having received his early educa- tion at Hall Gate school in that town, he spent three years in teaching in private schools at Whitby, in pretaration for future Church work. Proceeding to Durham University in 1890, be reumined there until 1893, when he took his BA. degree. He became • Licentiate in Theology in 1594 and M.A. in 1891',. While at Durham Mr. llollingworth used to visit the pit village of Kimfilemworth where he preached and worked with much acceptance amongst tbe mining ' community. In 1895 lie was ordained deacon by the Bishop of Manchester and appointed to the curacy of St. Mary's, W•rdlewoitti, Rochdale, ahem be served under too successive vicar., namely, the late Rev. R. Napier Sharpe and the Rev. Canon kempthorne, ettliee• ,piewly Rector of Liverpool and now Bishop of Hull. During his stay at Rochdale Mr. Hollingworth hail charge of the Howard-at.eet Mission, and also to ,k a keen interest in the day and Sunday schools. After leaving Wardleworth became curate of St. Paul's, Tranwiere, where he remained for eighteen months, and in 1899 he wan appointed curate of St. Paul'e, Warrington, the Vicar at that time being the Rev. E. R. Tarbuck, now of St. George's, Altrincham. Mr. Hollingworth's special sphere of work ism All Saint's Mistime. Hoyle-street, Bewsey. and his devoted offorts for the advancement of Church life in that populous neighbourhood met with a warm response front the people. Towards the end of 1899 the living of St. It irnabas' became vacant and the Rev. E. R. Tarbect, who an. the patron, had no hesitation in offer- ing it to Mr. Bollingworth. That the choice was a good one has been proved by subsequent evente, for St. Barnalies' Church and its activiri•s have gone forward tumid, and uninterruptedly. Mr. Hollingworth is a staunch High Churclimon, with es firm belief in the value of simple, definite doctrinal teaching. and he haa consistently sought to instruct his congregation no that they may be in a osition to give "reasons fir the faith that is in them." One of . the 111,11 t satisfactory features of the wort at St. , Barnabas' is the great increase in the number of , communicants and the remarkable advance in the observance of the great festisala of the Church. TIM Sunday school. which is a model r f what such institutiona should be, has over NO children on its registers arid there is not only a uniform lemon but a regular system of , catechising. The children's guild ileitis a powerful , influence in training the young to be regular church- goer... each member being the possessor of an album in which is placed a stainp,benring a suitable symbolic design, to mark each attendance on Sunday.. and • Holy Days. Many much-needed improvements have been made in the interior of the church, including the provieion of • new west window, a handsome oak , lectern and—most recently of all—a credence table, I feldstool and saticruary chairs. A echeme is wow ' fleeting fruition, f r the introduction of an organ. the cost 'act having already been let. and the inglUrnelit is expected to be in OW very shortly.</p>
        <p>A debt on the schools has been cleared off and the finances of the church ate in a thoroughly sound condition. in connection with the young men's Bible class, which has a large member- ship, there is a club room where recreation is engaged in during the winter months. Every department of the church gives evidence of zealous work and Mr. Hollingworlh, who was faced with an uphill task when he came to the parish, is to be congratulated upon the happy conditions which now omen] and upon having enlisted the help of an earnest and enthusiastic band of voluntary lay workers.</p>
        <p>co--1k it% , •</p>
        <p>'Park;nson. REV. J. O.G. HOLLINGWORTH.</p>
