        <head>Preferment for a Former Warrington Clergyman</head>
        <p>Preferment for a Former Warrington Clergyman</p>
        <p>The Parish Church Magazine says i—lt is a great pleasure to on, to record the appointment of the Rey. P. W. Cook ta of. urate at the Pariah Church) to one of the leading churches in Hastings. Many remember his valuable work aniongst us, Ids simple loving ways combined with the exhibition evident to all of great ability, n truly wonderful combination of gentleness sod strength. Petition some will remember his friend, an assistant curate at Leeds, an he then were the present Archbishop of York coming one Sunday to address his Bible class. It was a notable occesion, though we did not realise tries the honour conferred ups A Hastings magazine, giving an account of Mr. Cook's career 1111)b title as deacon was to the Perish Church of Warrington, where for nearly four years be had the eacellent experienoe of nothing on. curate Inn pop:dous industrial illatrict. Ile had charge of a large Moon Hibl• Clam and Communicants' Guild, gaining their counts and regard by playing in their fuoturill team and leading them under all circumstances to pier the gamy. Mr. Cook's institution as 'Vicar took place oaths 2.ltit February.</p>
        <p>Lenten Services at the Parish Church. - The Lenten observance, at the Warrington Parish Church commenced on Wednesday (Ash Wednesday), when there were celebration, of the Holy Cumnsunion at 6 and 7 s.m. ; aervice for the day schools at 9 : Matins, Litany and Comminntion Service at II ; and evensong at p.m. The preacher at the latter service mos the Rev. Canon Stevenson who pave an impressive discourse on the Parable of the Prodigsl sun. Referring to the special services which have been erraoged for Lent, the " Parish Church Megsslne " rays We earnestly hope tbst it will IPA observed with that loving obedience which the Church, our apiritual Mother, requires of her children. We shall look with great expect:Gen to an increase in our numbers at the weekeley celebrations. ‘Ve would urge those, too, who come occasionally on Sunda., to begin to come regnlarly rind to think not only of their own souls but of the souls of others. for the Blessed Sacrament is not only the Hely Communion of the Body and Blood of Cbriat, but the Holy Eucharist in which we plead before God—the Sacrifice of Christ for the Sins of the whole world. And in Lent we should endeavour to make R step forward in knowledge ea welles grace. Lent is indeed an important time for making a great protest against the worldliness of the •ge, for growing in grace nod for endeavouring to walk humbly with our God. We ere thankful to seoy that we have secured the mervice, of the Rev. Euatace Hill, One of the Mitfleld Clergy belongiog to the Community of the Resurrection,for Holy Week and Easter.</p>
