        <p>All necessary preparations for the choir organ are provided for, so that no structural elterstions will lex reunited to complete the inxtrnment. The key board will be placed in position •nd the building frame con- structed to receive the enund boards, extra size of bellow* reunited. end all channels and arrangements for the action and cotiplers. St. Pau! s Church Annual Concert and Soval Gathering.</p>
        <p>The annoy! tea party and social evening, in connec- tion with Bt. Panl• Chores, in aliveye an interesting fentnrn of the year'. work in the perish, for it gives the parishionms at least one opportunity of social inter- course during Did twelve month. and on Turnrisy even- ing when the gethering wan held there wee a crowded schoolroom. An excellent concert had been arranged which was much appreciated hy the audience. A fenture of tho programme vre• the fine rendering of eeverel &amp;emetic recital. hy Mr. Chaslee Donaldson, 'Dr. Law). Perhaps the most inn reesive wan his inter- ptention of the Trial Scene in 't The merchant of Venice" and by hi• rendering of it Dr. law showed himself to he no mean esponent of Shakeeponro's genius. Ile also gave the quarrel scene from" The school for .010(1.1." and concluded with an impressive recitation entitled "The monkey's paw." Another intermsing fmtitre of the programme wm the singing of the Warrington Mn!. Voice Queerest&amp; con- elating of Mee... Cook, Lloyd, Geldard and Aukland. they contributing in clever manner • rendering of " Tom,the piper's eon." Miss Connie Wilkinson eerie in a tasteful manner " The swallow.," whilst in his violin nob o " Polonaim In Concert "(Weinawskii Mr. Butcher showed that he wait a careful and clover violinist. " The yeomen's wedding song " was sung in an admirable manner ha the Rev. J. 11. Bann, whilet the male members of the choir masons entitled "The Sergeant, of the line" mug,d great amusement. To an interval of the proceedings the Chairman (Mr. J. IL Smethurst , who presided in the absence of the sitar, made a few remarks. He was very sorry, he mid, that the vicar was riot there, brit he (Mr. Smethurst) horrid the when hereto.. in the ceorm of a few weeks they would find him in the pink of condition. lie could bonmtly any, he continued. that the pariah wee in a very tiouriehing condition indeed. The peoplede warden Nlr. John Rigby) had placed the figures in hie hand, aml he understood that at Earlier they wools! be .41 the right side tionncially. There were, however, one or two funds on the financial eide of the church that might be taken more notice of. First of all there was the Clergy Fund. They were not recei•ing any amount from any outside society what- ever, to help this fund. Then were hoping, however, in the near fame to obtain a gnu! Iron one of the large societies. Ife firmly believed that before long they would be receiving a good grant from • certain eociety, for which papers had already Men tient tin. with • high recornmonststion in the firm of • letter from the Biehop of Liverpool. (Hem, beer, The stipend of the clergy depended almost entirely on voluntery effort. If anyone wee willing to contribute to the fund, he, in hie capacity as Treasurer, would be pleased to receive sub. iptions. Mr. Smethurst alluded to the reredos and pointed ant tint there.... &amp;title matter of £lO owing on the fond, and he would like to see It cleared off. Since the Eduntion Act came Into force they were all dultitlese aware tilt Increased reeponsibilty had been placed on the managers of the schools in order tocomply with the requirements of the Board of Education. During the past summer they Lad had to spend over £l6O in alterations. They ind either to do that or close the schools,. that they were compelled to make the altentions without having the necessary amount of money to do it with. As a consequi-nre they had had to borrow. In connection with that matter the Church of England 31en's Society had done noble work in collecting the money. Mr. Smethstret created much interest by his announcement that 41.040 Coin. had been received disrior the past year, including four sovereigns., nine half-sovereigns, 88 holf•crowne, 08 two-shilling pieces and no on, 5,663 then ?penny pieces. while the "coppersmith" was res- , ponsihle for 24,377 pennies end for 7.458 half-pennies. , Mr. Smothoret ni., eulogised the work of the clergy during the Feet :dear. • Amongst those present during the everting were the Revs. E. Peters and J. Id bankes and Alderman and ! Mrs. Smethurst.</p>
