      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>Rev. W. Bracecamp's Tour.</head>
        <p>Rev. W. Bracecamp's Tour.</p>
        <p>The current 4204 of St. Paial's Parieh Nfagatine contains the following letter from the Vim' of St. Paul's 'the It., W. Itracecamp) Luxor, tel meaty 14th. 1911. My deer friende,—By the nun OWit month's imne of our Parish Jlngstine in in your hand., we Mall he thinking etriously of preparing to return home again having had a trip of life-time. We left London on Jentiary 11th. and all went welt mail we arrived at Doter, and then we minted th•t Ire were going to hero • rough 'orig. euro.. to Calais. The an was very rough indeed and every person on board was ill. On arrival at Celan we bad aver, comfort- •ltle emit reserved for us in aver, fact cowriee train which brought es to Pane at 13.45 r.m. We etayed the night at the Wand Hotel. The next rocreing we left Paris at 90. ant travelled the whole day in the Cote d'Aziar Exprefor, which brought us to Marseille. at '7.25 p.n.. !fere we stayed the night and the next morning lee left in the es. Persia, and I ern sorry to my I nave nothing very satiffectory to my about our trip to Port Said which took Is four day., except that the sea WWI very rough the whole way, the ship tossed and rolled, tbe natural result. followed. Mrs. Ilracectitcp vowed if the was 'pared to go back to</p>
        <p>Warrington she would never gu to sea &amp;tailn. On arriving at Port Said them was a general buetie to get our luggage exanfibed h.n. Alum some delay we got into the train for Cairo, •uu after • very pleawfut journey we arrived at Cairo at 10.55 p.m. hence. had nor first real Introduction to Egyptinn life. There was such a bustle among the porters, all trying to get our luggage for on, jabbering away in a language which none of us understood. However, we got away eventu• ally quiti all right and arrived at our hotel, the Geeireb Palace, obe of Pin moat beautiful hotels we have ever . _ .</p>
        <p>teen. He ts• stayed live days, visiting the places of interest in the town and neighbourhood. which included of course, the celebrated Pyramids, the Sphint, rho. During our stay at Clltino we ware pleased to have a chat with two Warringtonians, who are at present : living in Cairo. heaving Caird oft 35 my 24th we embarked on hoard the steamship Ramerren for our trip up the Nile. It would he impriasilde to describe to you what ft trip on the Nile really Means. We felt very much like tha Queen of Sheln felt when she visit. King Solomon—ten had heard something is otir own land shoot the river and its bentity," hat not half had been told us." Its beetity and the pleasure connected with it far exceeded anything we have ever heard or evaded. The steamship Home.a is. floating lintel, she carried 79 pantieniterri, all more or lens sociable, and agreeable. We Imo servile on hoard on the Suoilny, anil of crier., we couldn't possibly du without the Iflolai " CollaajOa:. ao our offertory amounted to Lll Inn, which wins handed over to the hompital et Luxor. Ike Nile is 11,400 miles long, bitt we have only been an far an the first vararart, a distance of about 600 miles. Here we saw the largest dam In the wild, hnonnes the Ass.. Darn, which coat flwr million roods to build in the feat instance ; now they are raising it soother it feet. It is a mile and . iituirter is length. We are now renting a day or two at Luxor liively npot on the Nile, which had ooly a few yenr• ago a fee mud hut. ; now there are POPO people and several beautiful hotels. I wish yort could all 11. the one we are staving in, it is an magnificent. I ant writ- ing this in its lovely grounilt, turrounded by all hind. of beautiful trees and flowers in full bl.m. Donkey rides are featuree of our trip, and we have rnentiged them very well. On one particular day my donkey fell and threw to. over its heard I was told by the donkey lioy it didn't matter. a God srrang.l every- thing. We leave Luxor on Wednesday. February 1501, in the aterim•hip knmettea the Great, for f 'mini, then we go to Port Said and from thence to Paleatina We hope to arrive in Wtrrington alter the 12th of March. I hope to he preeent at church iin Hospital , Sunday, Much 1910, when 1 hoca there will he good congregatious and a record c:illection. It han been a great comfort to me to Ituow that everything has gone on so well at home. My colleague., I know, liaru done all in their powerand I feel mitre they hare linen backed up by a united congregntion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
