        <p>The normal congregations] tea and concert of the : &amp;NM. !Mission was held on Monday evening last, in the schoolroom, and proved to be one of the most socceseful of recent years. About SO friends set down to n suhabrotial boa, presided over by Mrs II Well and • number of the Mission India. After tea the numbers were considerably augmented end a grand entertain. went was given by the following artistes, who acquitted thenteelves edrnirably Mrs J Misr May Isherwood, Mr W Lowe, Mr .1 Senior. Mr J Coswell, and the members of the choir. the accom- penists were Miss Panne Senior, Miss Lowe, and Mr Rog Wallington. MrJW Brown was • very energetic Seroetars. end the proceedings were presided over by the Rev Harry Well.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>Railway Mlssloe.</head>
        <p>The Railway Mlssloe.</p>
        <p>In •idl of the above Mirsiun t grand concert ute held in the Railway Institute linen Duey on Saturday last. Many thanks aro dine to the °theists of tbe L. &amp; NW. iteilteey who kindly lent the Inetituta for the occaaion. The following gave their services freely : Mine Floutrom, Mir Cott:tenth Mr. W. Ifornby, Mr. E. lien•on, Mr. Cooper, Mr. Farrar, Mr. Fulen, and Mr. G. lieunett who was nt hie beet a ith him line humor°. wings. During • abort interval the chairmen Mr. W. It. smith, C.L.C. gave a brief address. He praised the Vail a• Mission tonvalescent Horne at Southport, a• a beautiful pleas, where any railway one who required! rut and change after aicknose or injury nook! go. Ile referred also to the good Christian work dune by the Lady Mianionnr, Mi. Boutroni, who by her kindness and love was winning trio ...put and esteem of the railway men of Werringtr n. Ile appealed to all present to give her all the aid they could lathe good work ahe has undert•ku. A ulendtd entertainment was aiventoally brought Ito edam, by Miss lirrentrroll who exprened the thanks for the good mid:endues and hoped that they bed all enjoyed themselves. Bold-street Wesleyan Men's Bible Class.</p>
        <p>The weekly meeting of the litildwtreet Men's Bible Clean continues to ppoper as well it might front the quality of the addeesses that are delivered, Sunday after gunday. lost Sunday the speaker was Mr. I Mvid James who dealt with the xiii.ject F'dith and its Psycholegy " in a lucid and eltament . manner. Mr. J. C. HolMod, on usual, presided. The lecturer commenced by explaining the term and science of Psychology. It was, DO maid, the positive science of the mind end dealt with the states of con- scions... Faith WAS dealt with then in the general hearing —the faith of the modern world and supersti- toto of the primitive one. Faith in the religion. Renee had a more limited .d dignified meaning, lie defined it as the general expreamon for subjective religion. it W... for conviction as to certain ultimate facts, relating to the order Of the tini and the place nf men in it. And he showed that convic- tion can not the result of purely intellectual judgment, hut had a more vital origin. It Involves an eag•r and loyal choice—a resolution to abide by the hypotheeta that the nature of things wen good and to abide by the side of g00d.... The lecturer gave a 'Mort eynopeis of the history of the complex idea of faith, and turned to examine the primary ground of faith. He avid that every human being had an innate natural tendency to believe. Ife examined the conscioneuese of the child; •ntl clearly Oxoned that the printery ground or faith was feeling. Then authority sus definer! and Omen its pla^e to he in concord with the reasonable testimony of our experience. The authority of the Christi. religion was Christ.Feith ad an art of the will w. explained, and the Nloderniat movement nf the Roman Church, end Sitar:oMo mo•e- ' meat of the German thought and pragmatist of American thought were apoken of in this juncture. Faith and reason were next approached, and the lecturer emphasised that reason did phev an important part in the faith which worka, not as the Nip.° underatands it, but through the consciousness. Bewsey-road Wesley Guild.</p>
        <p>The liessey•road Wesley (laud Feativel wa• held lest week-end and as towel ions attended by a great number nf people. For some seam the Rev. George Ihirbottle a former respected minister of Wrrringtoni her teen the preacher sod speaker, bur thin year a change Wee made and the Rev. W. J. Tunbridge, of Birkenhead. Intl whose former ruperintendeut wee the Rev. E. R. binding, took his place. , Mr. Tl'Nfiall.oa at the morning service dank meinly with the work 1.1 foreign misalons end remarked that the foreign field prepegnndab.d might Ilea lot reepect- ing unity at home an out among the heathen missionaries of all denominations tecogideed that their only change of Christianising the world was by unity in spirit. It was on the foreign geld where •Il minor differences were nest Beide and where they ell netted in the principles upon which they 'rood all agreed. We were not like the people in the Fast. Are nation we were nuts religion. people, except in name. We areas commercial nation, muter. of industry, makers of machinery. If se wanted to get at the top in Foglend we rout have ceeh. Materiel things were tint with us. Josue aaid they were not firs' and tbe Dimino with all his decadence end with all his failing., had never placed the monied men first. In India the prophet bail elves,s been superior to the merchant. It was the men who we• in touch with the uneeen, the visionary, the rn3 stio who sometimes had the euprenic piece and the greatest respect. lie did not mean to Pay thet some of the notions of the bast did not need correctioe, hat if Justin Christ wee to he followed the East had something to tench its and that wee to place float things first. When the Enetero mind concentrated its attention upon the things which to a Westerner were not as clear as they might be. then. good many obecore pasehge• in the Bible would be elucidated. During the 'weeks the choir gave noreditable rendering of " The Lord is my Shepherd." A noisier' servioe 1.11 hold in the afternoon, river which Mr. John Upton di'., presided. An interesting addrees wes delivered hy Mr. F. A. Harris. The Warrington Male kioice Choir renderer: some choice selections, giving (lather evidence of their established reputation es • wellobalanced end highly trained choir. Among the items submitted were Mart:t rs of the •rene" (De }Dile ' nine Hymen before Action " (Walford Davies„ "The Fishermen "(Maldwyn Price,, and" The long day c10g.." iSulliven,. During the •vening service the choir gore,. roirnir- Ode interpretation of" The Mcsseogers," reflecting great credit upon Mr. W. llipkiss, the choirmaster. At the conclusion of the ordinary service a Guild devotional meeting leas held, hinny mornhere of the congregation remaining. The quartette " Incline Thin. ear' wee well rendered by Misses F. Mason, .1. Winetenlyy end Messrs W. Fears. end T. Meskell. Following up the usual cum'urn a lecture had been arninged and the Rev. W. J. Tunbridge took as bra sahject The religion of a young man." The Re•. E. B. Keeling presided over • large attendance The</p>
        <p>lecturer mid "l'hea Christianity did not stand or fall by in, and we were not t Judge it.,. It began id fetus Chrim end Co it Mood nod fall he ihia. Cbt mood before the world at the Friend of alone's. Die problem that no. at the bottom of all problem-i was how to I induce bed new to become good. It had been mid that I men who had bad bearts would always have them, but I If that bad been true, why had be come there, and shy did he preach ? Christ was the greatest her,, toe world bad ever seen and He never became a bore as every ether hero did. Through all ages He held hi. own. He satisfied the soul. of Hie disciples to-day as He did •gea I ego. His we. the only character that I Netion• differed and Lad their one peculimitimbut if men were brought from the four corners of He earth and ware shown a picture of God, they arc old all recognize it. 'Applause),</p>
