        <p>St. John's Presbyterian Church At Si. John.n there tore continited end inrreessing evidences of the atioceas which has attended. nod still attends the latimirs and miniatration• of the 1t.,. Wm. Iteirl. Sonde', after Sunday the new cleirch is filled airnoM tin its utmost oapscity. Last Sandny evening there was the lomat largo congregation when 'lr. Reid chose for hi. subject of discourse "The memage of Bose. " ("Oh, Israel return tint° the Lord thy (iod.") After briefly referring tn the minor propheta and their tetiljagt, Mt. Bold •splained what the privet. and domestics life of Hosea had been, and how it harl been his training fur the work of the prophet and for the delivering of ClorVis memage to the nation of Israel, whiche had sunk very deep in ein and idolatrous worship, generation after generation becoming permeated with the spirit of idolatry. While the nation had preerammd in material wealth, the people had entirely •lienated themmolves Born God, and unto Hosea wse given ta striking message to deliver to thern. It wan fur them, said the preacher, to take lip the great troth preen nine,! to them through the t remedy of a man's life, and e nation's life —how the Lord spoke to them to-day regarding the tame kind of thing. Those people whom God had ^hoarts and called tin himself had broken away from his reatnaining grace, and had sinned agninst his love and forgiven... Wa• thin not. true to.daY of some is, God'. paint!.? 'dhoti form alliances with things that cannot possihly be in accord with ins mm.t snd spirit. Choir Festival at Legh-street Church. The animal choir featly.] In connection with Lomb- st reet Primitive Methodist Church was hell on Sunday when two amnions were preached hy the pastor (Hev. W. Cooper . At the mnrnine service the anthem "The Lord is my Shepherd " (Mach/men wsis pleasingly rendered by the choir. The music chosen for the afternoon we. kfatinder'a " From Olivet to Cimivary," which reoeiverl •ffectice tremtment, the soloists b