      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>INVESTITURE OF THE PRINCE OF WALES.</head>
        <p>INVESTITURE OF THE PRINCE OF WALES.</p>
        <p>Our picture gives a general view of the historical C.arnarvon Castle, the scene fixed for the investiture of Prince Edward of Wales. Workmen are now busy preparing for the great ceremonial, which is the pride of all Welshmen and will prove lobe one of the most Interesting events of the Coronation year. The inset shows the famous Queen Eleanor', Gate, -shich is being restored to its original style, all traces of modern repair being removed. It is here that the Prince of Wales will stand and show himself to the crowd gathered below, is accordance with the ancient custom.</p>
        <p>- _it _ le'•-•Verri--t, • •• • 114 ...A [Tr IV? 14*I 4 vookit •wr ' -.tt a , - •• • • • .„ . ; 4 - ) -;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>NEW KING'S COLLEGE HOSPITAL.</head>
        <p>THE NEW KING'S COLLEGE HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>It was in 1904 that the Council of King's College Hospital, supported by the Committee' tif King Edward's Hospital Fund, decided to remove the Hospital, from its present site among the offices of Portugal Street and Lincoln's Inn, now a thinly populated area, to one where it would serve the needs of a million people The foundation stone was laid by King Edward VII. in 1909 on a site of 12 acres on Denmark Hill, given by the Hon. W. F. D. Smith, chair- mai. of the executive and removal committee. When complete there will be accommodation for 600 beds, and it is hoped that the opening will be in view before the close of 1911.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>WARRINGTON GI, j!L jrUS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON GI, j!L jrUS.</p>
        <p>FIFTY YEARS AGO.</p>
        <p>A Chapter of Jubilees.</p>
        <p>It is the agreeable custom of the "Times" and other old-established newspapers to serve up afresh the news that appeared in their columns a hundred years ago. Warrington could boast no newspaper in 1811, but if we decide to go back fifty years there is an abundance of material. A fifty years space is a convenient one, for the events will lie within the memory of not a few of our readers, and there are many others who while they do not know the charm of musty documents find pleasure in history of less ancient flavour.</p>
        <p>For the events of the present chapter we shall not wander far back along the stream of time. We are concerned only with jubilees. What happenings were ruffling the surface of our town life half a century ago? We put first an event which created wide- spread alarm, quoting from the "Guardian" of March 11th, 1861. THE BURSTING OF THE BRIDGEWATER CANAL. GREAT FLOOD AT LATCH FORD.</p>
        <p>"On Monday morning last an accident of a serious and extraordinary character,involv- ing a vast amount of damage to the property of the Bridgwater Trustees and the entire , stoppage of their traffic, occurred about two I miles from Warrington. At Lumbrook, in the township of Appleton, the canal is carried over the high road leading to " Appleton Thorn," by means of an aqueduct, the embankment of which is 25ft. above the level of the adjoining ground. Between eleven and twelve o'clock on the day in question, this embankment suddenly gave way on the south west side causing an immense breach about 50ft. wide down which the water rushed with terrific force, carrying with it immense quantities of sand, earth, and blocks of stone.</p>
        <p>"The ring wall of the bridge on that side was completely swept away, but beyond this the bridge itself sustained no damage. . .</p>
        <p>" InformatiOn of the catastrophe was con- veyed to the office at Stockton Quay by one of the trustees tenants, Mrs. Houghton, who lives near the place, and who was the first to discover what had happened; immediate steps were taken to get up the "stops," or flood gates, but unfortunately those on the east and west sides of the breach proved ineffective, in consequence of which a larger volume of water was allowed to escape than would otherwise have done.</p>
        <p>"The scone which the place presented alter the occurrence was that of a complete wreck ; great blocks of stone, etc., lay about in indescribable confusion ; and a vast , quantity of earth and sand—probably seven thousand feet—had been washed up from the bed of the canal, and carried down into the road, and into the low ground in the neigh- bourhood. The force of the current must have been immense to have enabled it to scoop out of the embankmert for a distance of 100 lineal yards, this vast quantity of material. The water course was filled with sand, and it took a considerable time to re-open it. On Wednesday, about a hundred navvies were act to work to fill up the embankment; and a great number more, as well as masons, have since been put on. The repairs are being vigorously carried forward under the personal superintendence of Mr. Forrester, of Runcorn, the trustee's resident engineer ; but the traffic cannot be expected to be resumed under a month from this time.</p>
        <p>"The cottages in thel.n.;;;;;:te—neighbour- hood were flooded.those on the Grappenhall- road being three feet two inches, and those I on the opposite side of the canal about two feet 6 inches in water—but fortunately their occupants sustained but little damage beyond the inconvenience and trouble caused by the inundation. ' No precise information can be obtained as to the immediate cause of the accident; but it is attributed by the engineer to the late severe frost; and the high winds which have prevailed,causing flats to knock against the side of the canal, may poeiibly have had I something to do with it. A strict inquiry will, of course, be instituted by the Trustees. ' "We understand that on the morning of the accident, and only an hour or two before it occurred the bank rider,or inspector, passed the place, but failed to observe anything wrong. The bridge was considered the most firmly built of any along the canal, and had never been known to have the slightest leak. "Of course no estimate can as yet be formed of the amount of damage, but it is supposed that the repairs will cost some thousands of pounds; besides which the loss of traffic must be taken into considera- tion, which will, of course, be serious. "Great numbers of people from Warrington have visited the scene of the accident.</p>
        <p>"We recollect reading in the Gentleman's Magazine an account of a similar catastrophe which took place about 80 years ago at the same spot, or very near it ; and on that occasion a flat was precipitated into the road below. We are requested to state that the traffic will be worked by the Old Quay Navigation during the necessary repairs to the Bridgwater Canal."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>THE_ PUBLIC HALL.</head>
        <p>THE_ PUBLIC HALL.</p>
        <p>The autumn of 1861 witnessed an event of considerable local interest, the laying of the foundation stone of the Public Hall, now the Royal Court Theatre. The " Guardian " opened its description of the ceremony in the words :—" At length the hopes are realized, and the fears dispelled by those gentlemen who for three years have been labouring to secure the erection of a Public Hall. The foundation stone was laid last Thursday, amid a manifestation of public feeling never excelled on any similar occasion."</p>
        <p>Dr. Joseph Smith presided and the stone was laid by Gilbert Greenall, the Borough member, a bottle containing a collection of local literature being placed under it. . . .</p>
        <p>A year later the 'inhabitants had the delight of seeing the completed building, rmd a few words concerning that event may be appropriately added here.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>OPENIEO OF THE PUBLIC HALL.</head>
        <p>OPENIEO OF THE PUBLIC HALL.</p>
        <p>" On-TUaiiairy-tbis-10-nri-ii;llTi;d..otTfall was opened by the Oratorio of the Messiah in the morning, and a concert of Secular music in the evening. We entered so fully into the description of the hall on the occasion of laying the foundation stone, that we need only now add that it is erected upon a plot " of land long known as the 'Bowling Green,' " and in a new street,made from Bridge-street, nearly opposite the Lion Hotel, to Cairo- street, in a lino with Suez-street and Palmyra Square. The architectural beauties are few owing to the wish of the directors to have numerous rooms for committes, for dressing, cooking, etc. Internally, however, it is all that can be desired. The large hall will seat 800, the gallery 400 and the plat- form or orchestra 150 more. The gallery is remarkably light and elegant, the same</p>
        <p>height as the Free Trade Hall, Manchester, the architect of which. Mr. Walters, was also the architect of the new ball.</p>
        <p>The first speech in the new building was delivered by Colonel Wilson-Patten, who enlarged in graphic language upon the shifts to which they had been put in years past to find a suitable building for a large meeting. He recalled the day when some of the lead- ing lights of the nation had visited the town to speak upon a 'great measure of national education. At least two Cabinet Ministers were present, including Lord Derby, who gave a speech, but the accommodation was so limited that they were obliged to make a trap-door in the platform so that a crowd assembled beneath might hear.</p>
        <p>Yet he asked them not to entertain any feelings of dislike!towards the old Music Hall. He himself had spent many happy hours there. Occasionally, however, they had seen weird and distressing sights. He had, for instance, seen a prima donna making a vain endeavour to take her crinoline upon the platform and sticking woefully among the orchestra because unwilling to leave it behind. He had seen great musical events all but ruined because the conductor had actu shy not had room to wield his baton. The date of the opening was November 11th, and as the Prince of Wales had reached his majority two days before, the Colonel asked the audience to join in the National Anthem. The hall thus formally opened, Mr. Eau&amp; (the individual who had tried in vain to stretch his arms in the old Music Hall) began to lead his choir in a performance of" The Messiah."</p>
        <p>It may he mentioned here that the Mr. Walters who designed the hall was the architect alsoof the District Bank and it was in the cutting of the caps of the pillars of that building that the genius of the late Warrington Wood was first revealed. A GREAT SOCIAL HAPPENING.</p>
        <p>On April 30th,1861,an event occurred which stirred a local journalist to the following flight:</p>
        <p>"Births and marriages are a sort of terrestrial bows of promise in the cloudy regions of actual life. The advent of a "little stranger' is always welcomed in well regulated families, by a chorus embracing every quaver which the inmates can produce. The new comer presents the better side of human nature: shows what like coin the mint of heaven can produce and how great must he the wear, the tear, and the deterioration before it becomes the vixen, the dawdle, the rogue or the bloated drunkard of future years." . . . . .</p>
        <p>But it is not a birth that he is about to herald ; it is a wedding. The marriage of loving hearts and the joining of loving hands 'for better and for worse' is a vow of promise at which every lover of his kind must rejoice. It is a pledge of the perpetuation of the family relation- ship instituted in l'aradise; an assurance that Love asserts her lawful supremacy, however dethroned, and that patriots and 'men in the no‘olest sense will never be want- ing in the earth." After a few paragraphs to explain that there is another advantage in a marriage— that of givin ; opportunity to everybody to show their regard to the bride and bride- groom and giving them something better than the comfort of a costly but cold marble tomb, the scribe comes nearer to his subject. " But to Alderman Peter Rylands has been awarded the no small honour of his native town showing a deep interest in his marriage by hoisting its official flag, lining its narrow streets as the marriage cortege passed, and smiling welcomes and "God bless you-a" wherever the happy couple could see and hear. "It is not the reformer, the man without faults the please-everybody prodigy that was honoured. It was the man who incs spent perhaps a day a week since he left school in the service of his townsmen ; and who amid errors, it may he blunders, has secured for himself a name, and a place by doing their drudgery. Under circumstances such as those of Tuesday last, the enmity of little minds must have slept ; the sneerer's lip must have ceased to curl and the man ever ready to discover had motives for good lactions must have let his better nature have its fling pro tern at any rate. " There are others in Warrington besides Mr. Peter Rylands who, for long years of public and private usefulness, would be equally honoured, and equally well deserve it, should an opportunity occur. It is well that it should be so, The corps of Warrington worthies is not over large. A half-dozen of zealous young Tories and as many reformers would be a blessing. They would keep the town alive. They would emulate each other in supporting our local institutions, and doing that work which rests upon half-a dozen of old stagers who have long since earned the retiring pension. Had we such young men—and we could tell where they are to be found—would our public spirit so often flag ?" The writer waxes so eloquent through a column of introduction that he quite forg ts to tells us the name of the bride.' That there was a bride on the great °erasion! is proved by the description of her dress. It was "of white glace silk, plain skirt with a demi-train, trimmed with rosettes of tulle and clematis, open sleeves, and sash of gimped glace trimmed to correspond. Wreath of orange blossom, jessamire and clemaiis ; tulle veil ; honiton lace collar and sleeves, with handkerchief of the same."</p>
        <p>Perhaps the reporter felt that he had mentioned everything of importance but there will be no harm in our filling up a trifling deficiency by saying that the lady who wore all these things was Carol ine,eldest daughter of William Reynolds, of Latchford House.</p>
        <p>Events have shown that the opening reference "to little strangers" was not badly divined. They appeared in due course and one of them keeps the name alive in our midst. His name is William Peter.</p>
        <p>Two other scraps will exhaust our space.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>LONGEVITY AT ORFORD.</head>
        <p>LONGEVITY AT ORFORD.</p>
        <p>AN ECHO OF THE CENSUS OF 1861.</p>
        <p>Amongst numerous instances of ripe old age that have occurred in the census returns for this district, the following may be noted as indicative of a soil, and an atmosphere, conducive to health and longeity. The united ages of the inmates of one house (three in number) amounted to 223 years. The senior of the three (a lady of 88 years) is frequently in the habit of walking to War- rington and back, a distance of nearly six miles (including of course a short ramble about the town). At a tarm house having only two inmates, their united ages are 168 years and the work of the farm is performed by the venerable pair, the husband ploughing the whole of the land by his own manual force, employing the old fashioned implement called a breast plough. He is a remarkable specimen of ruddy and robust health.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>KING STREET MYSTERY.</head>
        <p>A KING STREET MYSTERY.</p>
        <p>While a boy was passing along King-street he picked up a human hand, in a state of partial decomposition. How it came to be there remains a profound mystery, but it was at the ti.se deemed highly probable that it had been removed from some midden situated at the back of the houses on the East side of King-street, which bad lately been</p>
        <p>By Pklllp &amp;Mu, M.P. - A MUCH NEEDED AMENDMENT.</p>
        <p>I called attention a few weeks ago to a very real hardship which still remains in connection with the Old Age .'ensions Act, after the poor law disqualification has been removed for the majority of cases. I have !had a number of instances brought to my notice where a man or his wife cannot get an Old Age Pension, though entitled to it by age and means, because the other partner is still in receipt of poor relief. The wife is, say fill, and therefore not of age for a pension. The husband is over 70 and entitled to one in all respects 90 far as be himself is con- cerned. But if the wife continues to get poor relief the husband can have no pension, because he is, according to law, in receipt of poor relief by the fact that his wife is, receiving it. I am given to understand that the Government are preparing a Bill to am end the Old Age Pensions Act in some parti- culars where experience has shown that amendment is needed, and I have reason to believe that the flaw I have been pointing' out will have consideration. It certainly ought to be altered, because 58. a week is not! enough to keep a husband and wife when' both are past work. A WISE AFTER-THOUGHT.</p>
        <p>1 ---- - ----- The announcement which was made last week that the cost of paying the pensions of' those who have recently come into them by the removal of the Poor Law disqualification will be met by the State has been received ' with universal satisfaction. It is difficult to understand how Mr. Lloyd George over came to entertain the idea that he could make the Guardians pay back the cost. It was an impractical scheme, and it was not worth doing if it could have been managed. It would have been a cause of endless irritation and would have made an invidious distinction between the two classes of pensioners. It would have given a most undesirable taint of pauperism to the pensions, which should by every effort be avoided. The cost is not very great. The Chancellor says it will cost him about £1,500,000 a year. He can well afford that. His revenue is coming in splendidly, and he should have a very large surplus at the end of March. He was plead- ing poverty in the House of Commons last week ; but this was probably to make the shock all the greater when lie announces how the Treasury is overflowing when he makes his Budget statements in April. This action of his in shouldering the cost of the additional pensions will undoubtedly he a relief to the local poor law bodice. To 1 what extent the rates will be relieved cannot be accurately calculated ; but in some places it is estimated it will amount to a rate of 2d. in the pound. The National Exchequer can better afford to bear this coat than the locality can to go on paying it. There have been a number of debates in Parliament lately about the relations between national and local taxation, and the case of the local authorities for relief finds sympathy in every quarter. For many years back the State has been adding to the duties of the municipal councils compelling them to spend more money, and the assistance given by the State has not been adequate. In the last twenty years, the amount of local rates has risen from £3B millions a year to £74 millions. Education is responsible for a large amount of this, and it is admitted by everybody that education is a national service for which the nation ought to mainly pay. While everybody admits that the State ought to pay for a great many of the things which are financed out of local rates at present, I am afraid that there will not be the same unanimity when it comes to levying the national taxes to pay for these services.l If the demands which are now being put forward for the payment by the Stated the whole cost of Education, Asylums, Poor Relief, Main Roads, etc., were conceded, there would require to be raised probably £25 millions more by national taxation. Now, how is that going to be raised? We had a fight two years ago which nearly upset the Constitution about raising less than half that amount of new taxation. Ido not see how either a Radical or a Unionist Chancellor of the Exchequer is going to deal with this question of a reform of the rating system without such additions to the national revenue as will involve forms of taxation to which neither of the two chief parties in the State are committed to-day. WHAT ABOUT PROMISED REFORMS. This question of the present yield of the revenue is of much interest to the Labour Party because it has much to do with the promised Unemployment and Infirmity Insurance schemes. The Chancellor was reported a few months back to have told al French journalist that these schemes would cost £16,000,000 a year, and that he had the money ready !Last week he said that if he did not take back the half of the land taxes which had been allocated to the local authorities he would not be able to carry out his insur- ance schemes. If less than half a million is going to make the difference between having the schemes this year and not having them, then they are certainly not going to cost £16,000,000 a year, unless, of course, the cost is to be met mainly by the workmen and the employers, a proposal which would be neither fair nor just. My hopes that these oft-promised schemes would mature this I year are getting weaker. The promise to Ideal with the question of local rating has been withdrawn. The Insurance Schemes will probably have to take their luck with Ithe Navy Estimates. If Mr. Lloyd George Can manage to turn a deaf ear to the scare- mtvger then we may get the Insurance Bills, but if millions more are to be voted for the Navy then, I am afraid, as was the case last year, li.oc i a I reform will have to be post- poned.‘, ... . . .</p>
        <p>• • : Some time ago there appeared in the press : lan outli of the Infirmity Insurance scheme: which, was alleged, the Chancellor had approve 4., refrained from commenting upon it the time because it appeared to me to have l, _en evolved out of the brain of an imaginat e writer, and now, in reply to a question the Chancellor says that it is not : his eche e. We must wait until the scheme is offici ly presented, before we offer any criticism, but in the meantime the trade unions and other working class bodies who are sure to be effected by it should be study- ing thc general question, and making up their minds as to the general lines on which they wm.ild like to see the system framed. This is a far more important matter to these organirations than Old Age Pensions were, for any' State scheme of Unemployment benefits or of sickness benefits will materially</p>
      </div>
