        <p>1 The following Vt'arringt cheminte and lending chemißta everywhere .ni.nly I,r ShilTn.tnn a .%,thma Cure (A.thmadf,r) on the g tee plat: of satisfaction or your money refunded, or gent poet paid On thie guarantee, hy Dr. It• dcbiffrosoo, 56, Clerkeowell.road, L n .1. E.C. HILL &amp; CO., 62, Buiterma.ket-eireet, BOOT'S CASH CHEMISTS aod Branches.</p>
        <p>alter the whole working of these departments of the trade unions and friendly societies. WHAT IS DONE ABROAD.</p>
        <p>in the recently published volume of Foreign Labour Statistics there is a very valuable memorandum dealing with the schemes of Insurance against Accident, Sickness, Infirmity and Old Age in foreign countries. This remarkable volume is packed full of the most interestingand useful information about foreign labour conditions, and should be in the possession of every student of labour questions. The price is 28. Id. Insurance against Sickness is compulsory in Germany, Hungary, Luxemburg, and to some extent in France, Norway, and Austria. But there is no instance yet of compulsory State Insurance against Unemployment, although several of the Continental countries have , voluntary systems, some of which are sub- sidised by the State, as in Denmark, or by the municipality as in Belgium and Germany. In Denmark, the State supplements the workmen's contributions to the Unemploy- ment fund by a sum 'equal to about one half and there is a further sum given by the municipalities of about half the amount of ' the State subvention. It appears that about 164 per cent, of the men and about 22 per cent. ,of the women who are eligible to join the unemployment fund are members of it. rho law has been in force only since 1907, so that it will be seen that it has achieved a good deal of success. The average amount paid out last year in unemployment benefit worked out at about 20 shillings per member. There is no compulsion to join the scheme. The Danish scheme seems to have done remark- ably well, which is probably explained by the thrifty nature of the Dane, to whom this plan of providing for a rainy day would naturally appeal. But in our country no scheme will deal with the difficulty unless it can do something for those who have no will to help themselves.</p>
      </div>
