        <p>Mrs. H. Roberts, 2.5, Durham-street, Southern. Portsmouth, writse '' Our little by, Walter, aged *even, fell down in the street whilst ilaying and accrued thy "kin eons ii;11 knuckle'. For is mouth I poulticed and dreamed Water's fingers, hut could not stop the bettering or draw out the poison. I then tried Zatn-fluk, which 1.0011 cleared away all poieonons matter, and in a Belie (Una grew new shin over Walter's fingers, leaving hia hand peke clear. Mre. Annie Bennett, 4, Thoenhill Beidge Place, Caledonian Road, London, N., writes :—" While putting memo wet clothes through the mangle. / sprained sty wrier rather badly. The wrist swelled and hmseme discoloured, and I 1;scl awful pain at the Joint. My hand tom of no nee, and I ewe in constant pain until I vas advised to try Zam•Buk. I first bathed my wrist in hot water, and then applied Zarn- fink and bandaged the wrist up. A little pernever- ante with this treatment took oat all pain sad swelling, and the shin was soon its nevural colour again. I kept on with Zam-Buk until I could use toy write again, and now I do all my waaldag and never bare the t'lighted pain in inconvenience. Yam- Risk ie • grand rain killer."</p>
