        <head>HANGERS OF THE MINK.</head>
        <p>HANGERS OF THE MINK.</p>
        <p>Joseph A. Whittle, a colliery fireman. of 31. Provi- denceintreet, St. lielens, was charged, lwforf, Si, IM•111 Gamble end Mr. J. Harontill, at the County Petty Sesnionn, on Tuesday, with iii•obeying the orders of the manager of the Bold (2colliery by tiring. shot " in the fast," •nd contrary to special rule 117 of the Coal Mines Regulation Art, awl Forth.., with endangering the safety of the minorw—Mr. Peace prosecuted for tile Collin. Wren Colliery Conip•ny, and mid that the danger of this practice hail Leen recognised ff,r many year'. In thin case the shot one put into lbw :coal close up to an roof, nod it wait eery dougerow.aa it might h... liriiiight down the roof, as o all as caused in explosion.—The magistrates, after, hearing the evideoce of the oteiteger, hoed Whittle 40, sad 105, costs.</p>
