      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>OUR SERIAL STORY.</head>
        <p>OUR SERIAL STORY.</p>
        <p>" Von Inuit him?"</p>
        <p>" Through thick and thin." " Captain Chinnery, are you a man to join another eager to make wrong right? To over- throw the machinations of a had woman, and to save • pure girl from • hideous fate?" " I'm on a three months leave, and like nothing better than unravelling intricacies, and joining in adventures," Chin nery re- plied. " ble I look forward to enjoying a lively twelve weeks." "Within the walls of Trafford Khan's mansion there is • treasure of gold, and • priceless treasure of love," murmured Law- son to himself. •• iftel4lll.lg MISS Aide Morant?" slid Chin- very, catching the last words, softly tho Ugh they were spoken. "Yes, I love her. She is my heart's dart- ing, the mistress thnt rules my life, my every thought. We have been separated by that vindictive witch, Miriam, who has in her pen- session the torn half of a plan to the secret vault; the remainder was concealed by me in the thick sole of my left boot. You may have noticed that I limp slightly." "Bat the gold stored- in 'Trafford House is the property of the heiress," remarked Blandford.</p>
        <p>" No, it is the property of Aide, and my- self. A quarrel between the father and daughter, which I overheard, disclosed to me Ihe rascality of the Khan. Having no proof beyond this conversation to go upon I re. mained silent."</p>
        <p>"Some men never know their own mental vigour," thought Blandford, "till they are driven, by stress of circumstances, into posi- tions that require the exercise of great in- genuity to extricate themselves. Lawson and I are both of that class." By the bye, Lawson," said Chinnery, "didn't you tell me that Trafford Khan started life in Teheran GS street water carrier or seller?" "I did."</p>
        <p>"You- -ere not correet," observed Chinnery. "I happen to know all about Trafford Khan. I heard it from a fellow in the Foreign Office, Trafford Khan's father was a needy and unscrupulous adventurer, who had to have England for the benefit of the country of his birth. His wanderings led him to Persia, and whibit loafing in Teheran, he hail the good fortune to win the admiration of • very rich and influential Armenian widow. His marriage was the stepping- stone to his prosperity, and he rapidly rose to power. Ile wan not the kind of man to let Idir the smallest opportunity that would !raise hint in the social scale. By assiduous flattery and loans of money, which he never asked to have refunded, he became the favourite of the reigning Shah, and his in- fluence over that potentate was completed when he cleverly managed to avert • disas- trous war by stealing from St. Petersburg a secret treaty that had paused from the Shah to the Russian Government, in defiance of obligationa entered into between Great Britain and Persia. The dinclosure of this double dealing would have destroyed the labour of our Prime Minister, who had been working indefatigably to gain the Shah an an ally in reality as a buffer to stop the ad- vance of the Rugged Bear of the North. From the moment that the despatch was plaeed in the hands of the Shah, Malcolm Trafford's influence was paramount, and when he jumped the boundary of the earth his son continued to wield the name sway at court as his father had done, and thin con- tinued until the death of the monarch. Con- vinced that he was surrounded by envious enentien. Trafford Khan came to England arid settled here. Ile must have foreseen that at 1.101/1e period he wotild have to take hurried flight Fronk the land of his father's Adoption, for he built Trafford House, and deposited his riches in this country, but none knew where. His death was sudden and un- expected. The water seller legend was a mere fable, invented by those whom he had</p>
        <p>'Chinnery ceased speaking, bet after • moment turned to his friend. "Blandford, show Lawson the contents of that jewel ease," he said. Lawann's languid air was swept sway by • burst of extraordinary emotion as his eyes fell upon the diamond necklace which was displayed in the jewel can</p>
        <p>recognim' the necklace? " mid Bland- ford and Chinnery in the same breath. " l'es, yes," he cried excitedly. "The property of Miss Miriam Trafford, eh?" suggested Chinnery. "Not at all," answered Lawson sharply. " No? " mid Chinnery, raising his eye- brow. in mrprise. "But she has worn that necklace; I've seen it blazing round her throat many a time.' "</p>
        <p>"Never t'o my knowledge," said Lawson, homily taking a stud from his shirt. "Now I come to think of it, you were never present at any of the, small Ainner parties. Where were your' Chinnery asked. "A prisoner in my room. I was for years treated as a mere slave. Such times as we met were by accident." Lawson replied. "Why did you tliviny no much emotion when you saw those diamonds!"</p>
        <p>"Tliat is easily answered," lie replied, taking from a silken bag which was slung around his neck by • narrow white ribbon a miniature portrait of • beautiful woman. "An admirable work of art !" cried Chin- nery, the enthusiastic. collec.tor. of .rareties. . . .</p>
        <p>, is the lady?" askedEilandiordTiOok- ing over Chinnery's shoulder at the minia- ture.</p>
        <p>I do not know. but I believe that it ie • portrait of my mother. I found the portrait in Miriam's bombir. Oaring at the features I felt instinctively that it vote more to me than 1k mere portrait ; mv lip, unbidden, framed the word ' Mother.' it you look at it closely, you will are that the necklace in that r 11.4., is an exact counterpart of thy necklace ,in the portrait. The peculiar cutting and shape of the large centre stone proves it in. ecntestably." Lawaon crafted apeaking, and replaced the miniature in its hiding place. "Why not announce yourself to Mini Tref- , ford?" was the next question asked by Chin. - . .</p>
        <p>eauee I quitted Trafford flow by stealth."</p>
        <p>"On the death of the master?" said Chin- no!y:</p>
        <p>"'And was tracked down by Mies Traf- ford."</p>
        <p>you have no legal claim to the dia- mond., or to any treasure that there may be." objected Chinnerv. "At pretient none. Kut there in the Khan." Chinnery ahrtigged his shoulder., and Blandford arched his brows. Both men believed that Lawson's mind was still some- what unhinged. "Did your guardian, or whatever he was. leave any written documents," questioned Chinnery. ; Lawson shook his bead. "I don't know." "Then abet is your meaning?" demanded Blandford.</p>
        <p>--'7lai..;frafrord Khan shall make resOu. lion. and adinowl^dge his villainy," replied Lawson, aeriously. But he is d,a,1," said Blandford.</p>
        <p>"I went to the funeral," asserted Chin- acre. "Death and funeral were alike a trek of Miriam's. I firmly believe that her father is confined in hie 0.11 house, and intd that house I must discover • way. Gentlemen, can I rely on your co-operattont" You may," mid the two friends. The clockwork in the interior of my head is rather runty for want of use. Cheek me if Igo wrong. But first lock the diamond necklace in the wardrobe or anywhere that to ,cure." Blandford immediately complied with his rennent. and on re.entering the room Lailoson said: "You, Captain Chinnery, might 'call at Trafford Home and ascertain whs4lter Shss Trafford is in reaidence there. If 00i %II t 00,,, I propose to force a window I ow • . .</p>
        <p>-- of, and thoroughly aearch the place.' Blandford and Chinnery were awe at the eudeeity of the proposition.</p>
        <p>"A dangerous proceeding," suggest,. Chinnery. doubtfolly. "We need the renowned Niggion for that kind of work," laughed Blandford. Ltasiin frowned, saying reproachfuny, "I did not expect thin of you.' At thin Blandford laughed °Wright. "Oh, its all right," he said. "If there s adven- ture mt foot, I'm your man. I'm not going to fly the white feather, eh, Chinnery 1" "Not very likely," Chiunery replied. "But Lawson a question." "A thousand," said Lawson, heartily. "Why did that rascally chauffeur purloin your banal" "I imagine that he might prevent identili• ration of my boots. My name and that of the maker was impressed on the tabs, in addition to a klaltene cross innide. Davis, the chauffeur, could not ponnibly geena that the torn half of the plan which gives the key to the labyrinths leading to the Temple of the Golden Sun wan a ,reted in one of them."</p>
        <p>Chinnery looked at his wateh. "Look here,- he said, "I shalt haws to go. I hit,e an appointment. Exemm iny *nay. but I cannot keep a lady waiting." When 'hull we nee you again?' asked !Amnon, uneie.ilv. "Will you both dine with me thin even• inE?"</p>
        <p>Where?" asked Blandford. At the Sappho," said Chinnery. "Eight o'clock, if that will suit you." "Captain Chinnery, there shall be no ron• cealment. After dinner I'll relate how I became possessed of the torn paper which Miss Trafford is no anxious to regain. To possess that paper she would not hesitate to hurry me into eternity." "You art whetting my appetite, tell me abuts it now," said Chinnery, forgett‘ag for • mrment the lady of whom he had me kr. Not to-night you shall listen to, perhaps, the strangest story you have ever heard. Good bye. Eight o'clock at the Sappho!" Captain Chinnery departed after ex- changing a warm handshake. Lawson and Blandford were very quiet, but now and then they looked askance at each other. The former .as pale and agitated, while the latter was in a state of mental distress. Their positions had all in a moment been reversed, and Blandford had uncomfortable thoughts of being once more cast upon the world again. "My task is ended," he said nervously. "Onlv beginning you mean," said Lawson decidedly. "Shall I draw a cheque for the balance at the London and Westminster? 'Tie yours." "Not mine—a free gift. Blandford. There in to my credit at the Bank of England the slim of twenty thousand pounds. After you have learned how I came by it. I will humbly beg you to accept an equal part." "Lawson, it's a splendid offer," Blandford exclaimed. "Why should you think I might refuse it?" "Because," answered Lawson, "I took the notes from the clenched fingers of • man you all believe to be dead."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>tALL</head>
