      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>CHAPTER VII.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER VII.</p>
        <p>?NI IDINTITY OF Till UNKNOWN.</p>
        <p>'Lawson advanced, sobbing, his head bowed, and utteily unmanned, and overcome by a revulsion of feeling, while Blandford and Chinnery gazed out of the window at the blue, fleecy clouds floating northwardi, their own eyes filmy, for Lawson'a emotional breakdown was catching. Biandford," murmured Lawson, his voice quivering. Blandford turned round, while Captain Chinnery remained loaning against the embrasure of the window.</p>
        <p>"My friend!" Blandford's hand fell lightly on the striken man's shoulder. oon't think me an ass, old chap. It iteetred to me as 'f a wire of steel snapped in my brain and then memory rushed in as w tier ekes into a dam when the gates are opened. Don't speak, let me think •-think— of your name?" Ile crossed the Hoer in a sivift stridt and gazed fixedly into Chinnery's good-Itching face, conning every feature, his tingers wandered through his hair, • painful heeitation ensuing. Then he cried aloud: "I cannot. I cannot. Oh, my God, has twat, ry eluded me again? I know my name is Michael Lawson," pronouncing the names slowly, and with difficulty. "From where did I come?" closing his eyes and corn. fleeing inwardly. "From where don't ..peak --you were there, • guest—l was jealous—of whom—don't—have met—where --where--where?" Ilia voice ascending in a issionate crescendo. "My broken, die- jointed memory is failing again, I feel the cord tightening, here, here," tapping his forehead impatiently. " The cord will snap again, and then—what then will happen?" Suddenly a flesh of vivid intelligence lit up his fare, his nostrils nnd lipn Inivered, and with a piercing shout of joy, cried: "I have It. you are Captain Archer Chinnery, and I nm Michael Lawson, no more unknown, no longer imbecile. I sin no longer like a raft at sea, drifting among undercurrenta, jagged rocks, and whirlpools, but a man, a man pre- pared to fight a man's battles. Trafford Khan is dead. But Miriam, the witch? What of her? I don't know. I don't know, but I will find out and the revelation will be ter- rible."</p>
        <p>ilia mind was still soinewhat distraught, though straggling to regain the complete thread of what had taken place immediately before Dlandford had succoured him in the old houw, near the Seven Dials. He looked wildly about, and grasped the two men by their arm,</p>
        <p>" This is not my room. Where am Tr " This .18 your room, Lawson," said Bland. ford. firmly. " You are living in Jermyn- street."</p>
        <p>Jermvn - street," he repeated. Of course. how absurd. You brought me hero after we walked through the ruined wall of the cellar into the water." Then he shivered, looking round apprehensively, whispering: "The rats! Recollection is coming back gradually. We have lived in these chambers quite a long time. A year or more. Am I correct?" he asked doubtingly. " Yen. answered answered Blandford .‘ _ _</p>
        <p>"Ani.l your guest?" he asked. "On the contrary I am yours," Blandford replied. Mn dear friend, explain yourseL" "When You thonght that you were dying you gave Me notes to the value of three thou- sand, five hundred pounds. On this we have lived. I had nothing, and I had promised not to desert you. I have therefore dispensed part of that money for you. I shall submit a statement of expenditure to you," he added. " Nonsense," Lawson cried. "You will do nothing of the kind. lam only conscious that but for you I should have been swindled out of every farthing of it." "But I have kept a complete account of every peony, said Blandford. "Then,' said Lawson, "throw cCounts to the dogs. I am not Dives casting crumbs to Lazarus. I was a stranger at your door and you took me in. I owe you more than I ran ever repay. I am not hazy now. I can give you the whole of the details. I started from the Burlington Hotel for a trip to Aldershot. On the return journey to town, my chauffeur steered the motor through a beastly low neighbourhood on the pretext that the princi- pal roads were under repair. In a vilely lighted thoroughfare he struck me a violent blow on the bead."</p>
        <p>" Should you know the man again?" asked Chinnery. Lawson did not immediately reply. 11. drank some ltionmel, put the glass down on the table, gazed abstractedly at his ques- tioner, his lirs hardening into • thin line; then maid, with cutting preuision, " I could pick that man out from among ten thou- sand."</p>
        <p>"And when you do—" " When 1 do—!" echoed Lawson, Le•rcely„,</p>
        <p>Blaudford and Chinnery exchanged rapid glances, they realised that the treacherous chauffeur's chance of life was small indeed should he ever come within the reach of Michael Lawson.</p>
        <p>"I believe that the attempted murder wan Instigated by Miriam Trafford, who no doubt in revelling in fake serority that I shall trouble her no further. She will learn to her cost that I am still very much alive." Law-on "ma now completely rational, and the others waited eagerly for the tweeted revelation._ . .</p>
        <p>...... "Blandford. you have been a true and firm friend in the hour of need," began Lawson. n You'll find the same quality in me. Thin Is quite an unusual occasion. nod we must drink to our luck. Captain Chinnerw, wiU you join on in a glans of champagne?" I shall be delighted," replied Cbinnery heartily. In Chinnery an old or new acquaintance Of your'?" murmured Lawson, In Blandford's . . . .</p>
        <p>"We were at Oxford together," Blandlord ',turned, in the same low tone.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>CHAPTER VIII. 71111</head>
        <p>CHAPTER VIII. 71111</p>
        <p>The Sappho Club was originally founded b, a rich and eccentric Mall of no great origin, whose highest ambition was to shine in the upper ranks of society. He had been ex- tremely desirous of being elected a member or the most exclusive club in London. but was ruthlessly blackballed therefor. Smart- ing under chat he considered an insult, he determined that he would found a proprie- tary club, which should completely eclipse all older institutions of its kind. In this he was partially successful, for he erected • building regardless of coat. The decorations of the interior were unusually luxurious—the Sappho van not to be surpassed by any club in Europe. Not content with architectural beauty, Its founder had engaged the most famous chefs that princely salaries could tempt from Continental capitals. The fame of the cuisine and the rarity of the wines became the talk of dubbin&amp; So the reputa- tion of the Sappho spread, and in spreading it drew between its walls many of the most distinguished men in the great world. Ad- mission to the Sappho was not easy. Captain Chinnery, being an intimate friend of the proprietor and one of the original members, was • persona grata at the Sappho, and his wishes always received the special attention of the waiters. So when he and his two guests arrived, they found themselves comfortably ensconed in a corner screened from intrusive observation. They dined excellently well, and by common con- sent refrained from broaching the subject uppermost in the minds of all three until the repast was concluded and the attendants had withdrawn, leaving the three men still at the table.</p>
        <p>Presently Lawson broke the silence which in anticipation had become almost painful. "My task is difficult, and I scarcely know how to begin," he said. "Begin at the beginning," euggested Chin- nery. "Blandford," Lawson said, "I don't know velo•ther you have confided to Captain Chin- nery the events which I related to you on th., evening of our first meeting?" v I read him my jottings," answered Bland- fir I, shortly. "Such being the cane, I'll commence from Pm point where I had reached the eery ants' quartern in safety. I noticed there was a want of If. in the house, not a man or woman was and I wondered at the absence of th 'la who were usually bustling about. I stn., away to my own room., and then, having removed the traces of dirt and Root from my clothes, hands, and face, I changed ity Oriental garb to a gentleman's lounge suit, mei sought the room from where I fol- lowed Trafford Khan to the Temple of the Ool.len Sun. No one was there. I plumed through room after room searching for Aide, but all were alike deserted. I met no one , from whom I might learn the reason of the death-like silence of the whole place. Life , there was none. Even the birds in their : gilded cages were gone. I wondered had the household suddenly departed, leaving me the sole occupant of that vast dwelling? I had been absent in the treasure vault longer than I supposed, for a clock struck the hour of three. Leaning cautiously from a window, I eat+ amazed to see the servants assembled in the courtyard, piling luggage on to several vehicles. into which they then stowed them- selves away. The porter opened the gates, and they drove away. Why this sudden de- parture, and why was I left alone in the house? The more I reflected, the more con- ' winced I became that there was a mystery of which I, at present, knew nothing. I con- tinued my search, hut was not rewarded by finding any solution to this tailing° state of things, and I obstinately determined to ascertain the cause of this sudden change. I was puzsled and alarmed. Several times I called the name of the Khan's confidential valet, but dared not raise my voice too lond. Obtaining no response, I sought his room. It was all in confusion and abandoned. Evidently he bad packed in hot haste. A storm wan brewing, a flash of lightning in. tensified the infleenee of the hour, then a lend thunder clap gave the aignal for a deluge of rain. I stood in the anloon tremb- .sng, and thoroughly unstrung. A wish to run from the spot seized me. I bounded to the bottom of the marble staircase into the vast ball, the latticed galleries seemingly haunted by unseen spectres. The heavy storm clouds darkened the light of day, and the gloom pre- vented me from seeing distinctly. I wrenched at the door; it was bolted and securely locked, as. *sere all the other. that I tried.</p>
        <p>I retreated discouraged to my room, a feeling of calamity weighing me down. You can con- ceive of my astonishment when I found that during my wanderings some person had ran- sacked every box, every drawer and cup- board. What could I do next? I thought of Aide. and then I knocked at her sitting-room door, which wan opposite to mine. Receiving no response, I entered. But Aide wan not there. The sun shutters were closed, the chamber in nemi-darkness. Almost myself, I rushed frantically into the pietur., gallery. but Fate mocked at my efforts. I could iliscover nothing. Some instinct whis- pered to me that there was another wanderer in the building, and I resolved to find who that wanderer was. Hearing a distant rust- ling, I crouched in the rear of a statue. The storm was at ite height, and the darkness profound ; I could only diatinguish dimly the furniture and the gleam of the grew ether law iocruppil rith cotPux.vd. "On"'</p>
        <p>The rustling came nearer, more cosii.,ei. sure•ratitious dread consumed me, which could not conquer. I was fast becoinin desperate, terror took posses...ion of my sen ieit. I had witnessed scenes of ruthle,, crime in my childhood, which Isere indelibly printed on my mind. A chill passed into in) veins, for a figure in white was stealing along the gallery. My bead was bathed in perspi- ration, my feet seemed to be rooted to the floor. A brilliant flash of lightning lit, up the gallery for an instant from end to end, and in that instant the form of a woman was revealed to me. For a moment I was stupe. fir-d, then I atarted in pursuit. Call me coward if you will, but I wan overcome, swayed, took a step forward, tripped, threw out my arms, and fell. I must have fainted. When my cones returned to me I ruse bewil- dered. There still misted in my brain the resolve to track that woman, and I staggered tower&amp; the archway through which she had •anished. The quiet and apparently empti- ness of the mansion bade me be cautious, and kept at the bark of the at ben till I reached the cortni nun hieh the white-robed figure had pushed aside. No one was to be seen, and I crept onwards until my course wan stayed by a moan. I wan petrified by an in- Rene fear. As I stood there I heard a sob- bing gasp. 'My God ! Is it murder?' I cried. "I mastered my fear just as the rustling warned me to be on my guard. and I premed the closer in my hiding place. A skirt touched my feet; I was no more a coward, for that sweeping skirt revealed exactly to me whom I had to encounter, for the perfume which war wafted from the woman'a clothing wan that used by Miriam Trafford." " With what are you charging heel" asked Chin nery frigidly. " With murder," answered Lawson promptly. " I mpoirible, " Chin nery rejoined indig- nantly.. cannot, I will not, believe it." " '</p>
        <p>"Cfrod forbid I sbn:dd bring a false accusa- tion against Miss Trafford or anyone else," said Lawson solemnly. "When you have heard all my story, yon, gentlemen, shall be the judges. I ('dare positively that Minn Trafford was the woman I saw at Trafford House that awful night. I peered after her. who had no unwittingly betrayed herself. and turned over in my mind what could be the reason of the sudden break-up of the house- hold? Why had the servants all gone. and why had she stayed behind? I remembered a furious quarrel I had heard between Miriam and the Khan, and the threat hurled at her that she was no true child of his, and that he would disinherit her and leave him vast wealth to Aide and myself, who had, he declared cynically, the hest right to it. I shook no longer; in that moment I became a terrible ad ry for her to dread. I will be her Nemesis, always and for ever."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>CHAPTER IX. oRD KEAN.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER IX. oRD KEAN.</p>
        <p>Michael l.awson lay dreamily back in his chair, immersed in the immenaity of his 11 rongs and thew° of his beloved Aide. To himself he was calling her by every tender and endearing name of which he could think. Be wrapped himself, as it were, in a mantle of solitude, and to men of the world like Blandford and Chinnery this creature of im- pulse and of Oriental training van an enigma which they could not underatatut. Chinncry wan the first to speak. " Your bare atatement would not satisfy the law, something more than your unmip- ported charge would he required." The law !" ejaculated Lawson, contemp- tuously. "Be calm," intervened Blandford. . . . . . .</p>
        <p>- . . "I shall not invoke the Engliah laws against a woman. In Persia there are other modes, and they are more effectual than yours. I shall adopt Eastern cunning. Miriam is far too clever not to have left a loophole, aye, a dozen, by which Ale can creep through fifty laws. I'll match her against any of your Scotland Yard detec tines,'' he ended.</p>
        <p>To' end the discussion Blandford again in- termitted: "Please so on with your story. I am impatient and intereated." "The situation in which I found mvself," Lawson continued, "was one of considerable difficulty, and produciel n mental blank which, however, did not last for any length of time. As noon as I was sure whom I had to deal with I reeolved In set about my qui,t matematically. Instir se in •iiimels bails them to find their mamters; rem:oiling mixer bestowed by Providence is implanted 111 man. and my deductions from the mysterious inci• dents of the day drew me to the Khan'e bed- room by a private moulage, which was known to very few persona. Its secret entrance wan behind the bed; I 'dipped the panel aunt pualted aside the curtaine. The chamber was /away,' lighted by a tiny hanging lamp, filled %nail sweet melting oil, that wan never allowed to go out, and the yellow glow it threw canoed dark shadows to fall here and there. My night by thin time was accustomed to the light, and I nearly shouted out aloud, for there lay the Khan, etark. stiff, and appa- rently dead, him eyee fixed upwards, his feature, horribly conveleed. I was not sheeted or even sorry, for he had been a cruel tyrant to me and to the girl I loved. I could not be taken by surprise, for there es% no need to enter tbe chamber. I beet wrong the bed. He wax !mildly cold, his legs were twisted an though he had died in agony; in hie right hand, tightly clenched. was a roll of notes, between the fingers of the left was a piece of stout paper upon which these linen were traced. I removed the notes by forcing the rigid fingers apart." "What did the left contain?" asked Chin- fiery, him faee aingularly blanched. "Evidently a chart, but the fingers that clutched the paper were immovable, mid bearing the ghostly rustle of Miriam's dress. I roughly tore it from the true grasp, and at the same instant • small gold inetrument got entangled in my cuff. I gale a warrh- log glance around, and by the bedside onus- the valet also dead. A footstep, and I betted slowly in the direction of the sound; Miriam had glided into the death-chamber. We re- mained motionless). gazing at one another. Her abject terror at my presence faded into tigerish hate. She rushed to seise me, but I allowed the curtain to fall, and quickly closed the panel." After an awkward pause, of which there had been several during the evening, Chin. nery said judiciously: "You speak id Miriam entering the death- chamber, yet you inserted a while ago that Trafford Khan is alive. You are contradic- tory, Mr. Lawson." Lawson smiled, unnamed at the rather haughty tone of his host. "No doubt, Captain C.itlonery. you con- sider me an undetected plunderer, hot it was not so. Later, when I came to examine the roll I found my own name written on it, and, in addition, the. wte..t• t • Web.' Lawson. Half year's income,' and below the date. 'f his , was in Trafford Khan's own writing. I de- termined to accept it as I found it. If mite der had been committed, and I were found there, I might have been the scapegoat, and I had no inclination to play the part of a martyr. Besides that, I had too keen an in- centive to line and to establieh my identity, which I was then more sure than ever wan one I ehould not be ashamed of." I Lawson mused for a moment or so. and then, taking a packet from his bresat pocket, unfolded several sheets of paper, disclosing 'a metal box; ineide this was • minute gold instrunient, which BI mdford was about to ' tend,. bust Lawson awiftly stopped him. "Don't touch that, or you may puncture your finger." "Poison!" the two men whispered. "Tell me, why should this instrument have been lying on the Khan's bed?" asked Chin- isery. "Did he inject morphia?" said Blandford. "Not he. He had nerves of steel. This was meet on those he wished out of his was for • period. Gentlemen, you cannot follow me, therefore I'll demonstrate the working of this daeserons toy. ebserve the bulb ne bigwer than • pet yet it contains ouffieient quantity of a drug to render the strongest man helpless. Insert that needle point in any part of the body, mess the piston, and a medieal man could not detect the difference between suspended animation and actual death."</p>
        <p>lilandlord and Chinnery gave a cry of amazement, their credulity was gone, they now un lerstood Las son's theory, respecting his belivf that Trafford Khan still lived. "Then you believe, really and truly be- .lve. thavvs,ie tate KW'. 'wag 90711 •</p>
        <p>3</p>
        <p>rizo</p>
        <p>trance:" raid Chinnery.'• "I do firmly." "But the funeral." .A sham." "A sham!" echoed the two men. "The body war remo,ed from the coffin, and another one rubstitoted for it."</p>
        <p>"A rinky trick," said Chinnery. "kinky• perhripn, but not difficult when there is an unlimited supply of money. What is more I would pledge my honour that I am right." There was a pasnionate calm in his manner that convinced his hearers of his sineerity. "The roll of banknoten, I was ronvinted, were intended for me. I wan convinced that, after the violent quarrel with his daughter, he intended to make restitution to me. I may be wrong In the even of the law, techni- cally wrong, though I hold the Khan'n written word.; but there in no retreat for me. I go focused, on and on, till my mimics!' is completed. 'The question is, Will you help 1.74; answer Chinnery and Blandford of. Tenni their hand., which !Amnon clasped in bin, tears of gratitude springing to bin eyes. "The necklace ought to be returned," mtg. rated Chinnery. "No, no. The diamond necklace Is a else to my parentage. Shon'd my conviction prove to be an Minden, then Blandford Atoll forward it to Minn Trafford. I cannot let it go while I am waiting for the day when I Atoll have no need to say to myself, 'Who art thou?'"</p>
        <p>"Don't be carried sway by your own en. thusiaam," said Blandford, in • tone of warning. "I won't. At pres-nt I am u man in the midst of his own loneliness. holding out his hands for help. Aided nr unaided, I must win my lost heritage." lie turned to them appealingly, his eyes radiant with hope. Will yoll he the men to help?" Together they answered: "We will.° (To be continued.)</p>
