        <p>Wast.v.v G.- In —A very euccestiful m7lsicel evening took place in the Wortley. Sunday school on Wednes- day evening, which was arm:teed foe by the Wesley Guild. rt.. Goods ably presided rind a fret class pro- gramme of instruntentel and vocal mueic was rendered. Mian tlery Watson played two pianoforte solos. Miss Dodds, Miss It. Demos and Miss 11. Munroe each sang two solos. Mimi Cook alto rendered greet assistence with her recitation and reading. LIBERAL Cite—A team of Millard players from the above club too played the return watch at the Runcorn Liberal Club on Saturday. The games were very evenly contested and at the close, after l'enketh winning ti games and the home team 9, the Runcorn chili were the winner. by 1011. The club entertained the visitors to ten which was greatly appreciated.</p>
        <p>SOCIAL AT 511,110,..—1n connection with the Penketh Tennis Club a social was held on Satorday last in the Went Senkey Stint's)? School, which proved highly successful in every respect The evening was npitst in progressive guessing competitions, songs, dancing, An amateur variety troope which was termed the Snooter troupe, had linen got together by Mr. E.G.kledley(secrelary ',and was greatly appreciated, contributing :k number of very attractive items which included "The soldiers chorus " frorn Faust." Songs were also rendered by the Misses E Foster, A P Jones and Miss Caudle, Messrs E 0 Medley, 0 II Griffiths, Cll Johnstone and I. Pierpont The supper which had been entered for, find served by the lady members of the club, did them great credit. Miss CFL Johnstone wise the accompanist. SANKEY PARISH (IOCNCTI..</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Sankey Psrish Council was hold in the Council School on Mondsy evening. Mr. W. Furness presided. nod the other members prwsent wstre hissers. In or, Helton, Wright, and J. Smith (dark).</p>
        <p>A FOI 1. .11R001C.</p>
        <p>A letter was preawnted hy the Clerk from the Rural Dint let Council, which stated that in eccordance nub instruction. the Medical Officer of ttenli h and the Sanitary Inspector had inspected a brook off Li voriimil. road, Santiey, and had found it wholly polluted and in a very fool state. Altogether there were eight sewers discharging into it, which reprimentml 36 hoiden and two Coral grounds. The Medical Ginger and Sanitary Inspector suggested that a nine inch pipe he laid alongside the brook, commencing at Wright'. farm, Chapel Prow. The whole length of newer to he laid would lie :7.1 yards, n-hick would include four manholes and one oes.pool. The nervevor estlmetel that the cost of the work would be £152.</p>
        <p>The Chairmen thought they oticht to necept the whole etheine. It M. 4 a scheme which was going to take the sewerage from Sankey for all time. After a long discussion it wee decided, on the pro- position of Mr. Halton, seconded by Mn. Taylor, to appoint a ,lepotation of members to visit. the brook and obtain timber details before coming to any decision.</p>
        <p>MR. .1. A. SEDDON AT KINKETH. LABOUR PARTY'S RECEPTION AND SOCIAL. A very successful reception one held in the Wesleyan Sunday Schools, Penketh last Saturday when the local branch of the Laboor Party held their annual social. About 250 were present at the entertainment. Ms. Seddon, who vies accompanied by Mrs. Seddon and the Ken. A. Harvey, of Widoes,vota accorded et grand recep- tion when he mounted the platfortn,the whole meeting cheering soul singing " For hose jolly good fellow." The Chairman Mr. G. F'oullmer in his opening speech referred t•• the local party's intention of buildiag a Labour Club in the village and stated that estialsctury progress wen being made with the subscriptions. Mr. Seddon in his speech referred to the "accident " which happened at the last election, and said he had been surprised at the kindisess which had hens shown to him since his defeat, by persons ,slio were not inti- mately acqoainted with biru shell, Mr. Seddon also mentioned the progress the ladies' section of the I.shour Party had male, nod said " the ladies ware going to prove themselves the better men." Their opponent., leed the Primrose League to interest the Indies and give them the .•pnortunity of helping, and so the ladies of the Labour Party were organizlng an independent line of attack. Mr. Seddon also dealt with the Rome Rule question and the Parliament fill s bic'n he wee confident woilid pass. .. • '</p>
        <p>The entertainment was grently improved by a sketch entitled Ihe Democratic Trio! of a Doke." Tne Duke was charged with" living without working," and his defence provokea much amusement. The principal character. were :--Duke, Mr. A. T. Clay ; Judge. r. H. Shaw ; Policemen, 11,. .1. Hughes ; Foreman of the Jury, Mr. 0. Carman. There were twelve jurymen, who found the Duke guilty, hut recommended flint to mercy on account if his limited.insder.tanding.</p>
