        <p>Padgate.</p>
        <p>Wlniy,t Cnroi d asp HOUR lhitaaloaa.—This ensue! Home Missionary meeting in connection with this place was bald on Tuesday evening, brit owing to counter-attractions in the town, the attendance was poor. Mr. Mark Howarth, who presided, related some of his experiences as a mission worker in Liverpool 'dome eerier the Rev. Charles Garrett. Is his address he remarked that although people csnnot he made sober or clean by Act of Parliament, if Parliament will only remove some of the drink shops which abound at. every street corner, and deal more drastically with the /waning problem the rest may safely be left in th• hand• of the missions.—The Rev. W. 1.. flannel], Ill)., to the course nt a most interesting speech spoke of the great longing for revival a hich actuate. many people at the present day, and stated his conviction that no result, would accrue until Christians acre not only nominally such, hut ware thoroughly soaked in and •baorbed by Christianity. With reenrd In the many wrongs from which humanity suffer, it should be Impossible from the Christian standpoint to look upon those wrongs aa anything hot Cu affront to the Gospel of Christ. In quoting an Incident from the life of Brad- laugh, the speaker illustrated how useless to the world and what at, Ot,jl3ll:t of contempt to the creediess man who thinks,is the milk-and-water Christian. liefmring to the real onenees of borne and foreign mission., Mr. Hannan contracted the state of the letter a hundred years ego with its preeent aspect. Then the white man carried the gospel ti India and dibina an liwas regarded as th• emissary of a Christian nation. At that time an Oriental wars rarity in this land, uovr hr is to be constantly met with in OUr large towns end universities, and hes every opportunity of geeing Roglish life as it really In. including muck districta as Ancoats, Whitachapel end Bermondsey. When the missionary, where work is already difficult enough, is confronted by this young man recently returned to his home, he is asked why he does not keep his religion I which is not neariv as old a• their's) at home and make his own land Chrietien in reality before coining there. Tho churches most be able to furnish an answer LO them quasi o /Land ao enable the missionary to say : " l'ou have seen our slums, have you seen our slum mission, the centre of life, energy, and Christian love and influence ? " The ritete• mast is often made that chiirchsa block the way of roforrn. lint thoitgla this was denied, it was emphatic- ally waserted that the real °batede to nay true revival lay in the want of spiritual unity in the church, in the many quarrels about small things when they should he ettaining ever upward and forward sword in hand to conquer the common foe in the name :of the Lord. 'I he report was reed by the 120 v. T. (Madding and chewed that the total contributions amounted to £38,000, a deficit on last deer of LI iv. The amount sent from the Warrington circuit was £l9, Padgete's contribution being E.:. A FARCE AT CRAPPENHALL. PARISH BICKERINGS.</p>
        <p>The idiocy:m.B.les of conic nf the Orappenhall villagers are generally nwinifest at a pariah meeting. In tact, they are becoming no familiar that one would feel atrange if thinga unloolied-f•r failed to happen. On Wednesday evening lest thin peculiar •lennent •gain emerged cad after the Chairnian Mr. liaditrth had declared the half early meeting closed some of the more irrepressible embers of the community took upon themselves to held", meeting if ratepayers "only to postpone their protests until another gathering to be bed a each nest Wednesday. '</p>
        <p>Among ihose present were the following Councillors: —Messrs A Lister•liaye, Bradford, Ellison, Lomas, Fox and Carter, arid also Mr C If Moult (the clerk.) The trouble began at the commencement of the meeting, which was not no well attended ant usual. After tee Clerk had read through the minutes of the two previobe meetings. Mr. Jenkins asked whether the agenda in connection with ths Isst meeting was gone into folly. The Chairman : lie wish to know if this is a correct record of toe minutes. We call dimities that •Iterserds. Without further ado the minutes were &amp;vend to, and then</p>
        <p>Jenkins repested his question. 'the Chairsuan I suppose he (Mn. Jenkins) is per- fectly aware that the rearrou was because the Chairman Jolt the meeting, and therefore it came tote abrupt termination.</p>
        <p>Mr. °Jenkins I suppose the Chairmen was quite juetifl, din leaving it if he felt no diepoeed. The Chairman of the Parish Council is not of necessity chairman of the parish meeting lie could refuse to be the chairman of the pariah meeting if he wished to do err, and that is no justificstion for the meeting closing and the ratepayer. being disappointed because the husinen which they had met to attend to was not done. If I am correctly informed, there win a resolution put to the meeting an to whether it should go on or not audit was carried that it,shoold. •• .</p>
        <p>...... Mr. Bredioril mid he would like to oak Mr. Jenkins whether at dal time he eat in the chair lie said the Chairman of the Parish Council was the chairman of any parish meeting. (Hear, bear). Mr. Jenkins r Mr. Bradford's memory is rather short. lam not surprised. I think you may remember as well as myself when I appealed to Mr. Bolt as to who was to take the cheir,be said that the chairman of the Parish Council ought to. . •</p>
        <p>—ni;:..fl;;3l:;nr.ito Mr. Jenkins): You appealed to y0n.0.11,</p>
        <p>Mr.J.nkini : Thank you. Mr. Bradford : You sat in the chair and said you were just going to ho chairmen. 51r. Jenkins : I think that Mr. Bradford is quite wide of the mark. What he said had little hearing on ! the matter in question. I want to know, since the meeting was called for the purpcxe of transacting busi- newt, why the busineas on the agenda was not gone into The Chairman : We cannot say anything forther on the matter, except that the Chairman left the meeting.. We can diecuse any other Login°aa you like to bring forward now.</p>
        <p>—.lll;:iirTs-cliord said he would like to ask why the Chairman left the meeting after cr.iling a meeting of ratepayers, ? Nir. denkinn took no notice of thin remark and turn- ing to the Chairman said You retiree to give me an answer</p>
        <p>The Chairman I cannot give you an answer and further you refuse to satinet thy question pat to you. Mr. Jenkins : I want to know ehy the September meeting ens not gone through. The Chairman (heatedly, t I should have thought that yon would have been the last person to have asked that. Ido not see that you have any tool grievance to bring to the meeting. I am surprised that you took the course you did, the meeting being brought to an abrupt termination instead of letting the teeming gone. ' Continiting,he said that some parishioners had asked for a meeting to I. held but he did not consider tne bus', seas WBll of sufficient importance to warrant one. Mr, Jenkins t Then you really tell the rat•payere In plain language, that you defy them in any and every form.</p>
        <p>—The Chairman Ido not tell them any such thing. NI, Jenkins : They come here to the meeting to trananCt certain twines.% and you close the meeting against them. They pas resolution that the meeting be continued. You close the meeting and you do not hold another meeting in face of that. I The Chairman was not at the meeting at all. Mr. Jenkins: Just no. You Were not in tho meeting hut you are in the chair tonight andl epires! to you.. the chairman.</p>
        <p>Tho th,rmr: Any other business ? Alr.Nicoolls considered that the Chairman had rather evasively answered thequostiondthr. Jenkins,hesalityvas treated, while he tam in the chair, exceedingly shabby. not even being thanked for bin service.. In fact, if be (the speolser; reel rightly his resignation was not accepted, hence he WWI the Chairman of the Parish !Council still. He did not think that Mr. Jenkins had I been treated in • civil or courteous way. I Mr. Jenkins ; Von know that it woe in the power of that meeting to elect another Chairman, but of course I was not there to see whim actmlly took place and I am surprised at the council acting in the manner they did, siruply bemuse rho Chairman retired. Mr. Bradford : Why did you call the meeting and leave the chair</p>
        <p>ThetisMr. Jeekirt my business, but I may tell Mr. Bradford now that ill had only my own wish to consult, and that only, I would readily explain why I left,which might not been pleasant to listen to. Icier Mr. Jenkins said theta chargo had been brought hi Mr. Burrow,. la raterinyerl tbat the bunibew of the meeting could have gone on though he (Mr. Jenkine) bad left the chair. Was that correct? The Clerk The Clerk is of the opiniorchat when the olficinl chairman, that is, when the chairmen of the parish council in chairman of the parish meeting and he commences the trumpets of the meeting and afterwards leaves the chair—and we have a precedent within recent years—Omit clip.. the meeting. Mr. Jetkin, Theo sir, allowing the argument</p>
        <p>advanced by Mr. Honk to be good may I he allowed to sels why you ea the auccevaor to the chair did not call, meeting no all to complete the businea• which IMO incomplete at the tie,.? The Chairman : The Clerk has given no ricient reuon• why the last meeting WWI pot proceeded with. The reason the ropiest for soother meeting wee not com- plied with was hecaime I did not consider the bilainesa important. 1. long ea it in my plait.ge to he chair. man of the Pariah Council I shall conaider whether the 6116,166 e is of sugicient importance before I call • meeting. Mr. Jenkins I think You are not replying to my question. I wanted to know why the meeting was bronght ton mei-mann, close ? The Chairman : I cannot answer that, I was not at the meeting. Mr. Jenkin• But yon, the Clerk, or the Council was renponsible for the Weise. on the agenda being gone . . _ . . .</p>
        <p>The Chairman : Any other business gentleman ? Mr. Jenkins Will you kindly tell on as chairman if the 14.0.11. Act of lt4 bears out your authority to any to the ratepayers who call a special meeting that they ahall not have is meeting. The Chairman Mr. Jenkins, Ido not know that it ii .4.0•114 f to give you any quotation. Mr. Jenkins : I beg your parrlon. The Chairman : I said I shell not cell n meeting unless I think the business is of eunicient importance. Mr. Nicholls wanted the Clerk to tell the meeting what the law on the point wee. The Clerk t (ii to. lawyer end pay for advice. The Chairman asked if there were any other business and noon. littering anything ha declared the meeting closed and forthwith, along with several councillors, proceeded to prepare to leave the room. The Clerk on being rorpiested to stay he two or thr•• retepayers present rentsrsed that the meeting had cloned ,no far.. be was concerned and.. he left the room Mr. Jenkins remarked that a reisolution would be tient to the Local Government Board respecting the Clerk's conduct Mr. Nicholle was votsil to the chnir, and after a few minutes of undecisi re talk it Yves reerlverl to call another meeting fora week on Wednesday. A vote of thnoke was unanimously passed by those remaining to Mr. Jenkinr for his services as member and chairman of the Pariah Council.</p>
