        <p>CONORE,LTI,AI. CHRISTIAN SERVO E Buss—This meeting was held on Monday evening in the school vestry when a good number of young people attended. Mr. Cyril Ilowcroft occupied the position of leader. Mr. J. H. Holt gave an address entitled " Trust in Christ "which was touch appreciated by everyone. The meeting closed with short prayer. from various member.</p>
        <p>LANTERN SERV !ClL—This service wag very well sttended ngnin on Sunday evening in the Primitive Methodist schoolroom. Mr. Broome conducted the meeting. Miss 'Si. J. Appleton rendered a aid° during the evening in a most pleasing way. The story was entitled, " A man I, I hie word " it being the remainder of last week's story, and wns illustrated with pictures on the screen. A colloction was taken et the close of the meeting. I,VNIM AND DISTRI,7 Woot FM's Linro ci. Asso, IA- SloS.—A social meeting was held on 'I buraday evening in the Primitive Methodist schoolroom, ats which a good number attendeil Mr. Weller gave a good speech and Mr. G. 1,. Welford presided. Songs were rendered by Miss lissartelli, of 51suchester,snd Mr. Ernest Jones of Brooklanda. Refreshments were nerved during the evening. This was the concluding meeting of the season.</p>
        <p>NATURAL 1114TOIni Lymut and Dis- trict Naturel History Society held their annual !emi- t.. meeting on Monday evening at Foxley. The Secretary, Mr. G. H. Howard, read a report of tie year's work. The past year hod been a most nocoess- tut one. Twenty-four meetings had hi-es held at eighteen of which there had been lectures or papers given. Also there bad Leen field ramble., museism emits, and a w.gonnette picnic to Pickmere. Mr Champ and Mr Honard were re-elected president and secretary respectively, and Mr Fielding was elected vice•president. It was proposed to give a series of public lantern lectures for which a small charge for admission will be mule, to cover expenseu. March being the commencement of the Society's year, anyone wishing to join may receive information by applying to the Secretory. Stun CHIZMTIAN F.NDEAvOUIt SOCIETV.—On Monday evening last, the members sad friends of this society nssembled in the schoolroom to hear an address by Mr. Holland, of Warrington, on " The ethic. of gambling," nhich proved very instructive. 'hiring his discourse, the apeaker told boo, when cortlieg home front London, he just escaped the bands of a card sharper. A man entered the Bartle compartment and asked him to have a game of cards. Mr. Hdlool told him he had never played in all his life. I've roan said he would teach nim, he pressed him, hut Mr. I lollnrd Wits limn Dud declined his odor. At Rugby another gentlemen got is, the card man netted hint to haven game and he con- sented. At Crewe the professional felt the train, sod Mr.} lo Hetet asking the gentleman if he had lost. he said he had lost L 5. This was between ltughy and Crewe ! Mr. Mort, the president of the Crouch, aim, added a few remarks on betting nod gambling, and then spoke on the approaching " Young people's mission to young people which begins on Solidly next. Alt 'ethic came n dust VVe went the young for Jesus," by Anita Pearce anti Clique Reniett, and a short prayer by the leader, Mr. P. Starr, brought a zuccessfut meeting to n close.</p>
        <p>THE MAY QUEEN FE,TIVA L.—A public meeting VMS held in Hunts Past Ilion is rue/Iday o•9lling,to consider the advisability of holding a May Quash Festival for the present year. A meeting is called annually to elect a committee for the current year, as the committee of the prsceeding year nesses to exist on the completion of the business connected vrith theif year of &amp;lice. The His. Treasurer, Mr. G. ff. Howard, produced a balance sheet fur 1910 which snowed a lots of over £9O on the year's working and a balance of only EIS ha 7d. It was therefore decided that, owing to the low state of the funds and the prolith.lity that there would be Coronation festivities in the sande month as the festival would beheld, it would be better to post- pone the event till 1911 An interesting event took place at this meeting. The presentation oft handeome oak clock with full chimes was made to Mr W Ashton, honsecretary to the committee. The chairman,Alr Jeri D IS Smith, in making the presentation, spoke in glowing terms of Mr Ashtou'a inestimeble services in connec- tion *ith the festival, and VOiCA the feelings of the whole committee, when he remarked that mulch of the mincers of the festival wan due to Mr Milton's capable management. Mr. .Ashton, in thanking the committee, said he should always value their present we it would be • perpetual reminder of the pleturent times he had spent ein the committee. Toe clock, *Lich was supplied by Mr. F. A. Morris, bore t silver plate inscribed es follows —Presented to Mr. F. W. Ashton by the Lyrum May Queen Fextival Coni- mittee, in recognition of his valuable serviceri as Ho. Secretary to this committee for the past live yearn, February 211 h, 1011.</p>
