        <p>Laureart Llti-TCltr..—On Monday evening, February 27th, Mr. if. Congress gene s lecture in the Weser.s schoolroom entitled " Afloat. and ;shore," being an account of a holiday on the Norfolk Broads. Mr. Conareve's lectures are alwsys looked forward to with great pleasure, consegio-ntly there was largo gather- ing to hest him on Monday last. The lecture wan illustrated alit, shoot ninety insiinitlnent Midi, of Mr. Congress'. own production, which, together with the charming and explicit way he tells his story, provided • very enjoyable entertainment for all. Mr. Congress also has such s memory for historical and geogrsphical facts which he weaves into his story that him lectures are quite ae education. The Res. F. W. Kernick, M A., who presided, asked Mr. Meldritm to propose the best thanks of the audience to Mr. Congreve, tbia was seconded hr Min, Dutton and enthiasiutically carried. Wg-it.ry OVIL,.—The monthly devotional meeting was held on Wednesday evening last.irrhen Mr. F. light, of Warrington, gave a %cry elontivot addreu on " Christian despondency." Mr. lAwton, the vice- president, presided users good attendance.</p>
