        <p>Rt7NC3RN RURAL COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>This fortnightly meeting of he Runcorn Meal Dis- trict Connell wan held in the Urban District Council Offices, Runcorn, on Toeaday owing to the outbreak of scarlet rover at the Dutton Workhouse. Mr. T. Wright presided neer a good attendance. The officers present wore :—Nleeters. D. F. Ashton (clerk), J. Swinton and Mr. Diggleiiiiiryayorsu.and J. Farrington (inspecto,,.</p>
        <p>ONIYL • 1-p.• Al TO SIOTICSII7 AG E.:</p>
        <p>The Clark read lettere from tiesers7C.-1-...lrans and luso Lawton complaining of the bad smell from the sewage ou Rusoortnroad below Moore Station. Mr. Lawton also stated that there was a cue of diphtheria and the nuisance might he the MO. of it. Continuing the Clerk said some Limo ago it mac alleged that there was a enhance in the same part and a 1101 UM ittee of the District Cetincil mat the Moore Parish Council who asked tho District Parish Council not to go in for a big scheme for dealing with the sewage, but to clean ' out the sewage ditches. Mr. Lawton, one of the gentle- men who complained, WAS a monitor of the Pariahl Council at she time and dirl nut ohjact to that colas, Mr. Jun' /mid that the matter complained of wee not a big affair at all. He suggested that the highway. Rumours Committee dealt with it iglus they were in the district.</p>
        <p>M. C. E. Linker, sen. It will be six weeks before we. go there. . . .</p>
        <p>Mr. G. Pilling asked if the ditches had been cleaned ont as ecommended he the Ooromittee. The Clerk replied In the allirmetive. Mr.Jonee said th it as regarding the Cale of diphtheria near to the tillage] nuisance. the father •f the child attributed the disease to the lad being in Warrington. Mr. Ferrington was of opinion that the alleged nide/ince had nothing to do with the diphtheria. Plenty of storm eater had gene down the ditch. Mr. Woodward toonght that scone steps should be taken to settle the matter as the question of Moore sewage had been going on for three years. Mr. Hdylence won also of opinion th it the ditch bad nothing to do with the diphtheria. He thought the committee which was dealing with the question would be well advised to cdnnect the pipes together through a portion of Mr. Jones field. Ph. matter wan left in the hands of the committee appointed some time ago.</p>
