      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>" WHO WAS JESUS CHRIST ?"</head>
        <p>" WHO WAS JESUS CHRIST ?"</p>
        <p>Interesting New Church Lecture.</p>
        <p>ho was Jesti• Christ r less the subject of a lecture 1,7 the Hev John Howarth, of livolton, in the Nis/tonic Hall, on Wednesday evening. The remora was given tinder the auspices of the New Charon, and was eel! attended.</p>
        <p>Vi hen couaidertng the question, said the lecturer, we were at 00ce confronted with the eiamintlal factor of all religious and Christian doctrine—namely with the unity of the Godhead. We could act close our eyes to the fact that there elOl all almolitte belief in the en istence of some supreme God—the existence of a Power above all others. Look where we might there was no doubt that there was a yearning to know and to learn ill ' about this great unknown Power. They were certain of one foot, and that was the presence of. Supreme Power. Hut they might go a step further than that and say that the idea of a Supreme lining was centred around a power or powers. Tne only answer to the question aeemed poseible by saying that the idea must have been implanted in the heart of man by that Supreme Power itself. It was needle. to assert that all thinking !ample acknowledged thi• supreme power to be God. "We are compelled to believe,"continued Nl•llowerth, that the greatest troth of all truths is that Gml is ore. You will be wondering why a steternent such as thisnpmarently an Altritiam, ahoold be made:Limes is an id. lunar minds of three Divine Persons, and for that reason alone. Immure of that idea, we are forced to believe the troth that foul is one." The lectures went on to esidonyour to show that Jehovah Himself sac the Redeemer of the World end that it was He Himself who came down to north amongst men; and read passages front the 01,1 Testament which, he said, proved that lend was the Redeemer of the tiorld. It was a strange fact, he setiddhat in no passage of the Bible in there any intinietion in reference to sending another to take the place of, iir act as subatitute for the great Jehovah Himself. The question resolved Itaelf into whether Jehovah did really corns into the World or whetter he sent another. That was the only yr...ion on which the doctrine of the God head could stand, and was the fundamental principle of all religious belief. They in the New Church with the evidence of the scriptures before them steno of the opinion that Jesus Christ wee God, the Word made Flesh. They believed that the Lord roust assume human form in order 4n carry omit Hie great work, and that He th. became Man rinii fonght against evil. "The bird Jean. Christ, tit one orily God, is the God main the New Church wor hip," added the lecturer. " For Ile alone is the ' Hod cher and Sa•iour of men." Speaking on the subj t of the Holy Spirit, AG. Howarth ramerked that norni gave him more poin than tri hear the term Holy Ghost a okra of for there was no reference in the Holy !scriptur to it from ieginning to end. They bad ample : e•idence (the fact that the Spirit Proceeded from the ' Father. "hey hail reference to that when David mid, " The spir t id Jelloish stood by me and His Spirit was in my to nth.' • British omen's Temperance Association. In coon tii n with the above aesocietion dire., of the work us visiiiid the Bewsey-road We,leyan Women'. (meeting on Monday afterhoon. Mrs. Hartille address ,9 based on' Christ's love for mankind." On Wed eday afternoon the usual monthly meeting waa held in Bold-street school. Mies Pestle Hutton presided Newspaper cuttings were read by several of the mbers. On Thursday afternoon a vieit was paid to tlie Wornen's meeting at the Independent Mehodi t. Scekton Heath, when Mrs. Clatworthy gave an utereating address on "Talents' and Mrs. Simpson ;ova a reading a the life of Fiances Dillard. New tuemlers were carolled at each</p>
