      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>Warrington s Drunkenness.</head>
        <p>Warrington s Drunkenness.</p>
        <p>Sir. In your report of the British Women'a Temperance Associattoo meeting I read with interest the outmpoken remarks of the Chairman and the Rev. A. M. Mitchell on the drunken state .4 our town.</p>
        <p>Certainly to stand second of ten Northern towns for drunkenness, to say the least, is not creditable. Ilowever,a short time ago we were almost assured that this Rev. gentleman was going to be severely dealt with for exposing the until, which time keeps mot, iog. In fact, if the critics would look around with an unbiased mind they would have to admit the statements were under and not overdrawn. . . _ . . .</p>
        <p>I would suggest that the Licensing Committee get • return of all so-called male and female tontine clubs held on licensed premises. They would then find to their astonishment we have thousands of young women and men in our very midst members of such, for what purpose ? We have legally tegisterel friendly societies to supply the requirements according to law. If the authorities and othms are no interested in the betterment of our town they should strike at the root of this evil.—Yours, . .</p>
        <p>who are not. By what system of morality arelivon going to decide who is right and who is wrong Please give cony man—even a Churchman—some credit for possesaing a conscience and if he doe. " bolster up the trade' credit him with as much honesty of purpose as I am willing to concede to the ; Bishop of Liverpool who supported the Licensing Bill at the meeting von refer to. I cl,, ant desiair of you conversion to tile paths of True Temperance instead of Bigotry, mid I hope to have an early i pportunity by mean, of a public meeting in Warrington to convince you that the Licensing Bill is by no mean, the last wool in Temperance Legislation. On one matter I think we can all agree .d that is the necessity fdr all of on to exercise more tolerance, more charity, and leas prejudice in our controversien—l sin Sir. Yours obedient eervant. HEBBERT CABEY. " Newholm " Leigh-road,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>RATEPAYER.</head>
        <p>A RATEPAYER.</p>
        <p>Mr. Chesterton on Women under the Law.</p>
        <p>Dear Sir,—There appeared not long ago in the Daily News," an article by Mr. G. K. Cheenerton on a Police Court Case. The facts of the case came to light later in the same paper. and on the facts as stated. we may well ask witu Mr. Chesterton " Are we all mad?' The facts as they appeared are as follows - On the 15th February. at Epping Police Court, Annie Woolmore,a young married woman, was sentenced to six weeks imprisonment in Holloway Gaol for " neglecting " her live children. It was stated that the children looked exceeding well in health" and that the woman was sober. She wars, however, stated to be ill, and her hush:led said "she had etarved herself to keep the kids." The husband earned 11;s a seek, out of which he paid 2a. ILI. for rent. There wax na water nniody in the cottage, and it had to h faebed in distance af a quarter it/Is Salle °eras,. For his inability to feed, clothe anti warn, seven people on 13s. lid. a ' week, this woman who was ilbst.d.vsd and overworked (for her husband left all the housework to her) was sent LO lILOLOLI with the despairing cry on her lips "Oh, Land BLOM my soul!" The house has now been condemned as uninhabitable —it oftvinualy watt unfit for habitation long since. Why was the landlord permitted to ask 24. id. in root for a house with no water supply ! The outrageous cruelty of this six weeks imprison- ment sentence is only to apparent. The woman had endeavoured ti do for Neven persons on 1:ls. 6,1, a week. She had had ill-health and no water supply to contend with. She had had no help in the housework from her husband, and when she was too ill and weak to struggle any more she was sent to prison. A case of this kind reserves all one's ideas of common justice. The fact that the mothers of the race are liable to be treated in this fashion, gives the lie to the platitude that laws made by men are eutTicient to treat all the demands of both sexes. Yours etc., M. L. C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>Christian Science.</head>
        <p>Christian Science.</p>
        <p>Sir,—! should be glad if you will allow me some space to reply to the remarks of your correspondent on my leference to ''Christian Science," so-called. In saying that it was neither Christian nor Science, I used the words in their ordinary sense. Christian- ity is a system of belief based upon the reliability of facts recorded in the New Testament concerning Jesus Christ and the substaniial accuracy of tuna report of His teachings." That the dvetrine of Mrs Lilly is very Inc from Christiao could be illustraied without end. Let one instance suffice. Ilr• Eddy says " God never made man capable of sin," " Man is incapable if sin, sickness and death," "Ilan cannot possibly sin, because how can god lapse into evil when God, the mind of man, never sins?" The New Testament says "He shall nave His people from their sins." Are both of these ideas Christian ? . .</p>
        <p>Science stands for "Such knowledge an we pos.., through the employment of our senses and our reason. of the fact+ end laws of nature, italuiling ourselves." Again let one instance suffice. Mrs. Eddy's Gloerary say. ' Nlatter—ruythology ; mortality,another name for mortal mind, illusion, intelligence, nulintance, and 11. in non-intelligence and mortality ; life resoling in death, sod 'tenth in life ; sensation in the eemm- tionles• mind originating in matter the nritssitto of truth . the opposite of •pirit ; the opposite of God ; that of which immort mind takes no cognicinee ; that which mortal mind se., feels, hears, teat., and smells only in belief." Can much a jumble of contradictions be called arientlfie in the usuel IleeeplatiOn cf the term 'The laws of matter, up illustrated by physics, have to be reckoned with by all mrclotrer. sn,sell a, mathetnet icings." Mut E,lilvist,t rave "The so-celled laws of matter are nothing hut false belief, in the presence of intelligence mid life where mind in not.- Is (hit, stientitit ? It is a bold assumption trf you- correspondent that all who cannot accept " Chrietian Science " are mentally incapable. rersonslly I am quite ready to welcome any light from Christian Science or any- where else but it must be in acc.o dance with "that True Light" which comes to us in Christ. I In not pctne an an •nthority on " Christian Science." I made a passing reference thought fully and with full grounds and after serious study. and I owe it to me hearers to state the fact, otherwi. I should not desire to use your faithfully,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>WALTER SEED. 2, Palmyra Square. Warrington, . l'ehroary 27th, Pill. The Church and the Trade.</head>
        <p>WALTER SEED. 2, Palmyra Square. Warrington, . l'ehroary 27th, Pill. The Church and the Trade.</p>
        <p>Sir,--In your article entitled " The Church and the Trade " you cotrinent op ,n the courteously worded letters " you receive in defence of th • " indu•try'of beer-selling and hoer-drinking." Will you permit we to may I think you display extreme tolerance in pith. liehing such letters hut at the same time I consider the attacks of what you term " unprincipled teen,. taller, " require and invite a rejoinder. After all. whether you like or dislike the liquor trade, whether you approve or c ndemn the " industry of beer-drink- ing," I think you fail to remember that the Government you support condescends t • legalise the habit of drinking and is good enough to collect an enormous revenue from the trade you attack Under these circumstance., does not the liquor trade deserve precisely the same treatment and consideration as you would accord to any o, her i:lrlustry carried out with public sanction and legislative support ? If your argument. are simply c .ncerned with them wel and physical effecia of excessive drinking I support you heart and soul, likewise, I would join you in a con- deinnaticn of excessive eating, excessive emoking and in anything. But am I mistaken in ramming that your articles show a marked tendency to merely throw °llium on a trade that apparently causes your unmerited indignation ? In regard to the letter of ' A Fortiori " hie contentions may be right or wrong. Does it very much matter if one section of the Church supports acertain Licensing Bill and another section condemns it? Neither the Church nor the Licensing Bill is infallible. There are Churchmen who support Prohibition, others who support moderate drinking, there are Churchmen who are abstainers and others 4</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>Ellie, Cheshire February 27th, 1911.</head>
        <p>Ellie, Cheshire February 27th, 1911.</p>
        <p>Sir,---I have read cony leader dealing with my letter appearing in your issue of Saturday last, with much Mier...et, and would thank you for the compliment that it, at any rate, was courteously worded. May I, also, venture to suggest that my comment was not altogether ill-timed, unit has enabled you to re-adjust your charge on a more congenial basis? The State, happily reprieved, is now free from the charge of bols•ering up the Trade and the crime now suggested is that the Church and the Trade support political views which. fortunately or unfortunately, labour under your dislike. You seem to be under the impression that it should have been my part to disprove, and merely deny, your statement shoot the Church and the rrarle. I, how- ever, would suggest that the burdeu of proof lies with the eyeliner, and that ton have recognised this by quoting the Licensing Bill. If that in to be your best question. I need only remind you that it has been the pride and boast of your party and its leaders that the majority of the Bishop+, who voice the Church in the House of Lords. were on the side of the Bill.</p>
        <p>The next accosation is that the trade substantially bolsters up the Church. Well, sir, in any eyes it should be a virtue, and not a crime, to liberally con- tribute towards the support if the religious and philanthropic work of a Christian Church, and I can conceive no man offering condemnation unless he courts Christiar it,' itself as an enemy. Intentionally or unintentionally, you have credited the members of the trade with good in lieu of evil and it will be a consolation to them to know that, whatever their sine, you feel sure that their place in the hereafter will be one with that of the Church. Troth is often reached by devious channel., and von seem in heve gained it through the minds of Cabinet Ministers and the tongue of the Home Secretary. If, as you kindly suggest, the local clergy were bound in gratitude to the Trade it is self-evident that this must have been tlre result of their having received from the brewer end the publican a far greater help in their wo: k for the people than has been rendered by those who so loudly profess temperance and righteousnesx,— lours, &amp;c.. March :Ird, 1911.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>FORTIORI.</head>
        <p>A FORTIORI.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CHURCH SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The Lenten Addresses.</p>
        <p>The Breda! Lenten services for men and women Which ere one of the most prominent and useful features of the work of the Wernogton Church Society, com- menced yesterday. In the afternoon the Bishop of Manche/nor (Dr. Knox), addressed a large congregation of women, on the words "And they went out and preached that men should repent." He arid that one of our old divines bud said very beautifully that if the sermon a Repent ye" were continually and always piescbed, and if people would really obey the instruc- tion, no other sermon should be needed. Repentance was before ell other things the message which God's servant was hound to pri.cleini, end especially during the season o* Lent. One of the flint elements of repentance was to make tip one's mind clearly as to what was meant by sin. If instead of making God the centre of our lives we naturally made self the centre, if we only studied our Once convenience and were alweys sayinr in our hearts "I have a right to please myself," if God, instead of tieing present always, was constantly out of mind and neglected, everything we looked upon or thought about was in eome degree teinted with sin. For sin men self put in the place of God. It wee solemnly true that God did, at His own time and in His own way, call nations to account for their sins. How soon He might call one nation to acconnt was known only to Himself. "111 were here as • prophet" continued the Bishop, "sit tell you that i before the end of thin year England would be overtaken by a great war, or that in the course of a year or two civil war would spread ii, blight . over the country, or that in the coming ' autumn pkgon would establish itself on the shores of . England—it I were able to tell you those things with a : prophet's firm conviction, I have DO doubt that SOMPAt ell events, would be moved to repentance. But mithough toward', the end of His earthly work, when 1 Ile saw the awful crime the nation wee going to nom. mit and He was trying to save them from it, our Lord spoke in terms of judgment, that was not the ordinary course of His miuistry, nor His ordinary teaching of . repentance. He loved to put iris other lighte—the. lost sheep found by the Good Shepberd ; the lost coin found by the diligent seeker ; the lout son found hy the agonised father—in these most attractive, heartseercbing forms, not of terror but of love, he put Ilia message of Repentance. . . . Repentance means touting out of darkness into light. It is the throwing ,pen of the shutters of the heart to let in the light of God Himself, If 1 might leave one simple prayer upon your hearts, to sum up all I have tried to say this afternoon, I . would leave with you one petition of the Litany which I think most of asses apt to pass over very lightly From blindness of heart, Good Lord deliver on.'" In the evening the Rev. Dr. Thorp, vicar of Bt. George's, Stockport, addressed a largely attended service for men, taking tie his subject a Is public worship a necessary part of religion ?" He said the question ins nets question that was ever asked by a man who really worshipped. It was got neceseery to ask him, and if he did give an anew, it would be " Yea." Reviewing some of the causes of the decline in public worship, Dr. Thorp said that in his opinion . the Church itself was se much to blame as anyone. He believed that preaching had been put before worship. I ping, and that nowedays people went through the other part of the to price as a kind of dory preparatory to the entertainment of the sermon Hasa. aln my opinion," he said, aWe stoull impress %Iron owns' tree the fact that we go to church to worship God, not ti bear a poor sinner give his own private Vie, on subjects." Th• rev. gentleman appealed to his hearers to light against the modern tendency to despise God and in ursine the necessity of public warship he emphaeised the primary importance of attendance at the 11.1 y Communion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>CROSS COUNTRY RUNNING.</head>
        <p>CROSS COUNTRY RUNNING.</p>
        <p>Ryland.. Will. Harriers had a good muster for an ordinary run over the usual course. Tim o novice section hada race to themselves with the result that E Hrsketh and J Wood deservedly annexed the awards in the order given, and both should prove of service to tbe team in the Liverpool District Novice Championships fortnight hence, or in the St. Helens sod District Z•iovice Teem Race a week later.</p>
        <p>-.Warring-ton A.C. junior section had II not fore race over 5j miles of mixed country. H. /lough showed very promising form by leading he novice division home in good style. The race coon rod for the Novice Points Competition in which E. Scott, wlito was close up behind Hough, still leads. The Novice Team Race at St. Helens on the 25th net should prove interesting, ass strict novice definition has been laid down, and the conditions of six to run and four to count should he very acceptable to the , clubs with • novice division roger for. six mile test.</p>
        <p>NATION F Antroblia, W Antrobus, W Bolton,' 11 Holbrook, 11 K. Jepson. .1 Parsonago, P Shadwell, T P Turion, C Vow, A Wilcock, and 'l' Wall, who have hoon tiolected to represent Warrington AC., at Taolow Court to-day bad • good training spin over sorely nine miles c eintry awl all appeared in good trim. The saloon party 1 • Bank Qoay at 8.20 is morning arriving Eitston 1230. and leaves Paddin on at 2 p.m. arriving Taplow 239 in time for the ce which starts at I p.m. Foreign opinion d.ios not favour the locals, bu the host of the opposition will have to go all the way. The juniors run front the Royal Oak branch at 4 p.m. • to-day.</p>
      </div>
