      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March 4 1911.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>WOMEN LIBERALS THE RECTOR AND ROME. Fairfield and Bewsey Ward Tea Reply by the Rev. Father Whittle. Meetings _ _ WHAT IS CATHOLICITY a</head>
        <p>WOMEN LIBERALS THE RECTOR AND ROME. Fairfield and Bewsey Ward Tea Reply by the Rev. Father Whittle. Meetings _ _ WHAT IS CATHOLICITY a</p>
        <p>It was a desirable innovation when some little time ego the executive of the Warrington Women's Liberal Associairon decided to hold ward tea meet- ings instead of one annual tea meeting of all the members of the Association in the l'arr Hall. The wiwlom of thi, met, was again proved on Tuesday evening when the women of Fairfield and Bewsey Ward partook of a substantial and excellently served tea in Bold-street Wesleyan schoolroom. Over .100 were present and this number was considerably augmented litter in the evening when a splendid entertainment followed. Mrs Dodds presided and amongsi those present were Councillor J. T. LockerJ.P., Mr. rieurge Walker, Councillor James Skelland, Mr. H. New, Miss Crostield, Mrs. Osborne (h.. secretary) Mrs. Walker. (hoe. tren,urer), Miss Walker, Mrs. E. V. Walker, Mrs. Penilierton, and Mrs. Simpson. Interesting speeches on the work of the Association and of the Liberal Party in the House of Commons were delivered by Mrs. Dcdils, Miss Cr.lield, Coun- cillor Locker, Mr. Grorge Walker, Councillor Skelland, and Mr. It New. Miss L. F. Waring of he W.W.S S., delivered art address on the Women's Suffrage question from a non-patty mint of view. The programme was in every way a rapitill one. Songs were given by Mitts Closfield, Miss Dodds, Miss L. Duckworth, Miss Booth, Mrs. E. A. Sanders, Mr. Nurse and Mr. N. Cook. The latter mid Mies Crosfield also took part in a duet. Mrs. Barlow gave some excellent recitations arid Mr. Sanders obliged with several humorous</p>
        <p>Preaching at St. Alban's Church on Sunday evening the Rev. Father Whittle replied to the criticisms of the teachings of Rome recently made in a speech ti the Warrington Church Scciety by the Rector (Rev. Canon Willie.) He said 7 —No tne has ever heard me attack, or say an unkind word against those of another Faith. And further, as a rule. I never take notice, either here or in the l'resa, of what people say against our Holy Religion. But there are times when it would be disloyal to keep silence. lioulitler, you now in the local Press of Saturday week that the Rector of Warrington went out of 'his way, as it eeems to roe, to make an attack on Rome and its teaching. I therefore consider it n ditty to expose his (to put it mildly) incorret t statement.. And first of all he asserts that Rome " has no right to call herself rotholie in Home things, though in other things she has." It all depends on what the Rector means by CorhoLc. What wc mean is that the Church, to be the Church of Cod, must " subsist in all eges, teach all nations, and maintain all truths." This we nay the Roman Church has dose without change since the time of Christ; whereas the Anglican Church dates only from the 10th century. Possibly the Rector may mean by Catholic all that in which be and Rome agree op in. In what they do not agree she is not Catholic. The Rector says " We all profess to be Catholics." Ido not think that very many of the Protestants of Warrington, or indeed anywhme, would say this. If in stranger were to conic into this town, and ask the cite in the street to take him to a Catholic Church, I ftel quite sure he would cot take him to St. Elphin..—unless, if course, it were the Rector himself. But we will pass on to the five points mentioned by the Rector and as given in the Press. And firstly it i• very evident that he hos a greet leaning to the ...Greek "or .• Orthodox Church, and has very littlesympathy with that of Rome. So much so that where Rome d(ffets from the Greek Church she is surely wrong. Hence he •ays ( I) Rome is wrong "by putting forth the claim,tv hich we, strongly repudiated by the Eastern Csurch, that the Pope wax the Vicar of Christ, and the Head of the Church on earth, w heroes he wee the great Patriarch of the %Vest, and no wore." In thin Canon Willis is entirely wrong. Up to the time of the greet Schiele between East and West towards the end of the 9th century, none of the Fathers of the Church were stronger in imholding the Ptie soy of the Pope of Rome then the Eastern Fathers. fiven Photius who began the Schism upheld this doctrine then, though he afterwards repodiat ed it because he could not get his way es to the "Filiorpte"—in the same way a 9 Henry VIII. threw over the Pope because he would not allow him to put navey kin lawitil wife, (2). The Rector finds fault eith Rome fir" sdding to the Nicene Creed certain words with regard to the Holy Ghost without the consent of the whole Church." Before enswering this I nowt explain and make clear ea to what the Rector refers. St.athennaitie hied in the 4th Century. The .kthanasian Creel, therefore, dates hack long before the great Schism. In that Creed, as everyone knows, it is stated that the Holy Ghost proceeds front the Father and the Son. Now the great Schism was calmed mainly hy the Greek Church declaring that Ile proceeds from the Father only. To thin the Pope as Head of the Church and its mouthpece would never consent. The controversy merit oti fur more then it hundred years. At last the Eastern Church, an this point principally, broke tawny from Rome finally. Then it was 'het Rome inserted the words " Filing. " in the Nicene Creed. The word. mt Enelith are " I behove in the Holy Ghost who proceeds from the Father and the Son, .00." Up to thin time the Church as e whole believed this, hut until it was totsitively denied by the Schismatics she did not thiuk it necessary to pronounce upon it dogmatically. This loas were leen the practice of the Church. Many doctrines are implicitly believed, but are not sh.lutely defined until someone with a big following openly d.iee that doctrine. Then she authoritatively pronounces sire this cone. Therefore. when Photitte and his following openly denied the Piocession of the Holy Chart from Father and Son I she inserted the doctrine in the Nicene Creed. One would think that the Rector could sear.ly object to this if he believes in the Athanasian Creed, to which I teenliwo ho sobscribed before becoming a clergymen. On this point, as also on the following, I would refer him to the " History of the Orthodox Greek Church" by Fortescue. (3). Here the Rector says : —"For nearly 1,010 yeses the whole Church believed that the Pres.cn of or Lord in the Holy Commtmion depended upon the invocation of the Holy Spirit, 5.. c.," This is quite inaccurate, indeed quite wrong. Not until the Ith century even in the East did this in.cation appear at all, wherens the words of institution " this is My Body, •kc.," were always and everywhere lined as necessary from our Lord's thee. This invocation pro- bably estendel, tnough not certainly, to parts of the West. But the Roman Ciautch held to the words of Institution as necessary ; and after the Schism definitely mid in what ...ration comaisted. On this point I most again refer the Rector to the above mentioned hinter, (4). On the fourth point the Rector says The whole Church believed in the Real l'resence, but about 1.000 years niter Chritst, the Church of Rome took it upate herself to teach the doctrine of transehetant ia- Coo. The Easteru Church hid always refused tuna. the equivalent to that word. elthotigh they believed them wax a change." The Rector in quite right in saying that "Thum whole Chotch belimed in ttitt Real Preen. "—but what does that name ? It mewls that accortitng to the testimony of all writers on the subject from the entlieet nee. St. Ignatius of Antioch, St. Justin the Martyr. St. Cyril of Jertiaalem, St. Chryrostom of Conatantinople, all these of the Eaetern Church, as well as S.S. Ambrose mad Augustine and • host of Weetern Fathom—all nay thet after the word. of consecration the " subatence of the Ilread and Wino are changed into the eubstance • of the tidy and Blootl or Christ." It may be true that the word toenaubstantiation was not used at that time— but they all lipeak of a change of imbalance, end what is that 'tut transuhetantiation? It in whet the word means, di, it matter of fact no one—et any rate norms of any account—either in the Latin or Greek Church thought of denying it until the 11th century, when one Berengarius presumed to do so,and at the Council of Rome, 1504, he was condemned, and the word tramathatantiation used officially in order to emplimim s doctrine not called in question till that time. The Rector further raps " The Eastern Chu ch alwayn refuted to use the eettivalent to that word." This I utterly deny. I grant they did not, and do nor, use that very word itself, for it is a Latin word, and all their • theist teaching and rite, are in Greek. But they use ita equivalent in the Greek word Metousimia which signifies a change of substance. Hence we find that the synod held hr the Greek Church at Jerumlem in 1672 declared that "after the consecra- tion of the bread and wine, the subteence of bread nod wine no longer remain, but the very Body and Blood of Christ and are to be honoured above all things, soil adored with real education." Other synod. since nice o.lin 1723 in reply to an Anglican deputation, rbeilareo Inc very same doctrine. In a word there has,, difference athatever,nor bus there ever been, between the Crock and letin Church on Oita pohita., Both helieveand boa-never believed it. the Real Presence and has real ...of substance after the consecration What they differ in is ax to the mode of administering I the Sacrament. And this Mines an to the fifth point, (5). The Rector says "In the year 1415 the Church of Rome took it upon herself to deny the Cup to the Lsity.The Eastern Church communicated her people in both kines." This ASV, tic.) as to fact is correct, though hem not quite sore xs to dete.liot this latter does not signify. I answer that this i• merely a case of di.ife liue wad custom, not of doctrine. Doctrine cannot change. Custom and discipline may very in different countries and at different times, provided there is no difference of doctrine, and that there is no difference of dec.rine between the Latin and Greek Courches has lees alrendy shown. Why the Latin Chtirch han an ordained I need not enter info now. Sactice ills nay that as Our Lord is contained whote and entire under either form alone, the recipient receive. Our Lord under either torm. These sea the five points the Rector specially calls /attention to. He further sposa• of the Sacraments ehich he awild like to he seven Instead of two, ashes been the case in the Anglican Church for the haat :WO yearn. For expreming such a desire be has been called to account by a Sunday school teacher. f most any the Sunday achool teacher is core stet, tr, and the Rector scarcely so. For I cannot see how he yen consistently remain an Anglican clergyman and yet tiphol•l that there lire amen Sacraments. But thee, lam an outsider, and not capable of judging per- p ha correctly. In conclusion, I will q.t., just a few were of the Rector's words. He says • " I think es a rule, ...la- irs bioadly, we may acknowledge that when the East and Went tree they are right." But the East end West Imils agree in denyine the salididity of Anglican or•lere. This beteg true (tic, how stands the Rector as to orders ? Father Whittle concluded his sillrees by ue exhorta- tion to tin p•ttple to live up practically to the Faith that wsa to them. There we,, large congregetion.</p>
        <p>St. George's Mission Congregational Tea. A very aucceelful gathering took place at St. lreorgebs ltlirien on Mond evening but in the above Mission room. The rabies were presided over by the different ladles connected with the church,Nand an excellent repast w. preened. The company included th• Rev. Canon Willix and Rex. T. A../. Ridpith. After ten dancing and game, were indolged in, and ,nga were well received from the Rev. I. A. J. Ridpirth, Mr. W. Donne., and also glees from the choir. Mr. W. Braodreth accompanwd•</p>
