      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>ST. DAVID'S DAY.</head>
        <p>ST. DAVID'S DAY.</p>
        <p>Celebration at Welsh Literary society.</p>
        <p>I The Welmh Literary Society celebrated the festival , of the Patron Saint ot Wales, St. David, lest Thursday ' eveniog at the Cm/Meld-al net Welrly Chapel. A large number of he Welsh residents of the town along with their trieneta set down to the tea tecles, which bud l-eon handsomely arraneed, end the daffodil, which is now the geurelly adopted emblem of Weles, wee worn by Kilned the tables %ere decorated with the iloweekleW.. 'l'. Will in ms,J. l'., president of the seciety,preaided over the eveniog's arrangements. The first tout, tent of the Sing aud Rout femily, was proposed by the President. In his speech, he referred to I the investitnre of the Prince of %Vale. at Carnervon I Castle next Jilly, alre to the latest announcene tit that hie klejerty will emit the Principality in July nest and will himself invert the Prince of Welles with the signs and symbols of the new dignity to the Prince. " God sue the King " wee then rung. The next tout was the " Immortal memory of Deal dent." This was propose° by kle D. James. Ile raid that St. David died on klarch tel io th, year :41, when lie woe e 2 years of age. Notwithstanding the myths end fable. that obscured the history they knew that their iestren Faint had lived end worked. Pope CNIWELUS elincalilled him, hot it woe the Welsh people who nude bite tt national mint. They were hazy shoot his chronology, hut they lived tinder the power of his influence. They might not know his history, as it wee only first written about live hundred years ago bye writer named Ilhegyfarch, who wrote it is ithout any substantial materiel as a bask, hut the principles that abided in his heart were those that were adopted by Wale., and slao that would raise the" old country " up in the sight of all nntions. Fur there reasons he asked them to drink a hearty and eethueittetic toast to 'the suiding memory 'ifs loving and it:ooring personality. Mr D. J. PHI, E, of I.ovely•lene, next proposed the tout of" Gar language, our country, and our nation." lean eloquent speech he relerred to the richness of the language, and the wealthy poems and compositions. What a lora it would be to a Welshman if he lost his Mother tongue ! It woold be a loss troth in national, ....etat and religious spheres and intlitencu. Then he showed that Welsh was the language of the heart of the Welshman and expressed his deepest sentiment. •In cunclesion he mentioned the awnkening spirit to receive the language and to the prominence of the cone of the Lend in the highest spheres of the realm—and one especially,none less tun the Cnancellar of the Exchequor—w here the spirit of the Welsh environment and the curly nurture had been the laming . powers to make him what Lloyd George is to-day. After the drinking of the tout Mr. Willie Hughcs sang "The Land of the Harp." Other touts were proposed including the" Success if theSociety"by MrJohn(lriffithe and ,a most enjoy.ittle evening tie spent in] ringing some of lb. most popular Welsh airs. The first part of the celebration war mainly carried on in the Weigh vernacular. The evenine concluded with ringing the Welsh National Anthem" e land of my fathers,"</p>
