      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>HONOURING THE DEAD.</head>
        <p>HONOURING THE DEAD.</p>
        <p>BATTLE OF PIETER'S HILL COMMEMORATED</p>
        <p>Mentor:ea of :he gallant par which the South Lancashire Regiment—the gradl old fighting fortieth "—played in the South African campaign were vividly recalled on Monday which was the eleventh anniversary of the brittle of Pieter', Bill. It was that decisive victory which opened the way for the rebel of Ladysmith. bravely the Lancashire men, amid an incessant hail of bullet., dashed up the side of the bill, with fixed bayonets, and carried the position at sunset. But the achievement cost them dearly, the enemy's fire working great havoc in their rankaand one of the first to fall was their commander —the beloved and intrepid Colonel McCarthy O'Leary. fire, therefore, fitting that as each anniversary of the battle comes, round, Warrington should keep green the memory of all the local soldiers who gave their lives 111 the South African war. And the occasion iii never overlooked. On Monday the soldier'n memorial in Queen's Carden, was once more decorated with wreaths and laurel, and many soldiers and civilians made pilgrimage to the spot, in reverent homage to Ike heroes who fell in bringing relief to beleagered comrades. A festoon of laurel leaves was hung round the granite plinth and at the base of the atatue were placed wreaths from the following : Officers of the Ist Battalion Smith Laneashire Regiment ; sergeants of the 3rd Battalion ; officers at the depot ; warrant officers and sergeants at the depot; officera of the 2nd Battalion ; members of the (sergeants' mess. 4th Battalion South Lancashire Regiment : and met, sth Battalion ; Captain F. M. Appleton and Volunteers (If of the South Lancashire Regiment : non-commisinoned offieers and privates, 3rd,llattalion ; Lieutenant-Colonel J. D. Fairclongh, V.D. and officare of the Ith Battalion ; officers of the 3rd Battalir n ; warrant officers and sergeants of the 2nd Battalion Ito Saturday afternoon the annual luncheon in commemoration of the victor), of Pieter's Hill, was held at the Winrnarleigh Cafe, about seventy non- commissioned officers, th..ir wives and friends, being present. Sergeant-Major S. T. Boast was a genial chairman awl be was supported by Captain and Mrs. Cotton and L'eutenant and Mrs. Baines. After lunch the usual tow.ce were honoured Captain Cotton made a couple of presentations b. members of the regiment. The first was to , • Sergeant J. Conley and the embricribers were tire Sergeant-Major and the sergeants and staff of the TIM d South Lancashire Regiment. " on his discharge to pension." This presentation consisted of a hoed- some clock and bronze ornaments to match. The other preseutation wart to Mr. Ives, former sergeant- dreamier, awl eonsimed of a ring with monogram. During the afternoon •n interesting musical pro- gramme wan gene Mr mali,the accompaniments being played by Mr. G. W. Lomas.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-03-04_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>COMMEMORATION DANCE.</head>
        <p>COMMEMORATION DANCE.</p>
        <p>The junior non-commissioned officers, drummers, and privates permanently stationed at the Smith I,ncashire Dep4 held a c,minemorstion dance at the \VintnerLeigh Cafe on Monday evening, More than a 'hundred guests were present and among them were Major Goren, Captain Cotton, Liemenant and Mrs. Bases. Captains Crosfield and Layton, Sergeant- Maj,r Boast and a number of other sergeants. Lance. Corporal Boast fulfilled Lie duties Of MA'. in a capable mynner and he was ably assisted by Corporals 111ite and Dixon who acted as stewards. The niusio was supplied by Mr. Thomas. Excellent refreshments were served by the management of the rate.</p>
      </div>
