        <p>The monthly n.eeting of the Warrington A 551,9- ment Committee teas heid at the Workhouse infirmary yeeterday morning. Mr J BiHinge presiding There were also present :—Mesers .1 W Knowles,' Forneee, W Giil, A J Jolley, W C Brcoks, A Brooks, A Bottomley (clerk). It R Meacock (valuer), and W 11 Woodcock (assistant overseer for Warsington).</p>
        <p>oorl-1,1• EN,I; APPEAL. Mr. F. W. Witt appealed on behalf of Messrs. Peter Walker and San, bullied, against the assess- tnent of an off-licence, The Oaks, 127 Bewsey-road. whirl] stood at ±:O4 grows, Y. 58 rateable, with an additional £3 10s. gross, t:3 rateable 1.. r a warehouse or died, near Pierpoint-street. lie said that since January 12,11. when a new tenant took posees.ion, the rent had been CM per annum. For the last three years the trade had averaged :I', birrels per week, but at preaent it was only 2; barrels. The decline in trade app., ed to be due m the competition of a neighboring licensed house. He considered the assessment should be gross and 1111 rateable.</p>
        <p>The Nitluer Is the 130 a rentsl fir the present year or for a short time ouly, or is it supposed logo</p>
        <p>Mr. Witt : It in 'opposed to go on. certainly. Mr. Woodcock maid lie eras of the opinion that the annesninent was too high It seas decided to reduce the joint annenninent of the off-licensed house and shed to .E.;,) grint. 1:12 rateable.</p>
        <p>THE VALI' \II, ,P 1</p>
        <p>. . The Valuer placed be? .re he ounnittee hi. re-valuation of the whole of the hotels, public-houses and beer-b..uses in the Uuion.</p>
        <p>The Chairman, Can you briefly elate, for the committee's information, what is the Labia of these valuati•lis?</p>
        <p>The Valuer : A personal visit and inspection of the premises and the best information I was able to I get with regard lathe rent and that sort of thing. I bane applied the usual and proper methods in every instance to find out what the law says we must find— the rent the tenant should give for the property. The Chairman : What a hypothetical tenant would give? The Valuer Exectly. Mr. Knowles expressed the opinion that the valua- tion, as submitted to the committee. oxght to embody something more than a mere decision of fact. The committee should be furnished with full details as to the rent, amount of trade in beer and spirits, A.c. I The particulars would then be recorded in the hooks of the Union and lie available for future use.</p>
        <p>The Valuer mui he had not 'sported in any way from the practice he hail fo ' ted during the whole of the time he had been therein. of the rnion. He was appointed under a certain net on of a certain Act to do certain work. He had donc tail and he hod done it well. They did not ask the ',edict.' officer at the Workhouse how he made ic t his prescriptions and tchy. I Mr. Furneee contended that there wan no analogy in the two cases and, although he ditl not take anv exception to the lines on which the valuer had acted, lie thought the detailemight be espplied to the com- mittee and than obviati the necescity of extracting all the infcrmation from the appellant. when they appeared before t Ite committee. The Valuer : The particulars supplied to me by individual ratepayers for the purpose, of the valua- tion, low not at deityl to make use of in any other way but fir the valuation. Mr. Knowles And to this committee.</p>
        <p>The Vahter And to this committee when those , valuations have been attacked. If there ate appeals n the list, then is the time for me to oat- why I did this. Continuing the valuer pointed out that a great . deal of the information given to him was oft very private nature lie did not think it would be in the best interests of the CIIIOO to give a'l patticulars to the committee before appeals were lodged_ The Clerk said the question so to whether the com- mittee were entitled to the information depended upon the agreement they h•d with the valuer on his appointment, and he the clerk) could not speak definitely Oil that point as he was not the clerk when Mr. Mescock W. appointed. No doubt Mr. Meacork obtained some information on the understanding that It was to he treated as sacred. Mr Jolley : Is the new valuation above or below the previous ore? The Valuer It will be above the previous one. . .</p>
        <p>Alter rlitic.eicn it Ras deeeled thet the Clerk should prepare a let showing the preeent and previous valuations in each CASS, and also the reduc- liana allowed under the Finance Act.</p>
        <p>_1:1. II %_II,_EI AN,' AND</p>
        <p>, Messrs. Richard Evans and Co., Ltd. had lodged !appeals against the following assessments : ll'arr Burtonwood, 5.2.07 G gross. .1:1353 ' rateable ; mines, Oast. land. buildings. S:c ilsydock, gee,. 111163; rsteable : mines and appur- ten.ueee.Nsenon. 13,541, grows, 1:1530 rateable. On the suggestion of Mr. Robinson. who aopeared for the firm, the committee adjourned the appeal for a further conference between the company and the valuer.</p>
